// People, part 1: Filipino heroes of the 1700s and 1800s.
window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"silang",t:"Gabriela Silang",i:"🐎",w:"Gabriela Silang",a:["Gabriela Silang","Diego Silang"],born:"19 March 1731, Santa, Ilocos Sur",died:"20 September 1763, Vigan",nat:"Filipino (Ilocano)",known:"Leading a revolt against Spanish rule in the Ilocos",r:["spanph","vigan","phrev"],
l:"Gabriela Silang was an Ilocano leader who took command of a revolt against Spanish rule after her husband, Diego Silang, was assassinated in 1763. Riding into battle on horseback, she became one of the first women to lead an uprising in the Philippines and remains a symbol of courage and resistance.",
d:[["1731","Born in Santa, Ilocos Sur"],["1762","British forces capture Manila; Diego Silang leads a revolt in the Ilocos"],["28 May 1763","Diego Silang is assassinated"],["September 1763","Gabriela’s attack on Vigan fails"],["20 September 1763","Executed by Spanish authorities in Vigan"]],
s:[["Early life","Gabriela Cariño was born in the Ilocos region of northern Luzon in 1731. As a young woman she married a wealthy older man, who died a few years later and left her his property. She later married Diego Silang, a messenger and trader who became a popular local leader."],
["Diego Silang’s revolt","In 1762 the British captured Manila during the Seven Years’ War, weakening Spanish control. Diego Silang led Ilocanos in rising against Spanish officials and heavy tribute payments. He took control of Vigan and declared that the Ilocos should be free of Spanish abuses, seeking British support."],
["Taking command","On 28 May 1763, Diego Silang was shot and killed by an assassin paid by Spanish authorities and church officials. Gabriela escaped to the mountains of Abra and took over leadership of the revolt, gathering fighters from Ilocano and Tingguian communities."],
["Defeat and death","In September 1763 Gabriela led an attack on Vigan, but Spanish forces defeated her army. She was captured and, on 20 September 1763, was hanged in Vigan along with many of her followers. The revolt had lasted only a few months after her husband’s death."],
["The Ilocos under Spain","In the 1700s, Filipinos under Spanish rule paid tribute, a tax, and were forced to provide labor. Spain also controlled trade in tobacco and other goods. These burdens, along with abuses by some officials, led to dozens of revolts across the islands during the colonial era."],
["The British in Manila","The British occupation of Manila from 1762 to 1764 showed that Spanish power could be challenged. Several revolts broke out during these years, including those of the Silangs in the Ilocos. When the war ended, Manila was returned to Spain under the Treaty of Paris."],
["A woman at war","Gabriela Silang is often remembered as the Joan of Arc of the Ilocos. In a time when women were rarely leaders, she rode at the head of her troops. Her story shows that women played important roles in Filipino resistance long before the revolution of 1896."],
["Remembering Gabriela","A statue of Gabriela Silang on horseback stands in Makati, along Ayala Avenue. Her life has been told in films, plays and songs. A women’s organization, GABRIELA, founded in 1984, is named in her honor, and schools and streets across the country bear her name."],
["Vigan today","Vigan, where Gabriela was executed, is now a UNESCO World Heritage City famous for its Spanish-era houses and cobblestone streets. The Silang revolt is part of the history visitors learn about at Vigan’s museums, including those in the Ilocos Sur provincial capitol area."],
["Her legacy","Gabriela Silang inspired later generations of Filipinos who fought for freedom. Her revolt was one of many that showed deep resentment of colonial rule more than a century before the Philippine Revolution. Today she is honored as a national heroine and a symbol of women’s strength."]],
sp:["The statue in Makati","The bronze equestrian statue of Gabriela Silang in Makati shows her charging forward on a rearing horse, holding a bolo knife. Made by sculptor Jose M. Mendoza, it stands at the corner of Ayala Avenue and Makati Avenue, one of the busiest business districts in the country. It reminds thousands of people who pass it each day of a woman who led an army more than 250 years ago."],
g:[["Revolt","An armed uprising against rulers"],["Tribute","A tax paid to colonial rulers"],["Occupation","Control of an area by foreign forces"],["Heroine","A woman admired for courage"]],
f:["Gabriela Silang was 32 years old when she was executed.","She escaped to Abra after her husband’s death.","Her revolt took place during the British occupation of Manila."]},

{id:"sora",t:"Melchora Aquino (Tandang Sora)",i:"👵",w:"Melchora Aquino",a:["Tandang Sora","Melchora Aquino"],born:"6 January 1812, Caloocan (now part of Quezon City)",died:"2 March 1919, Caloocan",nat:"Filipino",known:"Sheltering and caring for Katipunan revolutionaries",r:["phrev","bonifacio"],
l:"Melchora Aquino, known as Tandang Sora, was a shopkeeper and farmer who sheltered, fed and nursed members of the Katipunan during the Philippine Revolution of 1896. She was 84 years old at the time. Arrested and exiled by the Spanish, she became known as the Mother of the Philippine Revolution.",
d:[["1812","Born in Banlat, Caloocan"],["1896","Shelters Katipuneros at the start of the revolution"],["1896","Arrested and exiled to the Marianas"],["1903","Returns to the Philippines"],["1919","Dies at the age of 107"]],
s:[["Early life","Melchora Aquino was born on 6 January 1812 in Banlat, then part of Caloocan, now in Quezon City. Her parents were farmers. She could not attend school but learned to read and write. She was known for her beautiful singing voice and often sang at religious events in her community."],
["Family and work","She married Fulgencio Ramos, a local leader, and they had six children. After her husband died, she raised the children alone, running their farm and a small store. She became a respected figure, called Tandang Sora, tanda meaning old or elder and Sora from Melchora."],
["Helping the Katipunan","When the Philippine Revolution began in August 1896, Tandang Sora’s home became a refuge for Katipunan fighters. She gave them food, shelter and money and nursed the wounded. Andrés Bonifacio and other leaders reportedly met at her home. She was 84 years old."],
["Arrest and exile","Spanish authorities discovered her help to the rebels and arrested her. She was questioned but refused to reveal information about the Katipunan. She was exiled to Guam in the Marianas Islands. After the United States took over, she returned to the Philippines in 1903 and lived until 1919."],
["The Cry of Pugad Lawin","In late August 1896, Bonifacio and Katipuneros gathered in the area around Balintawak and Pugad Lawin, near Tandang Sora’s home, and tore up their cedulas, tax certificates, to mark the start of the revolt. The exact date and place are still debated by historians."],
["Life in exile","On Guam, Tandang Sora lived with other exiled Filipinos. Life was difficult, far from her family. Despite her age, she survived the years of exile. When she returned, she refused offers of government pensions and lived quietly with her family."],
["A long life","Tandang Sora lived to be 107 years old, seeing the end of Spanish rule and the beginning of the American period. She died on 2 March 1919. Her remains were later moved to the Tandang Sora National Shrine in Quezon City."],
["Places named after her","Tandang Sora Avenue in Quezon City and a barangay named Tandang Sora honor her. Her portrait appeared on the old 5-centavo coin. In 2012, on the 200th anniversary of her birth, the government declared a year of celebrations in her honor."],
["Women in the revolution","Tandang Sora was one of many women who supported the revolution. Others included Gregoria de Jesús, Bonifacio’s wife, who kept Katipunan documents safe, and women who cooked, sewed flags, carried messages and nursed the wounded. Their contributions were essential to the struggle."],
["Her legacy","Tandang Sora showed that courage does not depend on age or strength. By opening her home to the revolutionaries, she risked everything. She is remembered as a symbol of compassion, generosity and patriotism, and her story is taught to Filipino children in schools."]],
sp:["Mother of the Revolution","Tandang Sora earned the title Mother of the Philippine Revolution, sometimes also called the Grand Woman of the Revolution. Her care for the young fighters, many of whom were the age of her grandchildren, made her a beloved figure. Stories tell that she cooked rice and chicken for hungry Katipuneros and treated their wounds with herbal medicines. For many Filipinos, she represents the spirit of bayanihan, people helping one another in times of need."],
g:[["Katipunan","The secret society that began the revolution"],["Exile","Being forced to live away from one’s home country"],["Cedula","A Spanish-era tax certificate"],["Shrine","A place built to honor someone"]],
f:["Tandang Sora was 84 when the revolution began.","She lived to the age of 107.","Tandang Sora Avenue in Quezon City is named after her."]},

{id:"delpilar",t:"Marcelo H. del Pilar",i:"✒️",w:"Marcelo H. del Pilar",a:["Marcelo H. del Pilar","del Pilar","Plaridel"],born:"30 August 1850, Bulakan, Bulacan",died:"4 July 1896, Barcelona, Spain",nat:"Filipino",known:"Journalism and leadership of the Propaganda Movement",r:["rizal","phrev","spanph"],
l:"Marcelo H. del Pilar was a lawyer, writer and journalist who led the Propaganda Movement, a campaign by Filipino reformists for changes in Spanish colonial rule. Writing under the pen name Plaridel, he edited the newspaper La Solidaridad in Spain and became one of the most influential voices for Filipino rights.",
d:[["1850","Born in Bulakan, Bulacan"],["1880","Earns his law degree"],["1882","Helps found Diariong Tagalog"],["1888","Leaves for Spain to avoid arrest"],["1889","Becomes editor of La Solidaridad"],["1896","Dies of tuberculosis in Barcelona"]],
s:[["Early life","Marcelo Hilario del Pilar was born on 30 August 1850 in Bulakan, Bulacan, into an educated family. He studied at the Colegio de San José and the University of Santo Tomas, where he earned a law degree in 1880. He was skilled in Tagalog and Spanish."],
["Diariong Tagalog","In 1882 del Pilar helped found Diariong Tagalog, one of the first newspapers in the Philippines to publish in both Tagalog and Spanish. It criticized abuses by colonial officials and friars and called for reforms, reaching ordinary Filipinos in their own language."],
["Against friar power","Del Pilar campaigned against the great power of the Spanish friars, who controlled land, education and local government in many towns. He wrote pamphlets and satires, including a parody of the Lord’s Prayer and religious texts mocking the friars’ greed. His activities made him a target of the authorities."],
["Exile in Spain","In 1888, facing arrest, del Pilar left his family and sailed to Spain. There he joined other Filipino reformists, known as ilustrados. In 1889 he took over La Solidaridad, the newspaper of the Propaganda Movement, and edited it until 1895."],
["La Solidaridad","La Solidaridad, published in Barcelona and later Madrid, called for Filipino representation in the Spanish parliament, equal rights for Filipinos and Spaniards, freedom of speech, and replacing friars with Filipino priests. José Rizal, Graciano López Jaena and others wrote for it."],
["Rivalry with Rizal","Del Pilar and Rizal were allies but sometimes disagreed. In 1891 the Filipino community in Madrid argued over who should lead them, and Rizal withdrew from La Solidaridad. Both men, however, remained committed to reform and to the Filipino cause."],
["Poverty and illness","Running La Solidaridad was costly, and funds from the Philippines dwindled. Del Pilar lived in poverty, sometimes collecting cigarette butts to smoke. He developed tuberculosis and died in Barcelona on 4 July 1896, weeks before the revolution broke out. His remains were returned to the Philippines in 1920."],
["Plaridel","Del Pilar wrote under several pen names, the most famous being Plaridel, an anagram of del Pilar. The town of Quingua in Bulacan was renamed Plaridel in his honor. Journalists in the Philippines regard him as a father of Philippine journalism."],
["The Katipunan connection","Although del Pilar believed in peaceful reform, his writings inspired the Katipunan. Andrés Bonifacio and other members read La Solidaridad. Some historians say del Pilar came to believe that reforms might be impossible without independence."],
["His legacy","Del Pilar is remembered as a brilliant writer and organizer who sacrificed his comfort and health for his country. His birthday, 30 August, is a holiday in Bulacan. His image appeared on the old 50-centavo coin, and a shrine in his hometown honors him."]],
sp:["The Propaganda Movement","The Propaganda Movement, active from about 1880 to 1895, was a campaign by educated Filipinos, many living in Spain, to win reforms through writing and persuasion. Its members wanted the Philippines to become a province of Spain with equal rights rather than a colony. Spain largely ignored their demands. Its failure convinced many Filipinos that independence could only be won by revolution, leading to the founding of the Katipunan in 1892."],
g:[["Propaganda Movement","A campaign for reforms in Spanish rule"],["Ilustrado","An educated Filipino of the 1800s"],["Pen name","A false name used by a writer"],["Friar","A member of a Catholic religious order"]],
f:["Plaridel is an anagram of del Pilar.","He edited La Solidaridad for about six years.","His remains were brought home to the Philippines in 1920."]},

{id:"mabini",t:"Apolinario Mabini",i:"🧠",w:"Apolinario Mabini",a:["Apolinario Mabini","Mabini"],born:"23 July 1864, Talaga, Tanauan, Batangas",died:"13 May 1903, Manila",nat:"Filipino",known:"Being the Brains of the Revolution and first prime minister",r:["malolos","phrev","rizal"],
l:"Apolinario Mabini was a lawyer and political thinker who served as chief adviser to Emilio Aguinaldo and as the first prime minister of the Philippine Republic. Paralyzed from the waist down, he guided the revolution with his writing and ideas and became known as the Brains of the Revolution and the Sublime Paralytic.",
d:[["1864","Born in Talaga, Tanauan, Batangas"],["1894","Graduates in law from the University of Santo Tomas"],["1896","Loses the use of his legs"],["1898","Becomes Aguinaldo’s chief adviser"],["1899","Serves as first prime minister"],["1901–1903","Exiled to Guam"],["1903","Dies of cholera in Manila"]],
s:[["Humble beginnings","Apolinario Mabini was born on 23 July 1864 in Tanauan, Batangas, the second of eight children of poor farmers. He was a brilliant student and worked as a servant and teacher to pay for his education. He studied law at the University of Santo Tomas and graduated in 1894."],
["Illness","In early 1896, Mabini fell ill, and he lost the use of his legs, probably due to polio. For the rest of his life he could not walk and had to be carried in a hammock or chair. Despite this, he continued to work tirelessly for the Filipino cause."],
["Adviser to Aguinaldo","In 1898 Emilio Aguinaldo invited Mabini to be his chief adviser. Mabini helped organize the revolutionary government, wrote decrees and urged Aguinaldo to set up a strong government. He believed the Philippines must be fully independent rather than under any foreign power."],
["Prime minister","In January 1899 Mabini became president of the Council of Government, effectively the first prime minister, and secretary of foreign affairs of the First Philippine Republic. He opposed negotiating with the Americans unless they recognized Philippine independence, and he resigned in May 1899."],
["The True Decalogue","Mabini wrote El Verdadero Decálogo, The True Decalogue, a set of ten moral rules for Filipino citizens. It urges people to love God and honor, love their country, work for its welfare and independence, and treat others fairly. It was meant to guide a new nation."],
["Constitutional ideas","Mabini wrote a proposed constitution for the Philippines that called for a strong executive and a powerful role for the government during wartime. His plan was not adopted; the Malolos Congress approved a different constitution in 1899. He also wrote extensively on government and rights."],
["Capture and exile","During the Philippine–American War, Mabini was captured by American forces in December 1899. Because he refused to swear allegiance to the United States, he was exiled to Guam in 1901 with other Filipino leaders. In exile he wrote La Revolución Filipina, a history of the revolution."],
["Return and death","In 1903, ill and weary, Mabini took the oath of allegiance to the United States so he could return home. He arrived in Manila in February 1903 and died of cholera on 13 May 1903, aged 38. He spent his last months writing."],
["The Sublime Paralytic","Mabini’s nickname, the Sublime Paralytic, honors his greatness of mind despite his disability. He shows that physical limits do not prevent people from contributing greatly. He is admired for his honesty, simple living and refusal to compromise his principles."],
["His legacy","Mabini’s face appeared on the Philippine 10-peso bill and coin. His birthday is a holiday in Batangas, and his house in Tanauan is a shrine. The Mabini Shrine in Manila, a house where he lived, is now part of the Polytechnic University of the Philippines campus."]],
sp:["The True Decalogue","The first rule of Mabini’s True Decalogue tells Filipinos to love God and their honor above all things. Other rules say: love your country after God and your honor; strive for the happiness of your country; work to make it independent; and respect your neighbor. The final rule asks people to consider their countryman as more than a neighbor, as a friend, a brother or a comrade. The Decalogue is still studied in Philippine schools."],
g:[["Prime minister","The head of a government’s cabinet"],["Constitution","The basic laws of a country"],["Decalogue","A set of ten rules"],["Allegiance","Loyalty to a country or ruler"]],
f:["Mabini was carried in a hammock because he could not walk.","He was only 38 when he died.","He wrote a history of the revolution while in exile on Guam."]},

{id:"aluna",t:"Antonio Luna",i:"⚔️",w:"Antonio Luna",a:["Antonio Luna","General Luna"],born:"29 October 1866, Binondo, Manila",died:"5 June 1899, Cabanatuan, Nueva Ecija",nat:"Filipino",known:"Commanding Filipino forces in the Philippine–American War",r:["jluna","malolos","phrev"],
l:"Antonio Luna was a scientist, writer and general who commanded the Filipino army during the early part of the Philippine–American War. A trained pharmacist with a doctorate, he founded a military academy and a newspaper. Brilliant but hot-tempered, he was assassinated by soldiers of his own side in 1899.",
d:[["1866","Born in Binondo, Manila"],["1890","Earns a doctorate in pharmacy in Madrid"],["1896","Arrested by the Spanish and later exiled to Spain"],["1898","Returns and joins the revolution; founds La Independencia"],["1899","Leads Filipino forces against the Americans"],["5 June 1899","Assassinated in Cabanatuan"]],
s:[["Family and studies","Antonio Luna was born on 29 October 1866 in Binondo, Manila, the youngest of seven children. His brother Juan became a famous painter. Antonio studied at the Ateneo Municipal and the University of Santo Tomas, then went to Spain, earning a doctorate in pharmacy in Madrid in 1890."],
["Scientist","Luna studied in Belgium and France and wrote scientific papers on malaria and on chemistry. He returned to Manila in 1894, worked as a chemist and won a government post as a chemical expert. He was also a skilled fencer and marksman."],
["Writer","In Spain, Luna joined the Propaganda Movement and wrote for La Solidaridad under the pen name Taga-Ilog. His essays described Spanish life with humor and argued for Filipino rights. He later founded the newspaper La Independencia in 1898."],
["Arrest","When the revolution began in 1896, Luna was arrested, though he had not joined the Katipunan. He was imprisoned and then sent to Spain. While there, he studied military science under respected teachers, preparing himself for the battles ahead."],
["General in the war","After returning in 1898, Luna joined Aguinaldo’s government and became a general. When war broke out with the United States in February 1899, he led Filipino troops around Manila. He tried to impose strict discipline, punishing soldiers who looted or disobeyed."],
["Military academy","Luna founded the Academia Militar in Malolos in 1898 to train Filipino officers. He planned defensive lines, including trenches in the mountains of northern Luzon, to slow the American advance. He wanted the Filipino army to fight as a modern, disciplined force."],
["Quarrels and enemies","Luna’s fierce temper and harsh discipline made enemies among other officers and politicians. He clashed with officials who favored negotiating with the Americans. He disarmed and punished a unit from Kawit, Aguinaldo’s home town, for disobeying orders."],
["Assassination","On 5 June 1899, Luna went to Cabanatuan, Nueva Ecija, responding to a telegram summoning him to meet Aguinaldo. There he was attacked and killed by soldiers, including members of the unit he had punished. He was 32. Historians still debate who ordered the killing."],
["Heneral Luna","The 2015 film Heneral Luna, starring John Arcilla, told Luna’s story to a new generation and became one of the most popular Filipino historical films. It showed both his brilliance and his flaws and sparked public discussion about leadership and unity."],
["His legacy","Antonio Luna is remembered as one of the most capable Filipino generals and a patriot who placed his country above personal comfort. Many believe the war might have gone differently had he lived. Streets, towns and military camps are named after him."]],
sp:["The Luna brothers","Antonio and Juan Luna were among the most talented Filipinos of their generation. Juan was a celebrated painter whose Spoliarium won a gold medal in Madrid in 1884, while Antonio was a scientist and general. They shared a passion for their country but both had fiery tempers. Both died in 1899, Antonio in June and Juan in December. Their family home in Badoc, Ilocos Norte, where Juan was born, is now a museum."],
g:[["General","A senior army commander"],["Assassination","The murder of an important person"],["Pharmacist","An expert in preparing medicines"],["Academy","A school for special training"]],
f:["Antonio Luna held a doctorate in pharmacy.","He was a skilled fencer.","His story was told in the 2015 film Heneral Luna."]},

{id:"jluna",t:"Juan Luna",i:"🖼️",w:"Juan Luna",a:["Juan Luna","Spoliarium"],born:"23 October 1857, Badoc, Ilocos Norte",died:"7 December 1899, Hong Kong",nat:"Filipino",known:"Painting the Spoliarium",r:["aluna","rizal","impress"],
l:"Juan Luna was a Filipino painter whose huge painting Spoliarium won a gold medal at a national art exhibition in Madrid in 1884. His success showed that Filipinos could equal or surpass Europeans in the arts and inspired the reformists of the Propaganda Movement. His life also included a terrible crime that still shapes how he is remembered.",
d:[["1857","Born in Badoc, Ilocos Norte"],["1877","Travels to Spain to study art"],["1884","Spoliarium wins a gold medal in Madrid"],["1892","Kills his wife and mother-in-law in Paris; acquitted"],["1898","Serves as a diplomat for the revolutionary government"],["1899","Dies in Hong Kong"]],
s:[["Early life","Juan Luna y Novicio was born on 23 October 1857 in Badoc, Ilocos Norte. His family moved to Manila, where he studied at the Ateneo and earned a certificate as a sailor. He also studied painting at the Academia de Dibujo y Pintura in Manila."],
["Studies in Spain","In 1877 Luna went to Spain to study at the Real Academia de Bellas Artes de San Fernando in Madrid. He later worked in Rome under the painter Alejo Vera. He painted large historical and classical scenes in the academic style admired at the time."],
["The Spoliarium","Luna’s masterpiece, Spoliarium, about 4 by 7.7 meters, shows the bodies of dead gladiators being dragged from a Roman arena. At the 1884 National Exposition of Fine Arts in Madrid, it won one of three gold medals. Many Filipinos saw it as a symbol of colonial suffering."],
["Celebration in Madrid","Filipinos in Madrid held a banquet in 1884 to honor Luna and Félix Resurrección Hidalgo, who won a silver medal. José Rizal gave a famous speech praising them, saying their success proved that genius knows no country. The event encouraged the reform movement."],
["Paris","Luna moved to Paris, where his style became freer and more colorful, influenced by Impressionism and realism. He painted scenes of Parisian life, such as Parisian Life, and portraits. He married Paz Pardo de Tavera in 1886, and they had a son, Andrés, who also became an artist."],
["Tragedy","In 1892, in a fit of jealousy, Luna shot and killed his wife and his mother-in-law in Paris and wounded his brother-in-law. A French court acquitted him, judging it a crime of passion, as courts often did then. Today the killings are seen as a grave crime that cannot be excused."],
["Return and revolution","Luna returned to the Philippines and was arrested in 1896 on suspicion of supporting the revolution, then released. He later served the revolutionary government as a diplomat in Paris and Washington, seeking recognition of Philippine independence."],
["Death","In December 1899, Luna was in Hong Kong when he learned of the death of his brother Antonio. He died of a heart attack on 7 December 1899, aged 42. His remains were later returned to the Philippines."],
["The Spoliarium today","The Spoliarium now hangs in the National Museum of Fine Arts in Manila, where it is the first painting many visitors see. It is the largest painting in the Philippines. Luna’s works sell for record prices at auction."],
["A complex legacy","Juan Luna is honored as one of the greatest Filipino painters and a national hero of the arts. At the same time, many people today argue that his violent crime must be acknowledged alongside his art, as an example of the danger of glorifying people without examining their actions."]],
sp:["Genius knows no country","At the 1884 banquet in Madrid, José Rizal praised Luna and Hidalgo in a speech that became famous. He declared that their triumph showed that genius knows no country and blossoms everywhere, like light and air. Rizal used their success to argue that Filipinos deserved equal treatment. The speech marked a moment when Filipino reformists felt their people could stand proudly beside any nation in the world."],
g:[["Masterpiece","An artist’s greatest work"],["Gladiator","A fighter in ancient Roman arenas"],["Exposition","A large public exhibition"],["Diplomat","A person who represents a country abroad"]],
f:["The Spoliarium is about 7.7 meters wide.","Luna was also a trained sailor.","His son Andrés Luna de San Pedro became an architect."]}
);

