/* data/people2.js */
// People, part 2: Filipino leaders of the 1800s and 1900s.
window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"balagtas",t:"Francisco Balagtas",i:"📜",w:"Francisco Balagtas",a:["Francisco Balagtas"],born:"2 April 1788, Panginay, Bigaa, Bulacan",died:"20 February 1862, Orion, Bataan",nat:"Filipino",known:"Writing the epic poem Florante at Laura",r:["phlit","delpilar"],
l:"Francisco Balagtas was a Tagalog poet whose long poem Florante at Laura is one of the greatest works of Philippine literature. Called the Prince of Tagalog Poets, he showed that Tagalog could be a language of fine poetry, and his name lives on in the balagtasan, a Filipino poetic debate.",
d:[["1788","Born in Panginay, Bigaa, Bulacan"],["c. 1835","Imprisoned in Manila"],["1838","Florante at Laura is published"],["1842","Marries Juana Tiambeng in Orion, Bataan"],["1862","Dies in Orion, Bataan"]],
s:[["Early life","Francisco Balagtas, also known as Francisco Baltazar, was born on 2 April 1788 in Bigaa, Bulacan, to a blacksmith’s family. As a boy he moved to Tondo, Manila, where he worked as a houseboy for a wealthy relative in exchange for schooling."],
["Education","Balagtas studied at the Colegio de San José and San Juan de Letran, learning Latin, Spanish, philosophy and religion. He began writing poems for friends and neighbors and was coached by José de la Cruz, known as Huseng Sisiw, a famous Tondo poet."],
["Love and prison","In Pandacan, Balagtas fell in love with María Asunción Rivera, known as Celia. A rich rival, Mariano Capule, is said to have used his wealth to have Balagtas imprisoned. While in prison, Balagtas wrote Florante at Laura, dedicating it to Celia."],
["Florante at Laura","Florante at Laura, published in 1838, is an awit, a metrical romance in 12-syllable lines grouped in four-line stanzas. Set in the kingdom of Albania, it tells of the hero Florante, betrayed by the evil Adolfo, and his love for Laura. It also contains hidden criticism of Spanish rule."],
["Hidden meanings","Because writing openly against Spain was dangerous, Balagtas set his story in a far-off land. Scholars and readers, including José Rizal, saw in its descriptions of a land suffering under tyranny and betrayal a picture of the Philippines under colonial rule."],
["Later life","Balagtas moved to Orion, Bataan, where he married Juana Tiambeng in 1842 and had several children. He worked as a court clerk and translator. He was jailed again after a dispute, then released. He continued writing plays and poems, many now lost."],
["Death","Balagtas died on 20 February 1862 in Orion, Bataan, aged 73. Legend says he asked that none of his children become poets, to spare them the hardships he had endured. His tomb in Orion is a historical site."],
["The balagtasan","The balagtasan, a poetic debate in verse named after him, began in 1924. Two poets argue opposite sides of a topic before an audience, with a moderator. The first famous balagtasan featured José Corazón de Jesús and Florentino Collantes."],
["Influence on Filipino writing","Florante at Laura became a model for Tagalog poetry. Lines from it are still quoted, and it is studied in high schools across the country. Writers including José Rizal admired it. It helped establish Tagalog as a literary language."],
["Remembering Balagtas","His hometown, Bigaa, was renamed Balagtas in his honor. His birthday, 2 April, is celebrated as Balagtas Day. The Gawad Balagtas award recognizes outstanding writers, and his image appeared on the old 10-centavo coin."]],
sp:["A famous stanza","One of the most quoted parts of Florante at Laura is the hero’s lament about the land of Albania, ruled by cruelty and lies, where virtue is buried and evil rewarded. Many readers in the 1800s understood it as a description of their own country. José Rizal is said to have carried a copy of the poem with him in Europe."],
g:[["Awit","A metrical romance in 12-syllable lines"],["Stanza","A group of lines in a poem"],["Balagtasan","A poetic debate named after Balagtas"],["Allegory","A story with a hidden meaning"]],
f:["The town of Bigaa, Bulacan, is now called Balagtas.","Florante at Laura has 399 stanzas.","Balagtas was taught by the poet Huseng Sisiw."]},

{id:"aguinaldo",t:"Emilio Aguinaldo",i:"🎖️",w:"Emilio Aguinaldo",a:["Emilio Aguinaldo","Aguinaldo"],born:"22 March 1869, Kawit, Cavite",died:"6 February 1964, Quezon City",nat:"Filipino",known:"Declaring Philippine independence and leading the First Philippine Republic",r:["malolos","independence","phrev","mabini"],
l:"Emilio Aguinaldo was a revolutionary leader who declared Philippine independence from Spain on 12 June 1898 and became president of the First Philippine Republic. He led Filipino forces against Spain and then the United States. His role in the death of Andrés Bonifacio remains controversial.",
d:[["1869","Born in Kawit, Cavite"],["1897","Elected president at the Tejeros Convention"],["1897","Pact of Biak-na-Bato; exile in Hong Kong"],["12 June 1898","Declares independence in Kawit"],["1899","Inaugurated president of the First Philippine Republic"],["1901","Captured by American forces in Palanan"],["1964","Dies aged 94"]],
s:[["Early life","Emilio Aguinaldo was born on 22 March 1869 in Kawit, Cavite, into a well-off family. He left school early to help with the family business. At 26 he became gobernadorcillo, town head, of Kawit and joined the Katipunan in 1895."],
["Rise in the revolution","When the revolution began in 1896, Aguinaldo won victories against Spanish forces in Cavite, becoming a hero. The Katipunan in Cavite split into rival factions, the Magdalo, led by Aguinaldo’s cousin, and the Magdiwang, which supported Bonifacio."],
["Tejeros and Bonifacio","At the Tejeros Convention in March 1897, Aguinaldo was elected president of a new revolutionary government, defeating Bonifacio. Bonifacio rejected the result. He was arrested, tried by a military court and executed in May 1897. Historians debate how much Aguinaldo was responsible."],
["Biak-na-Bato","In December 1897 Aguinaldo agreed to the Pact of Biak-na-Bato with Spain, accepting money and exile in Hong Kong in exchange for ending the fighting. Neither side fully kept the agreement, and Aguinaldo used the funds to buy weapons."],
["Independence","In 1898, during the Spanish–American War, Aguinaldo returned with American help. On 12 June 1898, from the window of his house in Kawit, he proclaimed Philippine independence, and the Philippine flag was raised and the national anthem played for the first time."],
["The First Republic","The Malolos Congress wrote a constitution, and on 23 January 1899, Aguinaldo was inaugurated as president of the First Philippine Republic, the first constitutional republic in Asia. However, Spain had sold the Philippines to the United States in the Treaty of Paris."],
["War with the United States","Fighting broke out between Filipino and American forces in February 1899. Aguinaldo’s army was pushed north and turned to guerrilla warfare. On 23 March 1901, American troops captured him in Palanan, Isabela, using a trick, and he swore allegiance to the United States."],
["Later life","Aguinaldo retired to farming but remained a public figure. In 1935 he ran for president of the Commonwealth and lost to Manuel L. Quezon. During the Japanese occupation he cooperated with the Japanese, for which he was briefly jailed after the war and later pardoned."],
["A long life","Aguinaldo lived to see independence recognized by the United States in 1946 and the changing of Independence Day to 12 June in 1962. He died on 6 February 1964, aged 94. His house in Kawit is now the Aguinaldo Shrine."],
["His legacy","Aguinaldo is honored as the first president of the Philippines and as the leader who proclaimed independence. His role in the deaths of Bonifacio and Antonio Luna and his later cooperation with the Japanese make him a debated figure. Most Filipinos, however, see his 1898 declaration as a founding moment."]],
sp:["The Kawit balcony","On 12 June 1898, a crowd gathered at Aguinaldo’s house in Kawit to hear the Act of the Declaration of Philippine Independence, read by Ambrosio Rianzares Bautista. The flag, sewn in Hong Kong by Marcela Agoncillo, her daughter and Delfina Herbosa de Natividad, was unfurled, and the Marcha Nacional Filipina by Julián Felipe was played. Interestingly, the famous balcony of the house was added later; the declaration was made from a window."],
g:[["Declaration","A formal public announcement"],["Republic","A state without a monarch"],["Guerrilla warfare","Fighting in small, mobile groups"],["Inauguration","A ceremony that begins a leader’s term"]],
f:["Aguinaldo was 29 when he declared independence.","He lived to 94, longer than most leaders of his time.","Independence Day was moved to 12 June in 1962."]},

{id:"quezon",t:"Manuel L. Quezon",i:"🏛️",w:"Manuel L. Quezon",a:["Manuel L. Quezon","Manuel Quezon"],born:"19 August 1878, Baler, Tayabas (now Aurora)",died:"1 August 1944, Saranac Lake, New York, USA",nat:"Filipino",known:"Serving as president of the Philippine Commonwealth",r:["commonwealth","japocc","phlang"],
l:"Manuel L. Quezon was the president of the Commonwealth of the Philippines from 1935 to 1944, preparing the country for full independence. A powerful speaker and skilled politician, he championed a national language and social justice and led the government in exile during the Second World War.",
d:[["1878","Born in Baler"],["1899","Fights in the Philippine–American War"],["1909","Becomes resident commissioner in Washington"],["1916","Becomes president of the new Philippine Senate"],["1935","Elected president of the Commonwealth"],["1937","Proclaims a Tagalog-based national language"],["1944","Dies in the United States"]],
s:[["Early life","Manuel Luis Quezon y Molina was born on 19 August 1878 in Baler, then part of Tayabas province, now Aurora. His parents were teachers. He studied at the Colegio de San Juan de Letran and law at the University of Santo Tomas."],
["Soldier and lawyer","During the Philippine–American War, Quezon joined Aguinaldo’s army and became a major. After surrendering in 1901, he finished his law studies, passing the bar with high marks in 1903, and became a provincial prosecutor and then governor of Tayabas."],
["In Washington","In 1909 Quezon became one of the Philippines’ resident commissioners in the United States Congress. He campaigned for the Jones Act of 1916, which promised independence once a stable government was established, and created an elected Philippine Senate."],
["Senate president","Quezon served as president of the Philippine Senate from 1916 to 1935, becoming the most powerful Filipino politician. He led missions to Washington and helped win the Tydings–McDuffie Act of 1934, which set up the Commonwealth and promised independence after ten years."],
["Commonwealth president","Quezon was elected president in September 1935, defeating Emilio Aguinaldo, and took office on 15 November 1935. He built up national defense, launched social justice programs, supported women’s right to vote, approved in a 1937 plebiscite, and promoted land reform."],
["National language","Quezon believed Filipinos needed a common language. In 1937 he proclaimed Tagalog as the basis of the national language. He is called the Father of the National Language, and National Language Month, now Buwan ng Wika, is held in August, his birth month."],
["Helping refugees","Quezon supported a plan to welcome Jewish refugees fleeing Nazi persecution in Europe. Between 1937 and 1941 about 1,300 Jewish refugees settled in the Philippines, and Quezon offered land in Marikina for a settlement."],
["War and exile","When Japan invaded in December 1941, Quezon moved to Corregidor with General Douglas MacArthur, then escaped to Australia and the United States. He led the Commonwealth government in exile in Washington. He suffered from tuberculosis and died on 1 August 1944 at Saranac Lake, New York."],
["Quezon City","Quezon planned a new capital city in the hills northeast of Manila, with wide avenues and space for government buildings. Founded in 1939 and named after him, Quezon City served as the national capital from 1948 to 1976 and is now the country’s most populous city."],
["His legacy","Quezon is remembered for laying the foundations of an independent Philippines, though critics note he concentrated power in his own hands. His famous words, I would rather have a government run like hell by Filipinos than a government run like heaven by Americans, express his drive for self-rule."]],
sp:["Run like hell by Filipinos","Quezon is often quoted as saying he would prefer a government run like hell by Filipinos to one run like heaven by Americans, because a Filipino government could always be changed by Filipinos. The words express his belief that self-government, even imperfect, was better than rule by others. The quote remains popular in debates about Philippine independence and national identity."],
g:[["Commonwealth","A self-governing state linked to another country"],["Resident commissioner","A territory’s representative in the US Congress"],["Plebiscite","A direct vote by citizens on an issue"],["Government in exile","A government operating from abroad"]],
f:["Quezon City was the capital of the Philippines from 1948 to 1976.","Quezon’s face appears on the 20-peso bill and coin.","Quezon province and Quezon City are both named after him."]},

{id:"magsaysay",t:"Ramon Magsaysay",i:"🤝",w:"Ramon Magsaysay",a:["Ramon Magsaysay","Magsaysay"],born:"31 August 1907, Iba, Zambales",died:"17 March 1957, Mount Manunggal, Cebu",nat:"Filipino",known:"Popular presidency as the Champion of the Masses",r:["commonwealth","ph"],
l:"Ramon Magsaysay was the seventh president of the Philippines, serving from 1953 until his death in a plane crash in 1957. A former mechanic and guerrilla fighter, he was famous for his honesty, closeness to ordinary people and success against the Huk rebellion. He was called the Champion of the Masses.",
d:[["1907","Born in Iba, Zambales"],["1942–1945","Guerrilla leader during the Japanese occupation"],["1950","Becomes secretary of national defense"],["1953","Elected president in a landslide"],["1957","Killed in a plane crash in Cebu"],["1957","Ramon Magsaysay Award established"]],
s:[["Early life","Ramon del Fierro Magsaysay was born on 31 August 1907 in Iba, Zambales. His father was a carpenter and teacher. He studied engineering at the University of the Philippines and commerce at the José Rizal College, working as a chauffeur to support himself."],
["Mechanic and manager","Magsaysay worked as a mechanic and later as manager of a bus company. He loved machines and was known for being practical and hardworking. He married Luz Banzon in 1933, and they had three children."],
["War hero","When Japan invaded in 1941, Magsaysay joined the military and later led guerrilla forces in Zambales. By the end of the war, he commanded about 10,000 guerrillas. After liberation he was appointed military governor of Zambales."],
["Congressman","In 1946 Magsaysay was elected to the House of Representatives. He worked to secure benefits for war veterans and traveled to the United States to lobby for them. He became known for his energy and plain speaking."],
["Defeating the Huks","As defense secretary from 1950, Magsaysay fought the Hukbalahap, or Huk, communist rebellion. He reformed the army, punished abuses and offered land and help to rebels who surrendered. The rebellion weakened sharply, and his popularity soared."],
["President","In 1953 Magsaysay ran for president and won by a landslide. He opened Malacañang Palace to ordinary people, traveled widely to the provinces and set up a complaints office. He promoted rural development, land reform, roads and artesian wells."],
["Foreign policy","Magsaysay was a strong ally of the United States. In 1954 the Southeast Asia Treaty Organization, SEATO, was founded in Manila. Critics argued that he was too close to the Americans, while supporters valued his firm stand against communism."],
["Death","On 17 March 1957, the presidential plane Mt. Pinatubo crashed into Mount Manunggal in Cebu, killing Magsaysay and 24 others; one passenger, a journalist, survived. More than two million people are said to have attended his funeral in Manila."],
["The Ramon Magsaysay Award","Established in 1957, the Ramon Magsaysay Award is often called Asia’s Nobel Prize. It honors people and organizations in Asia for outstanding public service. Winners have included Mother Teresa, the Dalai Lama and many Filipinos."],
["His legacy","Magsaysay is remembered as one of the most beloved Filipino presidents, a man of the people who showed that honest leadership was possible. Monuments to him stand in many towns. Avenues and towns across the country carry his name."]],
sp:["Malacañang for the people","Before Magsaysay, Malacañang Palace was mostly closed to the public. He opened its gates so that farmers, workers and ordinary citizens could bring their problems directly to the president. He often wore a simple barong and ate with visitors. He also set up the Presidential Complaints and Action Commission so that complaints could be answered quickly. These gestures made him a symbol of a government that listened to the people."],
g:[["Guerrilla","A fighter in an irregular force"],["Rebellion","An armed uprising against a government"],["Landslide","An election won by a very large margin"],["Land reform","Redistributing land to farmers"]],
f:["Magsaysay was the first Philippine president born in the 20th century.","He was 49 when he died.","The Magsaysay Award is presented in Manila each year."]},

{id:"marcos",t:"Ferdinand Marcos",i:"⚖️",w:"Ferdinand Marcos",a:["Ferdinand Marcos","martial law"],born:"11 September 1917, Sarrat, Ilocos Norte",died:"28 September 1989, Honolulu, Hawaii, USA",nat:"Filipino",known:"Ruling under martial law until ousted by People Power in 1986",r:["edsa","ninoy","cory","phecon"],
l:"Ferdinand Marcos was the tenth president of the Philippines, ruling from 1965 to 1986. In 1972 he declared martial law and ruled with almost unlimited power. His supporters point to roads, schools and other projects built in his time; his rule is also remembered for widespread human rights abuses, corruption and debt. He was removed by the People Power Revolution in 1986.",
d:[["1917","Born in Sarrat, Ilocos Norte"],["1965","Elected president"],["1969","Re-elected"],["21 September 1972","Signs Proclamation 1081, declaring martial law"],["1981","Lifts martial law formally but keeps wide powers"],["February 1986","Ousted by the EDSA People Power Revolution"],["1989","Dies in exile in Hawaii"]],
s:[["Early life","Ferdinand Emmanuel Edralin Marcos was born on 11 September 1917 in Sarrat, Ilocos Norte. He studied law at the University of the Philippines. In 1939 he was convicted of the murder of a political rival of his father, but he argued his own appeal before the Supreme Court and was acquitted in 1940."],
["War record","During the Second World War, Marcos served in the army. He later claimed to have been a highly decorated guerrilla leader. Historians and United States military records have found that many of these claims were false or greatly exaggerated."],
["Rise to power","Marcos was elected to the House of Representatives in 1949 and the Senate in 1959. In 1954 he married Imelda Romuáldez. In 1965 he was elected president, and in 1969 he became the first president to win a second full term, in an election marked by heavy spending and violence."],
["Martial law","On 21 September 1972, citing threats from communist rebels and unrest, Marcos signed Proclamation 1081, placing the country under martial law; he announced it on 23 September. Congress was closed, opposition leaders and journalists were arrested, and newspapers and broadcasters were shut down or controlled."],
["Human rights","Under martial law, thousands of people were detained without trial. Amnesty International and other groups documented torture, killings and disappearances. In 2013 the Philippine government passed a law to compensate victims; a board recognized more than 11,000 claims."],
["The economy","The Marcos years saw new roads, bridges, schools and cultural buildings, such as the Cultural Center of the Philippines. Economic growth was strong in the 1970s, but foreign debt rose sharply and wealth was concentrated among close associates. In the early 1980s the economy fell into a severe crisis."],
["Corruption","After Marcos fell, investigators found evidence that he, his family and associates had taken billions of dollars from the country. Swiss courts returned hundreds of millions of dollars in deposits to the Philippines. Imelda Marcos became famous for her lavish lifestyle, including a collection of hundreds of pairs of shoes."],
["Downfall","The assassination of opposition leader Ninoy Aquino in 1983 sparked mass protests. Marcos called a snap election in February 1986 against Corazon Aquino. Widespread fraud led to the EDSA People Power Revolution, and on 25 February 1986 Marcos and his family fled to Hawaii."],
["After exile","Marcos died in Honolulu on 28 September 1989. His body was returned to Ilocos Norte in 1993 and buried in the Libingan ng mga Bayani, the Heroes’ Cemetery, in 2016, a decision that sparked protests. His son, Ferdinand Marcos Jr., was elected president in 2022."],
["A divided legacy","Marcos remains one of the most debated figures in Philippine history. Supporters remember stability and building projects; victims and historians emphasize the abuses, censorship and plunder of martial law. Teaching this period accurately is widely considered important for understanding Philippine democracy."]],
sp:["Remembering martial law","The Human Rights Victims Reparation and Recognition Act of 2013 officially recognized victims of human rights violations during the Marcos regime and set up a memorial commission. The Bantayog ng mga Bayani in Quezon City honors people who fought the dictatorship, with names carved on a Wall of Remembrance. Each September, Filipinos mark the anniversary of the declaration of martial law with discussions, exhibits and protests, so that its lessons are not forgotten."],
g:[["Martial law","Military rule replacing normal civil law"],["Dictatorship","Rule by one person with unlimited power"],["Human rights","Basic rights and freedoms of every person"],["Snap election","An election called earlier than scheduled"]],
f:["Martial law officially lasted from 1972 to 1981.","Marcos ruled for more than 20 years, longer than any other Philippine president.","EDSA, where People Power took place, is Metro Manila’s main highway."]},

{id:"ninoy",t:"Ninoy Aquino",i:"🕊️",w:"Benigno Aquino Jr.",a:["Ninoy Aquino","Benigno Aquino Jr."],born:"27 November 1932, Concepcion, Tarlac",died:"21 August 1983, Manila International Airport",nat:"Filipino",known:"Opposing martial law; his assassination sparked People Power",r:["cory","marcos","edsa"],
l:"Benigno Aquino Jr., known as Ninoy, was a journalist, senator and the leading opponent of Ferdinand Marcos. Jailed for more than seven years under martial law and then exiled, he was shot dead on the airport tarmac as he returned to Manila in 1983. His death united the opposition and led to the People Power Revolution.",
d:[["1932","Born in Concepcion, Tarlac"],["1950","Covers the Korean War as a young reporter"],["1955","Elected mayor of Concepcion at 22"],["1967","Elected senator"],["1972","Arrested when martial law is declared"],["1980","Allowed to go to the United States for heart surgery"],["21 August 1983","Assassinated on his return to Manila"]],
s:[["Early life","Benigno Simeon Aquino Jr. was born on 27 November 1932 in Concepcion, Tarlac, into a political family. His father and grandfather were politicians. He was a bright and restless student and began working as a journalist while still a teenager."],
["Young reporter","At 17, Ninoy covered the Korean War for the Manila Times, earning a Philippine Legion of Honor award. He later helped negotiate the surrender of Huk rebel leader Luis Taruc in 1954, gaining national attention."],
["Youngest politician","Ninoy became mayor of Concepcion at 22, vice governor of Tarlac at 27 and governor at 29. In 1967, at 34, he became the youngest elected senator in Philippine history. He married Corazon Cojuangco in 1954, and they had five children."],
["Critic of Marcos","In the Senate, Ninoy became the sharpest critic of President Marcos, warning that Marcos planned to rule indefinitely. He was widely expected to win the presidential election due in 1973, which never took place."],
["Prison","When martial law was declared in 1972, Ninoy was among the first arrested. He was held for more than seven years, much of it in solitary confinement, and a military court sentenced him to death in 1977. He went on a 40-day hunger strike in protest."],
["Exile","In 1980, after a heart attack, Ninoy was allowed to go to the United States for surgery. He lived in Boston with his family, teaching at Harvard and MIT and speaking against the dictatorship. In 1983 he decided to return home, despite warnings that he would be killed."],
["Assassination","On 21 August 1983, Ninoy flew into Manila International Airport. Moments after soldiers escorted him off the plane, he was shot in the head on the stairs to the tarmac. A man named Rolando Galman, blamed by the military, was also killed. Courts later convicted soldiers of the murder."],
["A nation awakens","About two million people joined Ninoy’s funeral procession, one of the largest in Philippine history. Protests grew, and his widow, Corazon Aquino, became the leader of the opposition. Within three years, the People Power Revolution ended the Marcos regime."],
["The airport","In 1987 Manila International Airport was renamed Ninoy Aquino International Airport, NAIA, in his honor. 21 August is a national holiday, Ninoy Aquino Day. His face appears on the 500-peso bill alongside his wife, Corazon."],
["His legacy","Ninoy is remembered as a symbol of courage and sacrifice for democracy. His son Benigno Aquino III served as president from 2010 to 2016. His words and example continue to inspire Filipinos who defend freedom and the rule of law."]],
sp:["The Filipino is worth dying for","In a speech he prepared to deliver on his arrival in Manila, Ninoy wrote that he had returned to join in the struggle for freedom through nonviolence. Earlier, he had told an audience in the United States that the Filipino is worth dying for. He never delivered his arrival speech, but its words, published after his death, became a rallying cry for the movement that restored democracy in 1986."],
g:[["Opposition","Politicians who challenge those in power"],["Solitary confinement","Imprisonment alone in a cell"],["Hunger strike","Refusing food as a protest"],["Tarmac","The paved area of an airport"]],
f:["Ninoy became a senator at 34.","He was in prison for more than seven years.","Ninoy Aquino Day, 21 August, is a national holiday."]}
);

;
/* data/people3.js */
// People, part 3: modern Filipinos.
window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"cory",t:"Corazon Aquino",i:"💛",w:"Corazon Aquino",a:["Corazon Aquino","Cory Aquino"],born:"25 January 1933, Paniqui, Tarlac",died:"1 August 2009, Makati",nat:"Filipino",known:"Leading the restoration of democracy as the first woman president of the Philippines",r:["ninoy","edsa","marcos"],
l:"Corazon Aquino was the 11th president of the Philippines and the first woman to hold the office. A shy homemaker until the assassination of her husband, Ninoy Aquino, she led the opposition against Ferdinand Marcos and became president after the People Power Revolution of 1986. She restored democratic institutions and oversaw a new constitution.",
d:[["1933","Born in Paniqui, Tarlac"],["1954","Marries Benigno Aquino Jr."],["1983","Ninoy is assassinated"],["February 1986","Becomes president after People Power"],["1987","New Constitution approved"],["1992","Hands over power peacefully"],["2009","Dies of cancer"]],
s:[["Early life","Maria Corazon Sumulong Cojuangco was born on 25 January 1933 in Paniqui, Tarlac, into a wealthy family of landowners and politicians. She studied in Manila and the United States, graduating from the College of Mount Saint Vincent in New York with a degree in French and mathematics."],
["Wife of a politician","In 1954 she married Benigno Aquino Jr., a rising young politician. For years she stayed in the background, raising their five children. When Ninoy was imprisoned under martial law, she supported him and carried his messages to the outside world."],
["Widow and leader","After Ninoy was assassinated in 1983, Cory became the symbol of opposition to Marcos. Wearing yellow, the color of the protest movement, she led rallies across the country. In 1985 she agreed to run for president after more than a million people signed a petition urging her to."],
["The snap election","In the snap election of 7 February 1986, Marcos was declared the winner, but there was widespread evidence of fraud. Computer workers counting votes walked out in protest. Aquino called for nonviolent civil disobedience and a boycott of companies linked to Marcos."],
["People Power","On 22 February 1986, military leaders broke with Marcos, and millions of Filipinos filled EDSA to protect them. On 25 February, Aquino was sworn in as president, and Marcos fled the country. The peaceful revolution inspired democracy movements around the world."],
["Restoring democracy","As president, Aquino released political prisoners, restored freedom of the press and set up a commission to recover wealth taken by the Marcos family. A new constitution, approved in a plebiscite in February 1987, limited presidents to a single six-year term."],
["Challenges","Her government faced many problems: a weak economy, huge debts, communist and Muslim rebellions, natural disasters and power shortages. Rebel soldiers attempted several coups, the most serious in December 1989. Critics said land reform did not go far enough."],
["Peaceful handover","In 1992 Aquino handed over power to her elected successor, Fidel Ramos, the first peaceful transfer of power between elected presidents in more than two decades. She returned to private life but spoke out on public issues."],
["Honors","Time magazine named Aquino its Woman of the Year for 1986. She received many international awards for promoting democracy and peace. After her death from colon cancer in 2009, crowds lined the streets for her funeral, and her face was placed beside Ninoy’s on the 500-peso bill."],
["Her legacy","Corazon Aquino is remembered as the mother of Philippine democracy and an example of quiet, principled courage. Her son Benigno Aquino III was elected president in 2010. The image of ordinary people and nuns facing tanks with flowers and rosaries remains an enduring symbol of her era."]],
sp:["Yellow ribbons","Yellow became the color of the Aquino movement because of the song Tie a Yellow Ribbon Round the Ole Oak Tree, about a prisoner returning home. Supporters tied yellow ribbons on trees to welcome Ninoy back in 1983. After his death, yellow became the color of protest. Cory wore yellow throughout her campaign, and yellow confetti, often made from torn telephone directory pages, rained down from office buildings along Ayala Avenue during rallies."],
g:[["Civil disobedience","Peacefully refusing to obey unjust laws"],["Coup","A sudden attempt to seize power by force"],["Constitution","The basic law of a country"],["Transfer of power","Handing government to a successor"]],
f:["Cory Aquino was the first woman president in Asia.","Her presidency lasted from 1986 to 1992.","She majored in French and mathematics."]},

{id:"fedelmundo",t:"Fe del Mundo",i:"🩺",w:"Fe del Mundo",a:["Fe del Mundo"],born:"27 November 1911, Manila",died:"6 August 2011, Quezon City",nat:"Filipino",known:"Pioneering pediatrics in the Philippines",r:["vacc","heart"],
l:"Fe del Mundo was a Filipino pediatrician, a doctor for children, who founded the first pediatric hospital in the Philippines. She devoted more than 70 years to caring for children, especially the poor, and developed simple medical devices for rural areas. She was named a National Scientist of the Philippines in 1980.",
d:[["1911","Born in Manila"],["1933","Graduates in medicine from the University of the Philippines"],["1936","Studies pediatrics at Harvard"],["1957","Opens the Children’s Medical Center in Quezon City"],["1980","Named National Scientist"],["2011","Dies aged 99"]],
s:[["Early life","Fe Villanueva del Mundo was born on 27 November 1911 in Intramuros, Manila, one of eight children. Several of her siblings died young, and the death of a sister from illness inspired her to become a doctor who would help children."],
["Medical school","Del Mundo studied medicine at the University of the Philippines, graduating as valedictorian in 1933. She received a government scholarship to study further in the United States, choosing pediatrics, the medical care of children."],
["At Harvard","In 1936, del Mundo became the first woman admitted to Harvard Medical School’s pediatrics program, at a time when the school did not accept women. According to her account, Harvard had assumed from her application that she was a man. She later studied at the University of Chicago and Boston University."],
["War work","During the Japanese occupation, del Mundo cared for children held in the Santo Tomas internment camp in Manila and set up a children’s shelter. People called her the Angel of Santo Tomas. After the war she directed a government hospital in Manila."],
["The Children’s Medical Center","In 1957 del Mundo sold her house and belongings to found the Children’s Medical Center in Quezon City, the first pediatric hospital in the Philippines. It is now part of the Fe del Mundo Medical Center. She lived in the hospital for many years."],
["The bamboo incubator","Many rural areas lacked electricity and expensive equipment. Del Mundo designed an incubator for newborn babies made of bamboo, which could be warmed with hot water bottles. She also devised a jaundice relief device and promoted simple methods suited to Philippine villages."],
["Public health","Del Mundo promoted vaccination, breastfeeding, nutrition and family planning. She trained health workers and midwives to work in rural communities and wrote more than 100 research papers and a widely used pediatrics textbook for medical students."],
["Lifelong work","Del Mundo kept working well into her nineties, making hospital rounds in a wheelchair. She never married, saying her patients were her children. She died on 6 August 2011, aged 99."],
["Honors","She received the Ramon Magsaysay Award for Public Service in 1977 and was named a National Scientist of the Philippines in 1980. In 2010 she was declared an Order of Lakandula recipient. Google honored her with a Doodle on her 107th birthday in 2018."],
["Her legacy","Fe del Mundo is celebrated as a pioneer for women in science and medicine and a champion of children’s health. Her life shows how a doctor can combine scientific skill with deep compassion and practical solutions for poor communities."]],
sp:["Angel of Santo Tomas","During the Second World War, the Japanese held thousands of American and European civilians in the University of Santo Tomas campus in Manila. Fe del Mundo volunteered to care for the children there. She set up a home for them and later convinced authorities to let her take children out to safer places. When the Japanese closed her shelter, she continued caring for children in a hospital that became the North General Hospital after the war."],
g:[["Pediatrics","The medical care of children"],["Incubator","A warm enclosure for newborn babies"],["Jaundice","A yellowing of the skin, common in newborns"],["National Scientist","The Philippines’ highest honor for scientists"]],
f:["Fe del Mundo worked as a doctor for about 75 years.","She was valedictorian of her medical class.","She designed an incubator made of bamboo."]},

{id:"lorenzoruiz",t:"San Lorenzo Ruiz",i:"✝️",w:"Lorenzo Ruiz",a:["Lorenzo Ruiz"],born:"c. 1600, Binondo, Manila",died:"29 September 1637, Nagasaki, Japan",nat:"Filipino (Chinese-Filipino)",known:"Being the first Filipino saint",r:["christ","spanph","manila"],
l:"Lorenzo Ruiz was a Filipino lay Catholic who was killed in Nagasaki, Japan, in 1637 for refusing to renounce his faith. In 1987 Pope John Paul II declared him a saint, the first Filipino saint. He is the patron saint of Filipino migrant workers and of the Philippines’ Chinese community.",
d:[["c. 1600","Born in Binondo, Manila"],["1636","Sails to Japan with Dominican missionaries"],["29 September 1637","Dies in Nagasaki"],["1981","Beatified in Manila by Pope John Paul II"],["18 October 1987","Canonized in Rome"]],
s:[["Early life","Lorenzo Ruiz was born around 1600 in Binondo, Manila, to a Chinese father and a Filipino mother. He served as an altar boy and clerk at the Binondo church run by Dominican friars, and he became a calligrapher, copying documents in beautiful handwriting."],
["Family","Ruiz married a Filipina, Rosario, and they had two sons and a daughter. He was a member of the Confraternity of the Holy Rosary. Life in Binondo, a district for Chinese residents, mixed Chinese and Filipino culture."],
["Fleeing Manila","In 1636 Ruiz was accused of a crime, possibly involvement in a killing. The details are unclear. Seeking safety, he joined a group of Dominican missionaries sailing to Japan, leaving his family behind."],
["Persecution in Japan","At that time, Japan’s rulers had banned Christianity and were persecuting Christians and foreign missionaries. When Ruiz’s group landed in Okinawa, they were arrested and taken to Nagasaki for trial."],
["Trial and torture","Ruiz and his companions were tortured to force them to renounce their faith. They were subjected to water torture and other cruelties. Ruiz refused. He is reported to have said that he would give his life for God a thousand times if he had a thousand lives."],
["Death","On 27 September 1637, the prisoners were hung upside down over a pit, a torture called tsurushi. Ruiz died on 29 September 1637. His body was burned and the ashes thrown into the sea. He was one of 16 martyrs, including Japanese, Spanish, French and Italian Christians."],
["Beatification in Manila","On 18 February 1981, during his visit to the Philippines, Pope John Paul II beatified Ruiz in Manila, the first beatification held outside the Vatican. Millions attended the ceremony at Luneta Park."],
["Saint Lorenzo","On 18 October 1987, John Paul II canonized Ruiz in Rome, along with 15 companion martyrs. He became the first Filipino saint. His feast day is 28 September. In 2012, Pedro Calungsod became the second Filipino saint."],
["Patron of migrants","Because Ruiz left home and died in a foreign land, he is the patron saint of Filipino overseas workers. Many Filipino communities abroad have chapels or groups dedicated to him. Binondo Church in Manila has a shrine in his honor."],
["His legacy","San Lorenzo Ruiz is honored as a symbol of faith, courage and the Filipino diaspora. Schools, parishes and a town in Camarines Norte are named after him. His story also reflects the long history of Chinese-Filipino families in Manila."]],
sp:["A thousand lives","According to records of the trial, when the judges asked Ruiz whether he would give up his faith in exchange for his life, he replied that he was a Christian and would die for God, and that if he had a thousand lives he would offer them all. These words, preserved by the Dominicans, are often quoted by Filipino Catholics. They appear on statues and paintings of the saint, who is usually shown with a rosary and holding his hands together in prayer."],
g:[["Martyr","A person killed for their beliefs"],["Canonization","The Church’s declaration that someone is a saint"],["Beatification","A step toward sainthood"],["Diaspora","People living away from their homeland"]],
f:["Lorenzo Ruiz was a calligrapher.","His feast day is 28 September.","Pedro Calungsod became the second Filipino saint in 2012."]},

{id:"salonga",t:"Lea Salonga",i:"🎤",w:"Lea Salonga",a:["Lea Salonga"],born:"22 February 1971, Manila",died:"",nat:"Filipino",known:"Starring in Miss Saigon and singing for Disney princesses",r:["opm","phdance","anim"],
l:"Lea Salonga is a Filipino singer and actress who won international fame playing Kim in the musical Miss Saigon. She was the first Asian woman to win a Tony Award and is the singing voice of two Disney princesses, Jasmine in Aladdin and Mulan. She is one of the most celebrated Filipino performers in the world.",
d:[["1971","Born in Manila"],["1978","First professional stage role at age 7"],["1989","Stars in Miss Saigon in London"],["1991","Wins a Tony Award on Broadway"],["1992","Sings Jasmine in Aladdin"],["1998","Sings Mulan"],["2011","Named a Disney Legend"]],
s:[["Child star","Maria Lea Carmen Imutan Salonga was born on 22 February 1971 in Manila. She began performing as a child, appearing in a production of The King and I at age 7. She also acted in films and television shows and recorded her first album, Small Voice, at 10."],
["Miss Saigon","In 1989, at 17, Salonga was chosen to star as Kim, a young Vietnamese woman, in the new musical Miss Saigon in London’s West End. The producers had searched the world for the role, auditioning many singers in Manila. She won the Laurence Olivier Award for best actress in a musical in 1990."],
["Broadway and the Tony","When Miss Saigon opened on Broadway in New York in 1991, Salonga won the Tony Award for Best Actress in a Musical, becoming the first Asian woman to win a Tony. She also won Drama Desk and Outer Critics Circle awards."],
["Disney voices","Salonga provided the singing voice of Princess Jasmine in Aladdin in 1992, including the Oscar-winning song A Whole New World, and of Mulan in 1998, singing Reflection. In 2011 Disney named her a Disney Legend."],
["Les Misérables","Salonga was the first Asian actress to play Éponine in Les Misérables on Broadway in 1993, and she later played Fantine. She sang in the 10th anniversary concert of Les Misérables at the Royal Albert Hall in 1995."],
["Continuing career","Salonga has starred in many musicals, including Flower Drum Song, Allegiance and Once on This Island. She has recorded albums, performed concerts worldwide and acted in films and television. She has received several honors in the Philippines."],
["Coach and judge","Salonga served as a coach on The Voice of the Philippines and The Voice Kids, helping young singers. Her advice and warm personality made her a popular television figure."],
["Training","Salonga trained in singing, piano and acting from a young age. She studied briefly at Fordham University in New York. She has spoken about the importance of discipline, rest and healthy habits for protecting the voice."],
["Representation","Salonga’s success opened doors for Asian performers in theater. She has spoken about the challenges Asian actors face and the importance of diverse casting. Many Filipino singers have followed her to stages in London and New York."],
["Her legacy","Lea Salonga is a source of national pride, showing that Filipino talent can shine on the world’s biggest stages. She has received many awards in the Philippines and abroad for her contributions to the arts."]],
sp:["Filipinos in Miss Saigon","After Lea Salonga’s success, many Filipino performers were cast in Miss Saigon productions around the world, playing Kim, the Engineer and other roles. The musical became a training ground for a generation of Filipino singers. A Filipino-led cast also appeared in the 25th anniversary production in London in 2014, starring Eva Noblezada, a Filipino-American, as Kim, and Jon Jon Briones as the Engineer."],
g:[["Musical","A play told through songs and dance"],["West End","London’s main theater district"],["Broadway","New York’s main theater district"],["Tony Award","America’s top award for theater"]],
f:["Lea Salonga was 17 when she first played Kim.","She sang A Whole New World in Aladdin.","She recorded her first album at age 10."]},

{id:"hidilyn",t:"Hidilyn Diaz",i:"🏋️",w:"Hidilyn Diaz",a:["Hidilyn Diaz"],born:"20 February 1991, Zamboanga City",died:"",nat:"Filipino",known:"Winning the Philippines’ first Olympic gold medal",r:["olymp","phsport"],
l:"Hidilyn Diaz is a Filipino weightlifter who won the Philippines’ first-ever Olympic gold medal at the Tokyo 2020 Games, held in 2021. Lifting a total of 224 kilograms in the women’s 55 kg category and setting an Olympic record, she ended a 97-year wait and became a national hero.",
d:[["1991","Born in Zamboanga City"],["2008","Competes at her first Olympics in Beijing, aged 17"],["2016","Wins silver at the Rio Olympics"],["2018","Wins gold at the Asian Games"],["26 July 2021","Wins Olympic gold in Tokyo"]],
s:[["Early life","Hidilyn Francisco Diaz was born on 20 February 1991 in Zamboanga City, the fifth of six children of a tricycle driver and a homemaker. As a child she helped sell vegetables and fish. She began lifting weights at 11, using homemade barbells made from plastic pipes and concrete."],
["First Olympics","Diaz’s cousin introduced her to weightlifting. She showed great talent, and at 17 she competed at the 2008 Beijing Olympics, the first Filipino woman weightlifter at the Games. She finished 11th. At London 2012 she failed to complete a lift."],
["Silver in Rio","At the Rio de Janeiro Olympics in 2016, Diaz won silver in the women’s 53 kg category. It was the Philippines’ first Olympic medal in 20 years and the first Olympic medal won by a Filipino woman."],
["Asian champion","In 2018 Diaz won gold at the Asian Games in Jakarta and Palembang. She also won gold at the Southeast Asian Games in 2019. She also serves in the Philippine Air Force."],
["Training through difficulty","When the COVID-19 pandemic struck in 2020, Diaz was stranded in Malaysia, where she was training. She trained in a small space using water jugs and makeshift weights. She also faced online criticism and funding problems."],
["Tokyo gold","On 26 July 2021, at the Tokyo Olympics, Diaz lifted 97 kg in the snatch and 127 kg in the clean and jerk, a total of 224 kg, beating China’s world record holder Liao Qiuyun. Her final lift set an Olympic record. She wept with joy on the podium as the Philippine national anthem played."],
["Rewards and recognition","After her victory, Diaz received millions of pesos in cash rewards, houses and lifetime benefits from the government and private companies. She was promoted in the Air Force and honored across the country. Her story inspired a surge of interest in weightlifting."],
["Life after Tokyo","Diaz married her coach, Julius Naranjo, in 2022. She founded a weightlifting academy to train young athletes and has spoken about the importance of supporting athletes with funding, sports science and mental health care."],
["Weightlifting explained","Olympic weightlifting has two lifts. In the snatch, the bar is lifted from the floor to overhead in one movement. In the clean and jerk, it is lifted to the shoulders, then pushed overhead. The best result in each is added for the total."],
["Her legacy","Hidilyn Diaz showed that Filipino athletes can win the highest prize in sport. Three years later, gymnast Carlos Yulo won two golds in Paris. Her journey from homemade barbells to Olympic champion inspires young Filipinos to pursue their dreams."]],
sp:["A 97-year wait","The Philippines first competed at the Olympics in Paris in 1924. Over the next 97 years, Filipino athletes won silver and bronze medals in boxing, swimming, athletics and weightlifting, but never gold. When Hidilyn Diaz lifted 127 kg in her final clean and jerk in Tokyo, she ended the wait. Millions of Filipinos watched on television and online, and her victory became one of the most celebrated moments in Philippine sports history."],
g:[["Snatch","A lift from floor to overhead in one movement"],["Clean and jerk","A lift to the shoulders, then overhead"],["Olympic record","The best result ever achieved at the Olympics"],["Podium","The platform where medalists stand"]],
f:["Hidilyn Diaz trained as a child with homemade barbells.","She competed in four Olympic Games.","She lifted 224 kg in total to win gold."]},

{id:"pacquiao",t:"Manny Pacquiao",i:"🥊",w:"Manny Pacquiao",a:["Manny Pacquiao","Pacquiao"],born:"17 December 1978, Kibawe, Bukidnon",died:"",nat:"Filipino",known:"Winning world boxing titles in eight weight divisions",r:["martial","phsport"],
l:"Manny Pacquiao is a Filipino boxer and politician, widely regarded as one of the greatest boxers of all time. He is the only boxer to win world titles in eight different weight divisions. Rising from poverty in Mindanao, he became a national hero and later served as a congressman and senator.",
d:[["1978","Born in Kibawe, Bukidnon"],["1995","Turns professional at 16"],["1998","Wins his first world title, at flyweight"],["2008","Defeats Oscar De La Hoya"],["2010","Wins an eighth division title"],["2016–2022","Serves as senator"],["2025","Returns to the ring at 46"]],
s:[["Childhood","Emmanuel Dapidran Pacquiao was born on 17 December 1978 in Kibawe, Bukidnon, and grew up in General Santos City. His family was very poor, and he left school to work, selling bread and doing odd jobs. At 14 he moved to Manila, where he worked and began boxing."],
["Early career","Pacquiao turned professional in 1995 at 16, weighing just 48 kilograms. In 1998 he won his first world title, the WBC flyweight championship, in Thailand. His speed, power and aggressive left hand soon made him famous."],
["Climbing the divisions","Over the next 12 years, Pacquiao won world titles in ever-heavier weight divisions, from flyweight to light middleweight, gaining more than 20 kilograms. No other boxer has won world titles in eight divisions."],
["Great fights","Pacquiao’s famous fights included wins over Marco Antonio Barrera, Erik Morales, Juan Manuel Márquez in a four-fight rivalry, Oscar De La Hoya in 2008 and Miguel Cotto in 2009. His 2015 fight against Floyd Mayweather Jr., which he lost on points, was one of the richest in boxing history."],
["Fighting style","Pacquiao was a southpaw, a left-handed boxer, known for extraordinary speed, footwork and the ability to throw punches from unusual angles. He trained for many years with American coach Freddie Roach at the Wild Card Gym in Los Angeles."],
["National hero","When Pacquiao fought, the Philippines seemed to stop. Streets emptied as people gathered to watch, and some reports said crime dropped. Soldiers and rebels were said to pause fighting. He became one of the most famous Filipinos in the world."],
["Politics","Pacquiao was elected to the House of Representatives in 2010, representing Sarangani province, and to the Senate in 2016. He ran for president in 2022 but lost. His political career drew both support and criticism for his views and frequent absences."],
["Other interests","Pacquiao has acted in films, recorded songs, played professional basketball as a player-coach in the Philippine Basketball Association and become a devoted Christian preacher. He also founded charities and built houses for the poor."],
["Comeback","Pacquiao retired in 2021 but returned in July 2025, at 46, to fight Mario Barrios for the WBC welterweight title. The fight ended in a draw, showing his remarkable fitness at an age when most boxers have long retired."],
["His legacy","Manny Pacquiao’s rise from selling bread on the streets to boxing’s highest honors makes him a symbol of Filipino determination. He is admired for his humility and generosity, and his achievement of eight division titles may never be equaled."]],
sp:["Pacman","Pacquiao’s nickname, Pacman, came from fans and the media, who compared him to the video game character for the way he relentlessly attacked his opponents. His fights drew huge audiences on pay-per-view television worldwide. In the Philippines, his matches were often shown free in town plazas, gyms and even churches, so that everyone could watch the Pambansang Kamao, the National Fist."],
g:[["Southpaw","A left-handed boxer"],["Weight division","A class grouping boxers by weight"],["Pay-per-view","Television watched for a fee"],["Draw","A match with no winner"]],
f:["Pacquiao turned professional at 16.","His nickname is Pacman, and Filipinos call him the National Fist.","He once played and coached in the PBA."]}
);

;
/* data/people4.js */
// People, part 4: scientists and mathematicians.
window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"einstein",t:"Albert Einstein",i:"🧑‍🔬",w:"Albert Einstein",a:["Albert Einstein","Einstein"],born:"14 March 1879, Ulm, Germany",died:"18 April 1955, Princeton, New Jersey, USA",nat:"German-born; later Swiss and American",known:"The theory of relativity and E = mc²",r:["relat","quantum","light","nuclear"],
l:"Albert Einstein was a physicist whose theories of relativity changed our understanding of space, time, gravity and energy. His equation E = mc² showed that mass and energy are linked. He won the Nobel Prize in Physics, and his name has become a byword for genius.",
d:[["1879","Born in Ulm, Germany"],["1905","His miracle year: four groundbreaking papers"],["1915","Completes the general theory of relativity"],["1919","Eclipse observations confirm his predictions"],["1922","Receives the 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics"],["1933","Moves to the United States"],["1955","Dies in Princeton"]],
s:[["Childhood","Albert Einstein was born on 14 March 1879 in Ulm, Germany, and grew up in Munich. He was fascinated by a compass his father showed him at age five, wondering what invisible force moved the needle. He loved mathematics and taught himself geometry."],
["Student and patent clerk","Einstein disliked the strict, rote learning of German schools. He studied physics and mathematics at the Federal Polytechnic in Zurich, Switzerland, graduating in 1900. Unable to find a university job, he worked from 1902 at the Swiss patent office in Bern, examining inventions."],
["The miracle year","In 1905, while still a patent clerk, Einstein published four papers that transformed physics. They explained the photoelectric effect, showing that light comes in packets called photons; proved that atoms exist through Brownian motion; introduced special relativity; and showed that E = mc²."],
["Special relativity","Special relativity says that the laws of physics are the same for everyone moving at a steady speed and that the speed of light is always the same. As a result, time runs slower and lengths shrink for fast-moving objects. These effects are tiny at everyday speeds but real."],
["E = mc²","The equation E = mc² means that energy equals mass times the speed of light squared. Because the speed of light is so large, a tiny amount of mass contains an enormous amount of energy. This explains how the Sun shines and how nuclear power and nuclear weapons work."],
["General relativity","In 1915 Einstein published general relativity, describing gravity as the bending of space and time by mass. It predicted that light bends around the Sun, confirmed during a solar eclipse in 1919, which made Einstein world-famous. It also predicted black holes and gravitational waves."],
["Nobel Prize","Einstein received the Nobel Prize in Physics for 1921, awarded in 1922, not for relativity but for explaining the photoelectric effect. This work helped launch quantum physics, although Einstein later doubted parts of quantum theory, famously saying God does not play dice."],
["Leaving Germany","Einstein was Jewish, and when the Nazis came to power in 1933 he left Germany and settled in Princeton, New Jersey, at the Institute for Advanced Study. In 1939 he signed a letter warning President Franklin Roosevelt that Germany might build an atomic bomb, which contributed to the US starting its own program."],
["Peace and later life","Einstein did not work on the atomic bomb and later regretted signing the letter. He campaigned for peace, civil rights and nuclear disarmament. In 1952 he was offered the presidency of Israel but declined. He spent his last years searching unsuccessfully for a theory unifying all forces."],
["His legacy","Einstein died on 18 April 1955. His theories underpin modern physics, from GPS satellites, which must correct for relativity, to the study of black holes and the Big Bang. Time magazine named him Person of the Century in 1999."]],
sp:["GPS and relativity","GPS satellites carry extremely accurate atomic clocks. Because they move fast, special relativity makes their clocks run slightly slow; because they are farther from Earth’s gravity, general relativity makes them run slightly fast. The net effect is that satellite clocks gain about 38 microseconds a day. Without correcting for Einstein’s theories, GPS positions would drift by several kilometers each day. Every time you use a map on your phone, Einstein’s physics is at work."],
g:[["Relativity","Einstein’s theories of space, time and gravity"],["Photon","A particle of light"],["Spacetime","Space and time combined into one"],["Photoelectric effect","Light knocking electrons out of metal"]],
f:["Einstein’s brain was preserved for study after his death.","The element einsteinium is named after him.","He played the violin throughout his life."]},

{id:"darwin",t:"Charles Darwin",i:"🐢",w:"Charles Darwin",a:["Charles Darwin","Darwin"],born:"12 February 1809, Shrewsbury, England",died:"19 April 1882, Downe, Kent, England",nat:"British",known:"The theory of evolution by natural selection",r:["evo","mendel","eco"],
l:"Charles Darwin was an English naturalist whose theory of evolution by natural selection explains how living things change over many generations. After a five-year voyage around the world on HMS Beagle, he spent decades gathering evidence before publishing On the Origin of Species in 1859, one of the most influential books ever written.",
d:[["1809","Born in Shrewsbury, England"],["1831","Sets sail on HMS Beagle"],["1835","Visits the Galápagos Islands"],["1836","Returns to England"],["1858","His and Alfred Russel Wallace’s ideas are presented together"],["1859","Publishes On the Origin of Species"],["1882","Dies; buried in Westminster Abbey"]],
s:[["Early life","Charles Robert Darwin was born on 12 February 1809 in Shrewsbury, England, the son of a wealthy doctor. As a boy he collected beetles, shells and rocks. He began studying medicine at Edinburgh but disliked surgery and then studied at Cambridge to become a clergyman."],
["The Beagle voyage","In 1831, at 22, Darwin joined HMS Beagle as a naturalist and companion to the captain on a survey voyage. Over five years, the ship sailed around South America and the world. Darwin collected thousands of specimens, studied rocks and fossils and kept detailed notebooks."],
["The Galápagos","In 1835 the Beagle visited the Galápagos Islands off Ecuador. Darwin noticed that giant tortoises and mockingbirds differed from island to island. Back home, he realized that species might change to suit their environments, a key step toward his theory."],
["Natural selection","Darwin’s theory says that individuals in a species vary, and more are born than can survive. Those with traits that help them survive and reproduce pass those traits on. Over many generations, populations change, and new species can arise. He called this natural selection."],
["Years of evidence","Darwin worked on his theory for more than 20 years, breeding pigeons, studying barnacles and corresponding with scientists worldwide. He was cautious, knowing his ideas would be controversial. He lived quietly at Down House in Kent with his wife Emma and their children."],
["Wallace","In 1858 Darwin received a letter from Alfred Russel Wallace, a naturalist working in Southeast Asia, describing almost the same theory. Their ideas were presented together to the Linnean Society in London. Darwin then rushed to complete his book."],
["On the Origin of Species","Published on 24 November 1859, On the Origin of Species sold out on its first day. It presented overwhelming evidence for evolution from fossils, anatomy, breeding and the distribution of species. It provoked fierce debate, especially about humans’ place in nature."],
["Later works","In The Descent of Man, published in 1871, Darwin argued that humans evolved from earlier primates. He also wrote about orchids, earthworms, emotions in animals and climbing plants. He suffered from long-lasting illness for much of his adult life."],
["Evidence since Darwin","Darwin did not know how traits were inherited. Later discoveries in genetics, beginning with Gregor Mendel’s work, and the discovery of DNA confirmed and expanded his theory. Today evolution is the central idea of biology, supported by evidence from many fields."],
["His legacy","Darwin died on 19 April 1882 and was buried in Westminster Abbey near Isaac Newton. His portrait appeared on the British £10 note. His ideas changed how people understand life on Earth and our connection to all other living things."]],
sp:["Darwin’s finches","The small birds of the Galápagos now called Darwin’s finches have beaks of different shapes, suited to different foods: thick beaks for cracking seeds, thin beaks for catching insects. Darwin collected them but did not label them carefully. Later scientists, especially Peter and Rosemary Grant, who studied the finches for decades from the 1970s, observed their beaks changing in size within a few generations after droughts, watching natural selection happen in real time."],
g:[["Naturalist","A person who studies nature"],["Natural selection","Survival and reproduction of the best-suited individuals"],["Species","A group of living things that can breed together"],["Specimen","A sample collected for study"]],
f:["Darwin and Abraham Lincoln were born on the same day.","The Beagle voyage lasted almost five years.","Darwin studied earthworms for about 40 years."]},

{id:"newtonp",t:"Isaac Newton",i:"🍎",w:"Isaac Newton",a:["Isaac Newton"],born:"25 December 1642 (old calendar), Woolsthorpe, England",died:"20 March 1727 (old calendar), London",nat:"English",known:"Laws of motion, universal gravitation and calculus",r:["newton","grav","calc","light","copern"],
l:"Isaac Newton was an English mathematician and physicist whose discoveries laid the foundations of modern science. He described the laws of motion and universal gravitation, developed calculus and showed that white light is made of colors. His book Principia is one of the most important scientific works ever published.",
d:[["1642","Born in Woolsthorpe, Lincolnshire"],["1665–1666","Plague years: develops calculus, optics and gravity ideas"],["1668","Builds the first practical reflecting telescope"],["1687","Publishes the Principia"],["1703","Becomes president of the Royal Society"],["1705","Knighted"],["1727","Dies in London; buried in Westminster Abbey"]],
s:[["Childhood","Isaac Newton was born on Christmas Day 1642, by the calendar then used in England, in Woolsthorpe, Lincolnshire. His father died before he was born, and he was raised mainly by his grandmother. As a boy he built model windmills, sundials and water clocks."],
["Cambridge","Newton entered Trinity College, Cambridge, in 1661. He read the works of Galileo, Kepler and Descartes. In 1669, at 26, he became Lucasian Professor of Mathematics, one of the most famous academic posts in the world."],
["The plague years","In 1665–1666, when plague closed Cambridge, Newton returned home. In these years he developed the basics of calculus, experimented with light and began thinking about gravity. He later called it the prime of his age for invention."],
["The apple","Newton later told friends that seeing an apple fall from a tree made him wonder whether the same force reached the Moon. He realized that gravity could explain both falling objects and the orbits of the Moon and planets. The story may be simplified, but Newton himself told it."],
["Light and color","Using prisms, Newton showed that white light is a mixture of colors and that each color bends by a different amount. Because lenses blurred colors, he built a reflecting telescope using a mirror in 1668. Newtonian telescopes are still popular today."],
["The Principia","In 1687 Newton published Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica, the Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy. It set out his three laws of motion and his law of universal gravitation, explaining the motion of planets, comets, tides and falling objects with one set of rules."],
["Calculus dispute","Newton and the German mathematician Gottfried Leibniz both developed calculus independently. A bitter dispute broke out over who was first. Newton, as president of the Royal Society, oversaw an inquiry that favored himself. Today both are credited."],
["Master of the Mint","In 1696 Newton moved to London to run the Royal Mint, which made England’s coins. He reformed the coinage and pursued counterfeiters vigorously, some of whom were executed. He was knighted by Queen Anne in 1705."],
["Other interests","Newton spent much time on alchemy and on studying the Bible and ancient chronology, writing more on these subjects than on physics. He could be secretive and quarrelsome, especially with rivals such as Robert Hooke and Leibniz."],
["His legacy","Newton died in 1727 and was buried in Westminster Abbey. His laws governed physics for more than two centuries until Einstein’s relativity refined them, and they are still used to launch spacecraft. The unit of force, the newton, is named after him."]],
sp:["Standing on the shoulders of giants","In a letter to Robert Hooke in 1676, Newton wrote that if he had seen further, it was by standing on the shoulders of giants. The phrase, used by earlier writers too, expresses how science builds on the work of those who came before. Newton built on Copernicus, Kepler and Galileo. The phrase now appears on the edge of the British £2 coin and is often used to describe how knowledge grows."],
g:[["Gravitation","The force of attraction between masses"],["Calculus","Mathematics of change"],["Prism","A glass shape that splits light into colors"],["Mint","A place where coins are made"]],
f:["Newton was born in the year Galileo died.","He served as a member of Parliament.","The unit of force is named the newton."]},

{id:"lovelace",t:"Ada Lovelace",i:"💻",w:"Ada Lovelace",a:["Ada Lovelace"],born:"10 December 1815, London",died:"27 November 1852, London",nat:"British",known:"Writing what is considered the first computer program",r:["comp","ai","alg"],
l:"Ada Lovelace was an English mathematician who wrote detailed notes on Charles Babbage’s proposed Analytical Engine, a mechanical computer. Her notes included a step-by-step method for calculating numbers with the machine, often called the first computer program, and she foresaw that computers could do much more than arithmetic.",
d:[["1815","Born in London, daughter of Lord Byron"],["1833","Meets Charles Babbage"],["1835","Marries William King, later Earl of Lovelace"],["1843","Publishes her notes on the Analytical Engine"],["1852","Dies aged 36"],["1980","The Ada programming language is named after her"]],
s:[["Daughter of a poet","Augusta Ada Byron was born on 10 December 1815, the only legitimate child of the poet Lord Byron and Annabella Milbanke. Her parents separated weeks after her birth, and she never knew her father, who died when she was eight."],
["A mathematical education","Her mother, who loved mathematics, made sure Ada studied science and mathematics, partly hoping to steer her away from her father’s wild, poetic temperament. Ada was taught by leading tutors, including the mathematician Augustus De Morgan and the scientist Mary Somerville."],
["Imagination","As a girl, Ada was fascinated by machines and dreamed of building a flying machine, studying birds and designing wings. She described her approach as poetical science, combining imagination and mathematics."],
["Meeting Babbage","In 1833, at 17, Ada met Charles Babbage, a mathematician and inventor who had designed the Difference Engine, a calculating machine. She was fascinated, and they became lifelong friends and correspondents."],
["The Analytical Engine","Babbage designed the Analytical Engine, a far more ambitious machine. It would have a store for numbers, a mill for calculations and be programmed with punched cards, like those used in Jacquard looms. It had features of modern computers, but it was never built."],
["The notes","In 1843 Ada translated an article about the Analytical Engine by the Italian engineer Luigi Menabrea and added her own notes, which were three times longer. Note G described how the engine could calculate Bernoulli numbers, considered the first published computer algorithm."],
["Beyond numbers","Ada realized that the engine could work with symbols, not just numbers. She suggested it might one day compose music or produce graphics if these could be expressed in symbols. She also wrote that the engine could not originate ideas on its own, only follow instructions."],
["Later life","Ada married William King in 1835; he became Earl of Lovelace in 1838, and they had three children. She suffered from poor health, gambled on horse races and died of cancer on 27 November 1852, at 36, the same age as her father. She was buried next to him."],
["Rediscovery","Ada’s work was largely forgotten until the 1950s, when her notes were republished as early computers were being built. Historians debate how much of the work was hers and how much Babbage’s, but her insight into the potential of computers is widely recognized."],
["Her legacy","In 1980 the United States Department of Defense named a programming language Ada after her. Ada Lovelace Day, held on the second Tuesday of October, celebrates women in science, technology, engineering and mathematics."]],
sp:["Poetical science","Ada Lovelace believed that imagination was essential to science. She wrote that mathematics was the language through which nature could be understood, and that imagination allowed people to see unseen worlds. Her idea that machines could manipulate symbols to create music and art anticipated by more than a century the computers, digital art and artificial intelligence we use today."],
g:[["Algorithm","A set of step-by-step instructions"],["Program","Instructions for a computer"],["Punched card","A card with holes that stores instructions"],["Analytical Engine","Babbage’s design for a mechanical computer"]],
f:["Ada Lovelace’s father was the poet Lord Byron.","Babbage called her the Enchantress of Numbers.","Her notes were signed only with her initials, A.A.L."]},

{id:"rfranklin",t:"Rosalind Franklin",i:"🧬",w:"Rosalind Franklin",a:["Rosalind Franklin"],born:"25 July 1920, London",died:"16 April 1958, London",nat:"British",known:"X-ray images that revealed the structure of DNA",r:["dna","mendel","microbe"],
l:"Rosalind Franklin was a British chemist whose X-ray images of DNA, especially Photo 51, were crucial to discovering its double-helix structure. She also made important discoveries about coal and viruses. Her role in the DNA discovery was long underrecognized, and she died before the Nobel Prize was awarded for it.",
d:[["1920","Born in London"],["1945","Earns her PhD from Cambridge"],["1951","Joins King’s College London to study DNA"],["1952","Photo 51 is taken in her laboratory"],["1953","Watson and Crick publish the double helix"],["1958","Dies of ovarian cancer at 37"]],
s:[["Early life","Rosalind Elsie Franklin was born on 25 July 1920 in London into a prominent Jewish family. She was a brilliant student who decided at 15 to become a scientist. She studied chemistry at Newnham College, Cambridge."],
["Coal research","During the Second World War, Franklin researched the structure of coal and charcoal, work that helped improve gas masks. She earned her PhD from Cambridge in 1945. Her research on carbon is still cited."],
["Paris","From 1947 to 1950 Franklin worked in Paris, where she mastered X-ray crystallography, a method of firing X-rays at crystals and studying the patterns to reveal how atoms are arranged. She loved her time in France."],
["King’s College","In 1951 Franklin joined King’s College London to study DNA. She worked with student Raymond Gosling. Her relationship with colleague Maurice Wilkins was difficult, partly due to confusion over who was in charge of the DNA work."],
["Photo 51","In May 1952, Gosling, working under Franklin’s direction, took an X-ray image of DNA known as Photo 51. Its X-shaped pattern strongly suggested a helix. Franklin also determined key measurements and that the sugar-phosphate backbone lay on the outside."],
["The double helix","In early 1953, without Franklin’s knowledge, Wilkins showed Photo 51 to James Watson. Francis Crick also saw a report containing her data. Using this information, Watson and Crick built their double-helix model, published in Nature in April 1953 alongside papers by Franklin and Wilkins."],
["Viruses","Franklin moved to Birkbeck College in 1953 and studied the structure of viruses, including the tobacco mosaic virus and the polio virus. Her team made major discoveries about how viruses are built."],
["Early death","Franklin was diagnosed with ovarian cancer in 1956, possibly linked to her work with X-rays, though this is uncertain. She kept working until shortly before her death on 16 April 1958, at 37."],
["The Nobel Prize","In 1962 Watson, Crick and Wilkins received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for the DNA structure. Nobel Prizes are not awarded after death, so Franklin could not be included. Watson’s 1968 book The Double Helix portrayed her unfairly, sparking efforts to recognize her contribution."],
["Her legacy","Today Franklin is celebrated as a pioneer of molecular biology. Universities, research institutes and awards bear her name. The European Space Agency named its Mars rover Rosalind Franklin, designed to search for signs of past life."]],
sp:["X-ray crystallography","X-ray crystallography reveals the arrangement of atoms in a substance. Scientists shine X-rays through a crystal, and the atoms scatter the rays into a pattern of spots. By measuring the pattern, they can work out the structure. It required great skill and patience. Franklin prepared DNA fibers with extraordinary care and controlled their humidity, which allowed her to obtain clearer images than anyone before."],
g:[["Crystallography","The study of crystal structures"],["Double helix","Two strands twisted around each other"],["Molecular biology","The study of life at the level of molecules"],["Photo 51","Franklin’s famous X-ray image of DNA"]],
f:["Franklin’s DNA images were the clearest of their time.","She was a keen hiker and traveler.","A Mars rover is named after her."]},

{id:"hawking",t:"Stephen Hawking",i:"🌌",w:"Stephen Hawking",a:["Stephen Hawking"],born:"8 January 1942, Oxford, England",died:"14 March 2018, Cambridge, England",nat:"British",known:"Research on black holes and A Brief History of Time",r:["bh","bigbang","relat","quantum"],
l:"Stephen Hawking was a British physicist famous for his work on black holes and the origins of the universe. Diagnosed with a motor neurone disease at 21 and given a few years to live, he lived for more than five decades, communicating through a computer voice. His book A Brief History of Time made cosmology popular worldwide.",
d:[["1942","Born in Oxford"],["1963","Diagnosed with motor neurone disease"],["1974","Proposes Hawking radiation"],["1979","Becomes Lucasian Professor at Cambridge"],["1985","Loses his voice; begins using a speech computer"],["1988","Publishes A Brief History of Time"],["2018","Dies in Cambridge"]],
s:[["Early life","Stephen William Hawking was born on 8 January 1942 in Oxford, England, exactly 300 years after the death of Galileo. As a boy he enjoyed taking apart clocks and radios. He studied physics at University College, Oxford."],
["Diagnosis","While a graduate student at Cambridge, Hawking began having trouble with movement. In 1963, at 21, he was diagnosed with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, ALS, a form of motor neurone disease, and doctors expected him to live only about two years."],
["Black holes and the Big Bang","Hawking and mathematician Roger Penrose used general relativity to show that the universe must have begun from a singularity, a point of infinite density, at the Big Bang. Their work in the 1960s and 1970s strengthened the Big Bang theory."],
["Hawking radiation","In 1974 Hawking combined general relativity and quantum theory to show that black holes are not completely black. They should slowly give off radiation, now called Hawking radiation, and could eventually evaporate. This was a major breakthrough in theoretical physics."],
["Lucasian Professor","From 1979 to 2009 Hawking held the Lucasian Professorship of Mathematics at Cambridge, the post once held by Isaac Newton. He continued research and teaching while his body became more paralyzed."],
["A computer voice","In 1985 Hawking lost his voice after an emergency operation. He began using a computer that turned typed words into speech. He later controlled it with a muscle in his cheek. His synthesized voice became instantly recognizable worldwide."],
["A Brief History of Time","Published in 1988, A Brief History of Time explained black holes, the Big Bang and time for general readers. It stayed on the London Sunday Times best-seller list for more than four years and sold more than 25 million copies."],
["Public figure","Hawking appeared on television shows including The Simpsons and Star Trek, spoke about science, the future of humanity, artificial intelligence and disability rights, and experienced weightlessness on a zero-gravity flight in 2007."],
["Family","Hawking married Jane Wilde in 1965, and they had three children. They divorced in 1995. His life story was told in the 2014 film The Theory of Everything, for which Eddie Redmayne won an Oscar for playing him."],
["His legacy","Hawking died on 14 March 2018, Einstein’s birthday, aged 76. His ashes were placed in Westminster Abbey between the graves of Isaac Newton and Charles Darwin. He is remembered as a brilliant scientist and an inspiration to people with disabilities."]],
sp:["A party for time travelers","In 2009 Hawking held a party for time travelers at the University of Cambridge. He sent out invitations only after the party was over, reasoning that only people who could travel back in time would be able to come. No one arrived. He joked that this was evidence that time travel into the past may not be possible. The experiment showed his sense of humor and his talent for making science fun."],
g:[["Cosmology","The study of the universe as a whole"],["Singularity","A point of infinite density"],["Hawking radiation","Radiation given off by black holes"],["Motor neurone disease","An illness that damages nerves controlling muscles"]],
f:["Hawking was born 300 years after Galileo died.","He lived with ALS for 55 years.","His ashes rest between Newton and Darwin in Westminster Abbey."]}
);

;
/* data/people5.js */
// People, part 5: leaders for rights, freedom and peace.
window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"gandhi",t:"Mahatma Gandhi",i:"🕊️",w:"Mahatma Gandhi",a:["Mahatma Gandhi","Gandhi"],born:"2 October 1869, Porbandar, India",died:"30 January 1948, New Delhi, India",nat:"Indian",known:"Leading India’s independence movement through nonviolence",r:["hindu","mandela","mlk"],
l:"Mohandas Karamchand Gandhi, known as Mahatma, meaning great soul, led India’s struggle for independence from British rule using nonviolent resistance. His methods of peaceful protest, fasting and civil disobedience inspired civil rights and freedom movements around the world.",
d:[["1869","Born in Porbandar, India"],["1893","Goes to South Africa as a lawyer"],["1915","Returns to India"],["1930","Leads the Salt March"],["1942","Launches the Quit India movement"],["15 August 1947","India becomes independent"],["30 January 1948","Assassinated in New Delhi"]],
s:[["Early life","Mohandas Karamchand Gandhi was born on 2 October 1869 in Porbandar, in western India, into a Hindu family. He was shy as a child. At 13 he married Kasturba Makhanji in an arranged marriage. In 1888 he went to London to study law."],
["South Africa","In 1893 Gandhi went to South Africa to work as a lawyer. He was thrown off a train for sitting in a first-class carriage reserved for white passengers. Shocked by racial discrimination, he stayed for 21 years, organizing Indians to fight unjust laws."],
["Satyagraha","In South Africa Gandhi developed satyagraha, meaning truth force or soul force: resisting injustice through nonviolent action, accepting punishment rather than using violence. He believed that peaceful resistance could change opponents’ hearts and minds."],
["Return to India","Gandhi returned to India in 1915 and became a leader of the Indian National Congress. He traveled the country, wore simple homespun cloth and encouraged Indians to spin their own cotton rather than buy British cloth. The spinning wheel became a symbol of independence."],
["The Salt March","In 1930 Gandhi protested a British tax on salt. He and his followers walked about 390 kilometers over 24 days to the sea at Dandi, where he made salt from seawater, breaking the law. Tens of thousands were arrested in protests that followed, drawing worldwide attention."],
["Quit India","In 1942, during the Second World War, Gandhi launched the Quit India movement, demanding that Britain leave India immediately. He and other leaders were jailed. His wife Kasturba died in prison in 1944."],
["Independence and partition","India became independent on 15 August 1947, but it was divided into India and Pakistan. Partition led to terrible violence between Hindus, Muslims and Sikhs, and millions were forced from their homes. Gandhi fasted to stop the killing."],
["Assassination","On 30 January 1948, Gandhi was shot dead in New Delhi by Nathuram Godse, a Hindu nationalist who opposed his tolerance toward Muslims. He was 78. India mourned, and Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru said that the light had gone out of our lives."],
["Influence","Gandhi’s methods inspired Martin Luther King Jr. in the United States, Nelson Mandela in South Africa and many others, from campaigners for independence in Africa to the peaceful revolutions of 1986 in the Philippines and 1989 in Eastern Europe. His birthday, 2 October, is the International Day of Non-Violence."],
["A complex figure","Gandhi is admired worldwide, but historians also discuss his early views on race in South Africa, his opinions on caste and his treatment of family. His life shows how people can grow and change, and how even admired leaders have flaws."]],
sp:["The spinning wheel","Gandhi spun cotton thread every day on a small spinning wheel called a charkha. He urged Indians to make their own cloth, called khadi, instead of buying factory-made British cloth, which harmed Indian weavers. Spinning became an act of protest and a way of building self-reliance. A spinning wheel appeared on the flag of the Indian National Congress, and a wheel, the Ashoka Chakra, appears on India’s national flag today."],
g:[["Nonviolence","Opposing wrongs without using violence"],["Satyagraha","Gandhi’s method of truth force"],["Civil disobedience","Peacefully breaking unjust laws"],["Partition","The division of British India into India and Pakistan"]],
f:["Gandhi was nominated for the Nobel Peace Prize five times but never won.","Mahatma means great soul.","His birthday is a national holiday in India."]},

{id:"mandela",t:"Nelson Mandela",i:"✊",w:"Nelson Mandela",a:["Nelson Mandela","Mandela"],born:"18 July 1918, Mvezo, South Africa",died:"5 December 2013, Johannesburg, South Africa",nat:"South African",known:"Ending apartheid and becoming South Africa’s first Black president",r:["gandhi","mlk"],
l:"Nelson Mandela was a South African leader who fought against apartheid, a system of racial segregation, spent 27 years in prison and became the country’s first Black president. Through forgiveness and reconciliation, he helped South Africa avoid civil war and become a democracy for all its people.",
d:[["1918","Born in Mvezo, South Africa"],["1944","Joins the African National Congress"],["1964","Sentenced to life imprisonment"],["11 February 1990","Released from prison"],["1993","Wins the Nobel Peace Prize with F. W. de Klerk"],["1994","Elected president"],["2013","Dies aged 95"]],
s:[["Early life","Rolihlahla Mandela was born on 18 July 1918 in the village of Mvezo in the Eastern Cape, into a Thembu royal family. A teacher gave him the English name Nelson. He studied at Fort Hare University and law at the University of the Witwatersrand."],
["Apartheid","From 1948, South Africa’s government enforced apartheid, meaning separateness. Black, mixed-race and Indian South Africans were denied the vote, forced to live in separate areas, carry passes and use separate schools, hospitals and beaches. Mandela became a leader of the African National Congress, ANC."],
["From peaceful protest to armed struggle","Mandela and the ANC led peaceful campaigns against apartheid. After police killed 69 protesters at Sharpeville in 1960, the ANC was banned. Mandela helped found an armed wing, Umkhonto we Sizwe, which carried out sabotage against government targets."],
["The Rivonia Trial","Mandela was arrested in 1962. At the Rivonia Trial in 1964 he gave a famous speech, saying that he was prepared to die for the ideal of a democratic and free society. He and others were sentenced to life imprisonment."],
["Robben Island","Mandela spent 18 of his 27 prison years on Robben Island, breaking rocks in a lime quarry and living in a tiny cell. He studied, debated and became a symbol of resistance. The Free Mandela campaign spread worldwide."],
["Release","As pressure grew from protests at home and sanctions abroad, President F. W. de Klerk lifted the ban on the ANC and released Mandela on 11 February 1990. Millions watched on television as he walked free after 27 years."],
["Negotiating peace","Mandela and de Klerk negotiated an end to apartheid amid violence that threatened civil war. They shared the Nobel Peace Prize in 1993. In April 1994, South Africa held its first election in which all races could vote, and the ANC won."],
["President","Mandela served as president from 1994 to 1999. He promoted reconciliation between Black and white South Africans and set up the Truth and Reconciliation Commission to investigate apartheid-era crimes. He served only one term, setting an example of peaceful handover."],
["Later life","After leaving office, Mandela campaigned against HIV/AIDS and poverty and founded charities. He became one of the most admired people in the world. He died on 5 December 2013, aged 95."],
["His legacy","Mandela is remembered as a symbol of courage, forgiveness and democracy. The United Nations named his birthday, 18 July, Nelson Mandela International Day, when people are encouraged to spend 67 minutes helping others, one for each year he spent in public service."]],
sp:["The Rugby World Cup","In 1995 South Africa hosted and won the Rugby World Cup. Rugby had long been seen as a white South African sport, and many Black South Africans supported opposing teams. Mandela walked onto the field wearing the green Springbok jersey to present the trophy to the white captain, François Pienaar. The gesture helped unite the country and was retold in the 2009 film Invictus."],
g:[["Apartheid","South Africa’s system of racial segregation"],["Reconciliation","Restoring friendly relations after conflict"],["Sanctions","Penalties imposed by other countries"],["Segregation","Forced separation of people by race"]],
f:["Mandela’s clan name, Madiba, is widely used for him.","He spent 27 years in prison.","Mandela Day is 18 July."]},

{id:"mlk",t:"Martin Luther King Jr.",i:"🗣️",w:"Martin Luther King Jr.",a:["Martin Luther King Jr.","Martin Luther King"],born:"15 January 1929, Atlanta, Georgia, USA",died:"4 April 1968, Memphis, Tennessee, USA",nat:"American",known:"Leading the American civil rights movement",r:["gandhi","mandela","lincoln"],
l:"Martin Luther King Jr. was an American Baptist minister who led the civil rights movement for equal rights for African Americans. Using nonviolent protest, he helped end legal segregation in the United States. His I Have a Dream speech is one of the most famous speeches in history.",
d:[["1929","Born in Atlanta, Georgia"],["1955–1956","Leads the Montgomery bus boycott"],["28 August 1963","Gives the I Have a Dream speech"],["1964","Wins the Nobel Peace Prize; Civil Rights Act passed"],["1965","Selma marches; Voting Rights Act passed"],["4 April 1968","Assassinated in Memphis"]],
s:[["Early life","Martin Luther King Jr. was born on 15 January 1929 in Atlanta, Georgia, the son of a Baptist minister. He grew up under segregation, which separated Black and white Americans in schools, buses and restaurants. He entered college at 15 and earned a doctorate in theology."],
["Segregation in America","In the southern United States, Jim Crow laws enforced racial segregation, and many Black Americans were prevented from voting. Lynching and violence were used to intimidate them. King believed that these injustices could be overcome through nonviolent action."],
["Montgomery bus boycott","In December 1955, Rosa Parks was arrested in Montgomery, Alabama, for refusing to give up her bus seat to a white man. King, a young pastor, led a boycott of the city’s buses that lasted 381 days until the Supreme Court ruled bus segregation unconstitutional."],
["Nonviolence","King was inspired by Gandhi and by Christian teachings to love one’s enemies. He led marches, sit-ins and boycotts, accepting arrest without fighting back. He was jailed about 29 times and faced threats and attacks, including the bombing of his home."],
["Letter from Birmingham Jail","In 1963 King was arrested during protests in Birmingham, Alabama. In jail he wrote a letter explaining why people must disobey unjust laws, writing that injustice anywhere is a threat to justice everywhere. Images of police attacking young protesters shocked the nation."],
["I Have a Dream","On 28 August 1963, before about 250,000 people at the March on Washington, King gave his I Have a Dream speech at the Lincoln Memorial. He dreamed of a nation where his children would be judged not by the color of their skin but by the content of their character."],
["Victories","The Civil Rights Act of 1964 outlawed segregation in public places and discrimination in employment. After marches from Selma to Montgomery in 1965, the Voting Rights Act protected Black Americans’ right to vote. In 1964, at 35, King became one of the youngest Nobel Peace Prize winners."],
["Wider struggles","King later campaigned against poverty and the Vietnam War. In 1968 he planned a Poor People’s Campaign for economic justice. He went to Memphis, Tennessee, to support striking sanitation workers."],
["Assassination","On 4 April 1968, King was shot and killed on the balcony of the Lorraine Motel in Memphis by James Earl Ray. He was 39. Riots broke out in many cities. The motel is now the National Civil Rights Museum."],
["His legacy","Martin Luther King Jr. Day, the third Monday in January, became a US federal holiday in 1986. A memorial to him stands in Washington, D.C. His words and example continue to inspire movements for justice and equality worldwide."]],
sp:["Rosa Parks","Rosa Parks, a 42-year-old seamstress and civil rights activist, refused to give up her bus seat on 1 December 1955. Her arrest sparked the Montgomery bus boycott, which brought Martin Luther King Jr. to national attention. Parks became known as the mother of the civil rights movement. Her quiet act of courage showed how ordinary people can change history. She received the Congressional Gold Medal in 1999."],
g:[["Civil rights","Rights of citizens to equal treatment"],["Boycott","Refusing to buy or use something as a protest"],["Segregation","Separating people by race"],["Sit-in","A protest in which people occupy a place"]],
f:["King entered college at 15.","He was 39 when he died.","His I Have a Dream speech lasted about 17 minutes."]},

{id:"lincoln",t:"Abraham Lincoln",i:"🎩",w:"Abraham Lincoln",a:["Abraham Lincoln","Lincoln"],born:"12 February 1809, Hodgenville, Kentucky, USA",died:"15 April 1865, Washington, D.C., USA",nat:"American",known:"Leading the United States through the Civil War and ending slavery",r:["usrev","mlk"],
l:"Abraham Lincoln was the 16th president of the United States. He led the country through the Civil War, preserved the Union and issued the Emancipation Proclamation, which began the end of slavery. He was assassinated just days after the war ended and is often ranked among the greatest American presidents.",
d:[["1809","Born in a log cabin in Kentucky"],["1834","Elected to the Illinois legislature"],["1860","Elected president"],["1861","Civil War begins"],["1 January 1863","Emancipation Proclamation takes effect"],["19 November 1863","Gettysburg Address"],["14 April 1865","Shot at Ford’s Theatre; dies next morning"]],
s:[["Log cabin","Abraham Lincoln was born on 12 February 1809 in a one-room log cabin in Kentucky. His family moved to Indiana and later Illinois. He had less than a year of formal schooling but taught himself by reading borrowed books by firelight."],
["Lawyer and politician","Lincoln worked as a shopkeeper, postmaster and surveyor before teaching himself law. He became a respected lawyer in Springfield, Illinois, known as Honest Abe. He served in the Illinois legislature and one term in the US Congress."],
["Against slavery","Lincoln believed slavery was morally wrong and opposed its spread to new western territories. In 1858 he debated Stephen Douglas in a famous series of debates. He said that a house divided against itself cannot stand."],
["Election and secession","Lincoln was elected president in November 1860. Southern states, fearing he would end slavery, began to secede and formed the Confederacy. In April 1861 Confederate forces attacked Fort Sumter, starting the Civil War."],
["The Civil War","The Civil War, from 1861 to 1865, was the deadliest war in American history, killing about 620,000 to 750,000 soldiers. Lincoln worked to keep the Union together, chose generals and became an effective war leader, eventually appointing Ulysses S. Grant to command."],
["Emancipation","On 1 January 1863, Lincoln’s Emancipation Proclamation declared that enslaved people in Confederate states were free. It allowed Black men to join the Union army, and about 180,000 served. The 13th Amendment, passed by Congress in 1865, abolished slavery throughout the country."],
["The Gettysburg Address","On 19 November 1863, at the dedication of a cemetery at Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, Lincoln gave a speech of only about 270 words. He spoke of a nation conceived in liberty and of government of the people, by the people, for the people."],
["Victory and assassination","Lincoln was re-elected in 1864. The Confederate army surrendered on 9 April 1865. Five days later, on 14 April, actor John Wilkes Booth shot Lincoln at Ford’s Theatre in Washington. He died the next morning, the first US president to be assassinated."],
["Personality","Lincoln, at 1.93 meters, was the tallest US president. He was known for his humor, storytelling and humility, but also suffered from deep sadness. He wrote his own speeches, which are admired for their beautiful, simple language."],
["His legacy","Lincoln is honored for preserving the United States and ending slavery. The Lincoln Memorial in Washington, D.C., where Martin Luther King Jr. gave his I Have a Dream speech, and the carving on Mount Rushmore honor him. His face appears on the US $5 bill and the penny."]],
sp:["The Gettysburg Address","The Gettysburg Address begins: Four score and seven years ago our fathers brought forth on this continent, a new nation, conceived in Liberty, and dedicated to the proposition that all men are created equal. Four score and seven means 87 years, counting back to 1776. Lincoln spoke for about two minutes, after a two-hour speech by another orator. Few at the time realized it would become one of the most famous speeches in history."],
g:[["Emancipation","Freeing people from slavery"],["Secession","Withdrawal of states from a country"],["Union","The northern states that stayed in the United States"],["Confederacy","The southern states that seceded"]],
f:["Lincoln was born on the same day as Charles Darwin.","He was the tallest US president.","He held a patent for a device to lift boats over shallow water."]},

{id:"malala",t:"Malala Yousafzai",i:"📚",w:"Malala Yousafzai",a:["Malala Yousafzai","Malala"],born:"12 July 1997, Mingora, Swat Valley, Pakistan",died:"",nat:"Pakistani",known:"Campaigning for girls’ education; youngest Nobel laureate",r:["islam","gandhi"],
l:"Malala Yousafzai is a Pakistani activist for girls’ education who survived being shot by the Taliban at age 15. She went on to become the youngest person ever to win a Nobel Prize, at 17, and founded the Malala Fund to help girls around the world go to school.",
d:[["1997","Born in Mingora, Pakistan"],["2009","Writes a blog for the BBC about life under the Taliban"],["9 October 2012","Shot by a Taliban gunman"],["2013","Speaks at the United Nations"],["2014","Wins the Nobel Peace Prize at 17"],["2020","Graduates from the University of Oxford"]],
s:[["Early life","Malala Yousafzai was born on 12 July 1997 in Mingora, in Pakistan’s Swat Valley. Her father, Ziauddin, ran a girls’ school and encouraged her to learn and speak her mind. She loved school and dreamed of becoming a doctor."],
["The Taliban in Swat","From about 2007, the Pakistani Taliban, an armed extremist group, took control of parts of the Swat Valley. They banned television and music and destroyed hundreds of schools, and in 2009 ordered girls to stop attending school."],
["A voice for girls","At 11, Malala began writing a blog for BBC Urdu under a pen name, describing life under the Taliban and her fear of losing her education. She gave interviews and spoke publicly for girls’ right to learn, becoming known in Pakistan."],
["The attack","On 9 October 2012, a Taliban gunman stopped Malala’s school bus, asked which girl was Malala, and shot her in the head. Two of her friends were also wounded. The attack shocked the world."],
["Recovery","Malala was flown to Birmingham, England, for specialist treatment. She survived after surgery and months of recovery. Her family moved to Birmingham, where she continued her schooling."],
["At the United Nations","On her 16th birthday, 12 July 2013, Malala spoke at the United Nations in New York. She said that one child, one teacher, one book and one pen can change the world. The UN named the day Malala Day."],
["Nobel Peace Prize","In 2014 Malala shared the Nobel Peace Prize with Kailash Satyarthi, an Indian campaigner against child labor. At 17, she became the youngest Nobel laureate in history. She gave her prize money to building a school for girls."],
["The Malala Fund","Malala and her father founded the Malala Fund in 2013 to support girls’ education. It works in countries including Pakistan, Afghanistan, Nigeria and Brazil, helping local activists and pushing governments to provide 12 years of free, safe, quality education."],
["Books and studies","Her memoir, I Am Malala, published in 2013, became an international best seller. She studied philosophy, politics and economics at the University of Oxford, graduating in 2020. She has also produced films and continues to speak worldwide."],
["Her legacy","Malala has become a global symbol of the right to education. According to UNESCO, about 120 million girls are out of school worldwide. Her campaign reminds people that education is a basic human right."]],
sp:["One book, one pen","In her 2013 United Nations speech, Malala said that the Taliban thought bullets would silence her, but they had failed. She told world leaders: Let us pick up our books and pens. They are our most powerful weapons. One child, one teacher, one book and one pen can change the world. Education is the only solution. Her words are now quoted in classrooms around the world."],
g:[["Activist","A person who campaigns for change"],["Extremist","A person with extreme views who may use violence"],["Laureate","A winner of a major prize"],["Memoir","A book about the author’s own life"]],
f:["Malala was 17 when she won the Nobel Peace Prize.","She first wrote under the pen name Gul Makai.","12 July is Malala Day."]},

{id:"teresa",t:"Mother Teresa",i:"🙏",w:"Mother Teresa",a:["Mother Teresa"],born:"26 August 1910, Skopje (now North Macedonia)",died:"5 September 1997, Kolkata, India",nat:"Albanian-born; Indian citizen",known:"Caring for the poorest of the poor in Kolkata",r:["christ","magsaysay"],
l:"Mother Teresa was a Catholic nun who founded the Missionaries of Charity in Kolkata, India, to care for the sick, dying and poorest people. She won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1979 and was declared a saint in 2016. Her work has also been the subject of criticism.",
d:[["1910","Born in Skopje"],["1928","Joins the Sisters of Loreto"],["1929","Arrives in India"],["1950","Founds the Missionaries of Charity"],["1962","Receives the Ramon Magsaysay Award"],["1979","Wins the Nobel Peace Prize"],["2016","Declared a saint"]],
s:[["Early life","Anjezë Gonxhe Bojaxhiu was born on 26 August 1910 in Skopje, then in the Ottoman Empire, into an Albanian Catholic family. Her father died when she was eight. As a girl she was inspired by stories of missionaries working in India."],
["Becoming a nun","At 18 she joined the Sisters of Loreto in Ireland and took the name Teresa after Saint Thérèse of Lisieux. In 1929 she arrived in India, and for nearly 20 years she taught at a girls’ school in Calcutta, now Kolkata."],
["A call within a call","In 1946, on a train journey, she felt called to leave the convent and serve the poorest people in the slums. In 1948 she began working in the streets of Calcutta, wearing a simple white sari with blue borders."],
["Missionaries of Charity","In 1950 she founded the Missionaries of Charity. Its sisters take vows to give wholehearted, free service to the poorest of the poor. The order opened homes for the dying, orphanages, leprosy clinics and shelters."],
["Home for the dying","In 1952 she opened Nirmal Hriday, a home where dying people found on the streets could be cared for and die with dignity. She said that being unwanted, unloved and uncared for was the greatest poverty."],
["Growth worldwide","The Missionaries of Charity spread to more than 100 countries, running homes for the poor, sick and dying from Kolkata to New York. By her death, the order had about 4,000 sisters."],
["Honors","Mother Teresa received the Ramon Magsaysay Award in 1962, one of her first international honors, and the Nobel Peace Prize in 1979. She asked that the usual banquet be canceled and the money given to the poor."],
["Criticism","Some critics, including doctors and journalists, said that her homes lacked proper medical care and pain relief, and they questioned how donations were used. Others criticized her strong opposition to abortion and contraception. Supporters point to her lifelong dedication to people no one else cared for."],
["Sainthood","Mother Teresa died on 5 September 1997 in Kolkata. In 2003 she was beatified, and on 4 September 2016, Pope Francis canonized her as Saint Teresa of Calcutta. Her feast day is 5 September."],
["Her legacy","Mother Teresa became a worldwide symbol of compassion and service. The International Day of Charity is held on 5 September, the anniversary of her death. Her life raises questions about how best to help people in poverty."]],
sp:["Small things with great love","Mother Teresa often said that we cannot all do great things, but we can do small things with great love. She believed that every person deserved respect and care, no matter how poor or sick. Her sisters bathed, fed and comforted people who had been abandoned. For many, her message was that anyone, anywhere, can help someone in need, beginning with their own family and neighbors."],
g:[["Nun","A woman in a religious community"],["Missionary","A person sent to spread a religion or help others"],["Canonization","Declaring someone a saint"],["Leprosy","A disease affecting the skin and nerves, now curable"]],
f:["Mother Teresa received the Ramon Magsaysay Award in 1962.","Her sari became a registered trademark of her order.","The International Day of Charity is held on the anniversary of her death."]}
);

;
/* data/people6.js */
// People, part 6: rulers and explorers.
window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"cleopatra",t:"Cleopatra",i:"👑",w:"Cleopatra",a:["Cleopatra"],born:"69 BCE, Alexandria, Egypt",died:"August 30 BCE, Alexandria, Egypt",nat:"Egyptian (of Macedonian Greek descent)",known:"Being the last active ruler of ancient Egypt’s Ptolemaic Kingdom",r:["egypt","caesar","rome"],
l:"Cleopatra VII was the last active ruler of the Ptolemaic Kingdom of Egypt. Intelligent, multilingual and politically skilled, she allied herself with the Roman leaders Julius Caesar and Mark Antony to protect Egypt’s independence. Her death in 30 BCE marked the end of ancient Egypt as an independent kingdom, as it became a Roman province.",
d:[["69 BCE","Born in Alexandria"],["51 BCE","Becomes queen at about 18"],["48 BCE","Meets Julius Caesar"],["41 BCE","Allies with Mark Antony"],["31 BCE","Defeated at the Battle of Actium"],["30 BCE","Dies; Egypt becomes a Roman province"]],
s:[["The Ptolemies","After Alexander the Great died in 323 BCE, one of his generals, Ptolemy, took control of Egypt. His descendants, the Ptolemies, ruled for nearly 300 years from Alexandria. They were Macedonian Greeks who adopted the titles and customs of the pharaohs."],
["Young queen","Cleopatra was born in 69 BCE. On her father’s death in 51 BCE, she became queen at about 18, ruling jointly with her younger brother Ptolemy XIII, whom she was expected to marry, as was Ptolemaic custom. They soon quarreled, and she was driven from Alexandria."],
["Julius Caesar","In 48 BCE the Roman general Julius Caesar came to Alexandria. According to the ancient writer Plutarch, Cleopatra had herself smuggled into his palace, hidden in a bedding sack. Caesar backed her claim to the throne, and they had a son, Ptolemy Caesar, called Caesarion."],
["A skilled ruler","Cleopatra was highly educated and reportedly spoke about nine languages. She was the first Ptolemaic ruler to learn Egyptian. She managed Egypt’s economy, grain supplies and alliances during a time of famine and civil war."],
["Mark Antony","After Caesar’s assassination in 44 BCE, Rome was divided. In 41 BCE Cleopatra met the Roman general Mark Antony in Tarsus, arriving on a magnificent golden barge. They became allies and partners and had three children."],
["Actium","Antony’s rival, Octavian, Caesar’s adopted heir, declared war on Cleopatra. In 31 BCE, at the naval Battle of Actium off Greece, Octavian’s fleet defeated Antony and Cleopatra. They retreated to Alexandria."],
["Death","In 30 BCE, as Octavian’s forces closed in, Antony took his own life. Cleopatra died soon after, at about 39. Ancient writers said she was bitten by an asp, a venomous snake, but they were not certain how she died. Egypt became a Roman province."],
["Her appearance","Roman writers portrayed Cleopatra as a seductive enchantress, but they wrote as her enemies. Coins show her with a strong nose and chin. Plutarch wrote that her charm lay less in her beauty than in her conversation, intelligence and voice."],
["In art and story","Cleopatra’s story has inspired plays, such as Shakespeare’s Antony and Cleopatra, paintings, operas and films, including the 1963 film starring Elizabeth Taylor. Debates about her appearance and ancestry continue."],
["Her legacy","Cleopatra is remembered as one of the most famous women of the ancient world. Historians now see her as a capable ruler who used diplomacy, wealth and alliances to keep Egypt independent for as long as possible against the growing power of Rome."]],
sp:["Alexandria","Alexandria, founded by Alexander the Great in 331 BCE, was Cleopatra’s capital and one of the greatest cities of the ancient world. It was home to the Library of Alexandria, which held hundreds of thousands of scrolls, and the Lighthouse of Alexandria, one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World. Scholars from across the Mediterranean came there to study mathematics, astronomy and medicine. Parts of Cleopatra’s palace now lie underwater in the harbor."],
g:[["Ptolemaic","Relating to the Greek dynasty that ruled Egypt"],["Province","A territory governed as part of an empire"],["Asp","A venomous snake"],["Dynasty","A series of rulers from one family"]],
f:["Cleopatra reportedly spoke about nine languages.","She was the seventh queen named Cleopatra.","The Great Pyramid was already about 2,500 years old when she was born."]},

{id:"alexander",t:"Alexander the Great",i:"🏇",w:"Alexander the Great",a:["Alexander the Great"],born:"356 BCE, Pella, Macedonia",died:"June 323 BCE, Babylon",nat:"Macedonian Greek",known:"Conquering an empire from Greece to India",r:["greece","socr","cleopatra"],
l:"Alexander the Great was a king of Macedonia who, in just over a decade, conquered an empire stretching from Greece and Egypt to the borders of India. Never defeated in battle, he spread Greek language and culture across a vast area, beginning the Hellenistic age. He died at 32.",
d:[["356 BCE","Born in Pella, Macedonia"],["343 BCE","Begins studying with Aristotle"],["336 BCE","Becomes king at 20"],["331 BCE","Founds Alexandria; wins at Gaugamela"],["326 BCE","Battle of the Hydaspes in India"],["323 BCE","Dies in Babylon"]],
s:[["Prince of Macedonia","Alexander was born in 356 BCE in Pella, the capital of Macedonia, north of Greece, to King Philip II and Queen Olympias. His father built Macedonia into the strongest power in Greece."],
["Aristotle’s pupil","From about age 13, Alexander was taught by the philosopher Aristotle, who gave him a love of philosophy, science, medicine and Homer’s Iliad. Alexander reportedly slept with a copy of the Iliad under his pillow."],
["Bucephalus","According to legend, as a boy Alexander tamed a wild horse named Bucephalus that no one else could ride, noticing that it was frightened of its own shadow. Bucephalus carried him into many battles."],
["Becoming king","In 336 BCE Philip was assassinated, and Alexander became king at 20. He quickly crushed revolts in Greece, destroying the city of Thebes as a warning. Then he set out to conquer the vast Persian Empire."],
["Conquering Persia","Alexander defeated the Persians at the Granicus River in 334 BCE, at Issus in 333 and decisively at Gaugamela in 331. The Persian king Darius III fled and was later killed by his own nobles. Alexander became ruler of the Persian Empire."],
["Egypt","In 332 BCE Alexander entered Egypt, where he was welcomed as a liberator from the Persians and declared a son of the god Amun. He founded the city of Alexandria, which became a great center of learning and trade."],
["To India","Alexander marched east into Central Asia and across the Hindu Kush. In 326 BCE he defeated King Porus at the Hydaspes River in what is now Pakistan. His exhausted army refused to go farther, and he turned back."],
["Ruling an empire","Alexander founded about 20 cities, many named Alexandria. He encouraged the mixing of Greek and Persian cultures, adopting Persian dress and customs and marrying Roxana, a Bactrian princess. Some of his Macedonian followers resented these changes."],
["Death","In June 323 BCE, in Babylon, Alexander fell ill with a fever and died at 32. The cause is unknown; theories include malaria, typhoid and poisoning. With no clear heir, his generals divided the empire among themselves."],
["His legacy","Alexander’s conquests spread Greek language, art and ideas from Egypt to Afghanistan, creating the Hellenistic world. He is studied as one of history’s greatest military leaders, though his campaigns also brought great destruction and loss of life."]],
sp:["The Gordian knot","In the city of Gordium, in what is now Turkey, there was a famous knot tied so intricately that no one could untie it. A prophecy said that whoever untied it would rule Asia. According to legend, Alexander drew his sword and sliced through the knot in 333 BCE. Today, cutting the Gordian knot means solving a difficult problem with a bold, simple action."],
g:[["Hellenistic","The era of Greek culture after Alexander"],["Empire","Many lands ruled by one power"],["Phalanx","A tight formation of soldiers with long spears"],["Heir","A person who inherits a title or throne"]],
f:["Alexander was never defeated in battle.","He named a city after his horse, Bucephalus.","He died at 32."]},

{id:"caesar",t:"Julius Caesar",i:"🏛️",w:"Julius Caesar",a:["Julius Caesar","Caesar"],born:"July 100 BCE, Rome",died:"15 March 44 BCE, Rome",nat:"Roman",known:"Conquering Gaul and becoming dictator of Rome",r:["rome","cleopatra","gmyth"],
l:"Gaius Julius Caesar was a Roman general and politician who conquered Gaul, crossed the Rubicon to start a civil war and became dictator of Rome. His assassination on the Ides of March in 44 BCE led to the end of the Roman Republic and the rise of the Roman Empire under his heir, Augustus.",
d:[["100 BCE","Born in Rome"],["59 BCE","Elected consul"],["58–50 BCE","Conquers Gaul"],["49 BCE","Crosses the Rubicon"],["46 BCE","Introduces the Julian calendar"],["15 March 44 BCE","Assassinated in the Senate"]],
s:[["Early life","Gaius Julius Caesar was born in July 100 BCE into an old but not wealthy noble family in Rome. As a young man, he was captured by pirates, who demanded a ransom. He joked that he would crucify them; after his release, he did."],
["Rising politician","Caesar was a gifted speaker and spent lavishly to win popularity. In 60 BCE he formed an alliance with the general Pompey and the rich Crassus, known as the First Triumvirate. In 59 BCE he became consul, one of Rome’s two highest officials."],
["Conquering Gaul","From 58 to 50 BCE Caesar conquered Gaul, roughly modern France and Belgium, in the Gallic Wars. He also crossed into Britain twice. His book about the wars, the Commentaries, is still read in Latin classes. The wars brought him wealth, fame and a loyal army."],
["Crossing the Rubicon","The Senate, led by Pompey, ordered Caesar to disband his army. In January 49 BCE he crossed the Rubicon River into Italy with his troops, breaking the law. He reportedly said, The die is cast. This started a civil war, which he won."],
["Dictator","Caesar defeated Pompey and his supporters and became dictator, at first for limited terms and then, in 44 BCE, for life. He reformed laws, gave land to veterans, expanded the Senate and planned great building projects."],
["The Julian calendar","In 46 BCE Caesar introduced a new calendar of 365 days with a leap day every four years, based on advice from the astronomer Sosigenes of Alexandria. The Julian calendar was used in Europe for over 1,600 years, and its basic design is still used today."],
["Cleopatra","In Egypt, Caesar supported Cleopatra in a civil war against her brother, and they had a son, Caesarion. Cleopatra later visited Rome, which some Romans disliked."],
["Ides of March","Many senators feared that Caesar wanted to become king. On the Ides of March, 15 March 44 BCE, a group of about 60 conspirators, including Brutus and Cassius, stabbed him 23 times at a meeting of the Senate. He died at the foot of a statue of Pompey."],
["After Caesar","Caesar’s death led to more civil wars. His adopted heir, Octavian, defeated the assassins and later Mark Antony and Cleopatra. In 27 BCE Octavian became Augustus, the first Roman emperor, ending the Republic."],
["His legacy","The month of July is named after Caesar. His name became a title for rulers: the German Kaiser and Russian Tsar come from Caesar. Shakespeare’s play Julius Caesar made the phrase Et tu, Brute? famous, though Caesar’s real last words are unknown."]],
sp:["Veni, vidi, vici","After winning a quick victory at the Battle of Zela in 47 BCE, in what is now Turkey, Caesar sent a famous message to Rome: Veni, vidi, vici, meaning I came, I saw, I conquered. The three short words, which sound alike in Latin, showed off how fast and complete his victory had been. The phrase is still used today to describe a swift success."],
g:[["Republic","A state governed by elected officials"],["Dictator","A ruler with absolute power"],["Consul","One of two chief officials of Rome"],["Triumvirate","Rule by three people"]],
f:["July is named after Julius Caesar.","Kaiser and Tsar both come from Caesar.","Caesar was stabbed 23 times."]},

{id:"napoleon",t:"Napoleon Bonaparte",i:"🎖️",w:"Napoleon",a:["Napoleon Bonaparte","Napoleon"],born:"15 August 1769, Ajaccio, Corsica",died:"5 May 1821, Saint Helena",nat:"French",known:"Becoming emperor of France and conquering much of Europe",r:["frrev"],
l:"Napoleon Bonaparte was a French military leader who rose to power after the French Revolution and crowned himself emperor in 1804. He conquered much of Europe before his defeat at Waterloo in 1815. His legal code, the Napoleonic Code, still shapes laws in many countries.",
d:[["1769","Born in Ajaccio, Corsica"],["1799","Seizes power in France"],["1804","Crowns himself emperor; Napoleonic Code issued"],["1805","Victory at Austerlitz"],["1812","Disastrous invasion of Russia"],["1815","Defeated at Waterloo"],["1821","Dies on Saint Helena"]],
s:[["Corsican boyhood","Napoleone Buonaparte was born on 15 August 1769 in Ajaccio, Corsica, an island that had just become French. His family were minor nobles. He attended military schools in France, where he was teased for his Corsican accent, and became an artillery officer."],
["Revolutionary general","During the French Revolution, Napoleon rose quickly. In 1793 he helped recapture the port of Toulon. In 1796–1797 he led a brilliant campaign in Italy, defeating Austrian armies and becoming a national hero."],
["Egypt","In 1798 Napoleon invaded Egypt to threaten British trade with India. He took scholars with him, and his soldiers found the Rosetta Stone, which later helped decode hieroglyphs. But the British navy destroyed his fleet at the Battle of the Nile."],
["Seizing power","In November 1799 Napoleon took part in a coup and became First Consul, effectively ruling France. In 1804 he crowned himself emperor in Notre-Dame Cathedral in Paris, taking the crown from the pope’s hands and placing it on his own head."],
["The Napoleonic Code","The Napoleonic Code of 1804 set out clear laws on property, family and contracts, ending privileges based on birth and guaranteeing equality before the law for men. It limited women’s rights, however. It influenced legal systems in Europe, Latin America and beyond."],
["Master of Europe","Napoleon won great victories, including Austerlitz in 1805, against Austria and Russia. By 1810 he controlled or dominated most of Europe, placing family members on thrones. He reorganized countries and spread ideas from the Revolution."],
["Russia","In 1812 Napoleon invaded Russia with about 600,000 soldiers. The Russians retreated and burned Moscow. Forced to withdraw in the bitter winter, his army was destroyed by cold, hunger and attacks; only a small fraction returned."],
["Exile and return","Defeated by an alliance of European powers, Napoleon was exiled to the island of Elba in 1814. He escaped in 1815 and returned to power for about 100 days. On 18 June 1815, he was defeated at Waterloo, in Belgium, by British and Prussian armies."],
["Saint Helena","Napoleon was exiled to Saint Helena, a remote island in the South Atlantic, where he died on 5 May 1821. In 1840 his remains were returned to Paris and placed in Les Invalides."],
["Short or not?","Napoleon is often thought to have been very short, but he was about 1.69 meters, average for his time. The myth came partly from confusion between French and British inches and from British cartoons mocking him."]],
sp:["The Rosetta Stone","In 1799, during Napoleon’s campaign in Egypt, French soldiers found a stone slab near the town of Rashid, or Rosetta. It carried the same decree written in three scripts: hieroglyphs, demotic Egyptian and ancient Greek. Because scholars could read the Greek, they could use it to decode hieroglyphs. The British took the stone after defeating the French, and it is now in the British Museum. Jean-François Champollion deciphered hieroglyphs in 1822."],
g:[["Emperor","The ruler of an empire"],["Coup","A sudden seizure of power"],["Code","A collection of laws"],["Exile","Being sent away from one’s country"]],
f:["Napoleon was about average height for his time.","The phrase meeting one’s Waterloo means a final defeat.","He was born the year after Corsica became French."]},

{id:"marcopolo",t:"Marco Polo",i:"🐫",w:"Marco Polo",a:["Marco Polo"],born:"c. 1254, Venice",died:"8 January 1324, Venice",nat:"Venetian (Italian)",known:"Traveling to China and describing it to Europeans",r:["silk","china","mongol","explor"],
l:"Marco Polo was a Venetian merchant who traveled across Asia to the court of the Mongol emperor Kublai Khan in China, where he stayed for about 17 years. His book describing his travels gave Europeans one of their first detailed pictures of Asia and inspired later explorers, including Christopher Columbus.",
d:[["c. 1254","Born in Venice"],["1271","Sets out for Asia with his father and uncle"],["1275","Arrives at Kublai Khan’s court"],["1292","Leaves China by sea"],["1295","Returns to Venice"],["c. 1298","Dictates his book in prison in Genoa"],["1324","Dies in Venice"]],
s:[["A merchant family","Marco Polo was born around 1254 in Venice, a wealthy trading city. His father, Niccolò, and uncle, Maffeo, were merchants who had already traveled to China and met Kublai Khan, returning when Marco was about 15."],
["The journey east","In 1271, at about 17, Marco set out with his father and uncle. They traveled through the Middle East, Persia and Central Asia along routes of the Silk Road, crossing deserts and mountains. The journey took about three and a half years."],
["Kublai Khan","Around 1275 the Polos reached Shangdu, Kublai Khan’s summer palace, known in the West as Xanadu. Kublai, grandson of Genghis Khan, ruled the Mongol Yuan dynasty in China. According to Marco, the emperor took a liking to him."],
["Serving the emperor","Marco said he served Kublai Khan as an official and messenger, traveling across China and possibly to Southeast Asia. He described huge cities, the imperial postal system, paper money, coal used as fuel and the Grand Canal."],
["Return by sea","In 1292 the Polos left China escorting a Mongol princess to marry a ruler in Persia. They sailed via Sumatra, Sri Lanka and India. They finally reached Venice in 1295, after 24 years away."],
["Prison and the book","Around 1298 Marco was captured in a war between Venice and Genoa. In a Genoese prison he told his stories to Rustichello da Pisa, a writer of romances, who wrote them down. The book became known as The Travels of Marco Polo or Il Milione."],
["Wonders of the East","The book described the wealth of China, spices of the Indies, the customs of many peoples and lands Marco had heard about, including Japan, which he called Cipangu. Many Europeans found the stories hard to believe."],
["Doubts","Some historians have questioned whether Marco really went to China, because his book does not mention the Great Wall, tea or chopsticks. Most scholars, however, believe he did, as his accurate details about Mongol China are hard to explain otherwise."],
["Later life","Marco returned to Venice, married and had three daughters. He lived as a wealthy merchant. On his deathbed in 1324, friends are said to have asked him to admit his stories were exaggerated. He reportedly replied that he had not told half of what he saw."],
["His legacy","Marco Polo’s book inspired European interest in Asia. Christopher Columbus owned a copy, with notes in the margins, and hoped to reach the lands it described. Venice’s airport is named Marco Polo, and a sheep species, the Marco Polo sheep, bears his name."]],
sp:["Paper money","Marco Polo was amazed that the Mongol emperor’s subjects used paper money. He described how the bark of mulberry trees was made into paper, cut into pieces, stamped with the emperor’s seal and accepted as payment throughout the empire. Anyone who refused it could be put to death. In Europe, where people used gold and silver coins, the idea seemed almost magical. Paper money would not become common in Europe for centuries."],
g:[["Merchant","A person who buys and sells goods"],["Silk Road","Trade routes linking China and the West"],["Khan","A title for Mongol rulers"],["Dynasty","A series of rulers from one family"]],
f:["Marco Polo was away from Venice for 24 years.","Columbus owned a copy of his book.","Xanadu was Kublai Khan’s summer capital."]},

{id:"magellan",t:"Ferdinand Magellan",i:"⛵",w:"Ferdinand Magellan",a:["Ferdinand Magellan","Magellan"],born:"c. 1480, Portugal",died:"27 April 1521, Mactan, Philippines",nat:"Portuguese",known:"Leading the first expedition to sail around the world",r:["explor","lapulapu","cebu","spice"],
l:"Ferdinand Magellan was a Portuguese explorer who led a Spanish expedition that became the first to sail around the world. He found a passage around South America and crossed the Pacific, reaching the Philippines in 1521, where he was killed in the Battle of Mactan by the forces of Lapulapu. One of his ships completed the voyage in 1522.",
d:[["c. 1480","Born in Portugal"],["1519","Sails from Spain with five ships"],["1520","Passes through the Strait of Magellan"],["16 March 1521","Sights the Philippines"],["31 March 1521","First Mass on Philippine soil"],["27 April 1521","Killed at Mactan"],["1522","The Victoria returns to Spain"]],
s:[["Early life","Fernão de Magalhães, known in English as Ferdinand Magellan, was born around 1480 in northern Portugal into a family of minor nobility. As a boy he served as a page at the Portuguese royal court, where he learned about navigation."],
["Serving Portugal","From 1505 Magellan sailed with Portuguese fleets to India and Southeast Asia, fighting in battles and taking part in the capture of Malacca in 1511. He gained experience of the spice trade and the seas of Asia."],
["Changing sides","After a quarrel with the Portuguese king, Magellan offered his services to Spain. He proposed reaching the Spice Islands by sailing west, around South America. King Charles I of Spain agreed to fund the expedition."],
["The voyage begins","On 20 September 1519, Magellan sailed from Spain with five ships and about 270 men. The fleet crossed the Atlantic to South America, where they faced storms, a mutiny and the loss of one ship."],
["The strait","In October and November 1520, Magellan led his remaining ships through a dangerous passage at the southern tip of South America, now called the Strait of Magellan. One ship deserted and returned to Spain. They emerged into a calm ocean, which Magellan named the Pacific, meaning peaceful."],
["Crossing the Pacific","The crossing of the Pacific took more than three months. The sailors ran out of fresh food and ate rats, sawdust and leather. Many died of scurvy and hunger. In March 1521 they reached Guam and then the Philippines."],
["Around the world","On 16 March 1521 the fleet sighted Samar and landed on Homonhon. On 31 March, Easter Sunday, the first Christian Mass in the Philippines was held at Limasawa. In Cebu, Rajah Humabon and many of his people were baptized."],
["Battle of Mactan","Magellan demanded that Lapulapu, a chief of Mactan, submit to Spain. Lapulapu refused. On 27 April 1521, Magellan led about 50 men against Lapulapu’s much larger force. Wading through shallow water, the Spanish were defeated, and Magellan was killed."],
["Completing the voyage","The survivors burned one ship, reached the Moluccas and loaded cloves. Juan Sebastián Elcano led the Victoria across the Indian Ocean and around Africa, reaching Spain on 6 September 1522 with 18 of the original crew. They were the first people known to sail around the world."],
["His legacy","Magellan’s expedition proved that Earth could be circled by sea and showed the vast size of the Pacific. For Filipinos, it also marks the beginning of Spanish contact. In 2021 the Philippines commemorated the 500th anniversary, honoring Lapulapu’s victory as well as the voyage."]],
sp:["Pigafetta’s journal","Much of what we know about Magellan’s voyage comes from Antonio Pigafetta, an Italian scholar who sailed with the expedition and was one of the 18 survivors who returned on the Victoria. His journal described the people, food, languages and customs of the places they visited, including the earliest written account of the Visayas. He recorded words of the Cebuano language and described the Battle of Mactan, in which he was wounded."],
g:[["Circumnavigation","Sailing all the way around the world"],["Strait","A narrow sea passage between two landmasses"],["Mutiny","A rebellion by sailors against their captain"],["Scurvy","A disease caused by lack of vitamin C"]],
f:["Only one of Magellan’s five ships completed the voyage.","Magellan named the Pacific Ocean.","Pigafetta recorded the first list of Cebuano words."]}
);

;
/* data/people7.js */
// People, part 7: artists, pioneers and adventurers.
window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"beethoven",t:"Ludwig van Beethoven",i:"🎹",w:"Ludwig van Beethoven",a:["Ludwig van Beethoven","Beethoven"],born:"December 1770 (baptized 17 December), Bonn",died:"26 March 1827, Vienna",nat:"German",known:"Composing nine symphonies, many while deaf",r:["musicw","mozart","opera"],
l:"Ludwig van Beethoven was a German composer whose music bridged the Classical and Romantic eras. He wrote nine symphonies, 32 piano sonatas, an opera and many other works. Remarkably, he composed some of his greatest music, including the Ninth Symphony, after becoming almost completely deaf.",
d:[["1770","Born in Bonn"],["1792","Moves to Vienna; studies with Haydn"],["1802","Writes the Heiligenstadt Testament"],["1804","Completes the Eroica Symphony"],["1808","Fifth Symphony premieres"],["1824","Ninth Symphony premieres"],["1827","Dies in Vienna"]],
s:[["A hard childhood","Ludwig van Beethoven was born in Bonn, Germany, and baptized on 17 December 1770. His father, a court singer, pushed him hard to become a child prodigy like Mozart, sometimes beating him and waking him at night to practice."],
["Young musician","By 12, Beethoven was playing the organ at court and had published his first compositions. He supported his family after his mother died and his father’s drinking worsened. In 1787 he visited Vienna, where he may have met Mozart."],
["Vienna","In 1792 Beethoven moved to Vienna, the music capital of Europe, and studied briefly with Joseph Haydn. He became famous as a brilliant pianist, especially for improvising. Wealthy nobles supported him, allowing him to compose freely."],
["Losing his hearing","In his late twenties, Beethoven began to lose his hearing. By his late forties he was almost completely deaf. He used ear trumpets, and visitors wrote in conversation books so he could read their words."],
["The Heiligenstadt Testament","In 1802, in despair, Beethoven wrote a letter to his brothers, now called the Heiligenstadt Testament. He described thoughts of ending his life but decided to live for his art. He wrote that he could not leave the world until he had brought forth all that he felt within him."],
["The heroic period","Beethoven’s Third Symphony, the Eroica, completed in 1804, was longer and more dramatic than any symphony before. He originally dedicated it to Napoleon but angrily scratched out the dedication when Napoleon crowned himself emperor. The Fifth Symphony begins with a famous four-note motif."],
["Piano music","Beethoven’s 32 piano sonatas include the Moonlight and Pathétique sonatas. He also wrote five piano concertos and the short piece Für Elise, one of the most played piano pieces in the world. His only opera, Fidelio, celebrates freedom and love."],
["The Ninth Symphony","Beethoven’s Ninth Symphony, first performed in 1824, was the first major symphony to include singers. Its final movement sets Friedrich Schiller’s poem Ode to Joy. At the premiere, Beethoven, unable to hear the applause, had to be turned around to see the cheering audience."],
["Later works","In his final years, Beethoven wrote his late string quartets and piano sonatas, music so original that some listeners found it strange. Today they are considered among the deepest works of Western music. He died in Vienna on 26 March 1827; about 10,000 people attended his funeral."],
["His legacy","Beethoven expanded what music could express, influencing composers for centuries. The Ode to Joy melody is the anthem of the European Union. His life, overcoming deafness to create great art, inspires people facing difficulties of all kinds."]],
sp:["Composing without hearing","How could Beethoven compose while deaf? He had trained his inner ear for decades, so he could imagine sounds precisely when reading or writing music, as skilled musicians can. He worked out ideas in sketchbooks, sometimes for years. He also felt vibrations, reportedly using a rod clenched in his teeth and touching the piano. His deafness may even have pushed him toward more inward, original music."],
g:[["Symphony","A long work for orchestra"],["Sonata","A piece for one or two instruments"],["Motif","A short, repeated musical idea"],["Romantic era","A period of expressive, emotional art in the 1800s"]],
f:["The Ode to Joy melody is the European Union’s anthem.","Beethoven wrote nine symphonies.","He never married."]},

{id:"kahlo",t:"Frida Kahlo",i:"🌺",w:"Frida Kahlo",a:["Frida Kahlo"],born:"6 July 1907, Coyoacán, Mexico City",died:"13 July 1954, Coyoacán, Mexico City",nat:"Mexican",known:"Self-portraits exploring pain, identity and Mexican culture",r:["picasso","impress"],
l:"Frida Kahlo was a Mexican painter famous for her vivid self-portraits, which explore pain, identity, love and Mexican culture. After a bus accident left her with lifelong injuries, she turned to painting. Little known outside art circles during her life, she is now one of the most recognized artists in the world.",
d:[["1907","Born in Coyoacán"],["1913","Contracts polio"],["1925","Seriously injured in a bus accident"],["1929","Marries the painter Diego Rivera"],["1939","The Louvre acquires one of her paintings"],["1953","First solo exhibition in Mexico"],["1954","Dies at the Blue House"]],
s:[["Childhood","Magdalena Carmen Frida Kahlo y Calderón was born on 6 July 1907 in Coyoacán, Mexico City, in her family’s blue house. Her father was a German-born photographer, and her mother was of Indigenous and Spanish descent. At six she caught polio, which left her right leg thinner."],
["The accident","At 18, in 1925, Frida was riding a bus that collided with a streetcar. A metal handrail pierced her body, breaking her spine, pelvis and leg. She spent months in bed in a body cast and endured about 30 operations over her life."],
["Learning to paint","While recovering, Frida began to paint. Her mother set up a special easel and a mirror above her bed so she could paint herself. She said she painted herself because she was so often alone and because she was the subject she knew best."],
["Diego Rivera","In 1929 Frida married Diego Rivera, a famous Mexican muralist 20 years older. Their marriage was stormy; they divorced in 1939 and remarried in 1940. They traveled to the United States, where Rivera painted murals."],
["Self-portraits","About 55 of Frida’s roughly 143 paintings are self-portraits. She painted herself with monkeys, parrots, flowers and Mexican symbols, often with her striking joined eyebrows. Her paintings show her physical pain, emotions and identity honestly."],
["Mexican identity","Frida celebrated Mexican culture, wearing traditional Tehuana dresses from the Isthmus of Tehuantepec and Indigenous jewelry. Her art drew on Mexican folk art, religious paintings called retablos and pre-Columbian imagery."],
["The Two Fridas","In The Two Fridas, painted in 1939, two versions of the artist sit side by side holding hands, one in a European dress and one in a Tehuana dress, their hearts exposed and connected by a vein. It expresses her divided identity and pain after her divorce."],
["Recognition","The surrealist leader André Breton praised Frida’s work, though she said she painted her own reality, not dreams. In 1939 the Louvre in Paris bought her painting The Frame, the first work by a 20th-century Mexican artist in its collection."],
["Final years","Frida’s health worsened, and in 1953 part of her right leg was amputated. That year she attended her first solo exhibition in Mexico, arriving in an ambulance and lying in her bed in the gallery. She died on 13 July 1954, aged 47."],
["Her legacy","Frida’s birthplace, the Blue House, Casa Azul, is now the Frida Kahlo Museum. Since the 1980s, she has become a global icon of strength, individuality and Mexican pride. Her paintings sell for millions of dollars, and her image appears everywhere."]],
sp:["The Blue House","La Casa Azul, the Blue House, in Coyoacán was where Frida was born, grew up and died. Its walls are painted a deep cobalt blue. She and Diego Rivera filled it with Mexican folk art, pre-Columbian sculptures and plants. Since 1958 it has been a museum displaying her paintings, studio, clothes and medical corsets. It is one of the most visited museums in Mexico City."],
g:[["Self-portrait","A picture an artist makes of themselves"],["Muralist","A painter of murals on walls"],["Surrealism","Art based on dreams and the unconscious"],["Retablo","A small Mexican religious painting"]],
f:["Frida Kahlo painted about 55 self-portraits.","She had about 30 operations.","She sometimes claimed to be born in 1910, the year the Mexican Revolution began."]},

{id:"keller",t:"Helen Keller",i:"🤲",w:"Helen Keller",a:["Helen Keller","Anne Sullivan"],born:"27 June 1880, Tuscumbia, Alabama, USA",died:"1 June 1968, Easton, Connecticut, USA",nat:"American",known:"Overcoming deafblindness to become an author and activist",r:["signlang","ear","eye"],
l:"Helen Keller lost her sight and hearing as a baby, but with the help of her teacher Anne Sullivan, she learned to communicate, read and write. She became the first deafblind person to earn a bachelor’s degree, wrote many books and campaigned around the world for people with disabilities and for social causes.",
d:[["1880","Born in Tuscumbia, Alabama"],["1882","Loses sight and hearing after an illness"],["1887","Anne Sullivan becomes her teacher"],["1903","Publishes The Story of My Life"],["1904","Graduates from Radcliffe College"],["1964","Awarded the Presidential Medal of Freedom"],["1968","Dies aged 87"]],
s:[["A sudden illness","Helen Adams Keller was born on 27 June 1880 in Tuscumbia, Alabama. At 19 months she had an illness, possibly scarlet fever or meningitis, that left her blind and deaf. Unable to communicate, she grew frustrated and often had wild tantrums."],
["Anne Sullivan","In March 1887, Anne Sullivan, a 20-year-old teacher who had herself been nearly blind, arrived from the Perkins Institution for the Blind in Boston. She began spelling words into Helen’s hand using a manual alphabet."],
["Water","At first Helen did not understand that the finger patterns stood for things. On 5 April 1887, Sullivan held Helen’s hand under a water pump and spelled w-a-t-e-r. Helen suddenly understood. By evening she had learned 30 words."],
["Learning","Helen learned rapidly. She read Braille, wrote using a special typewriter and learned to understand speech by touching people’s lips and throats. She learned to speak, though her voice was hard for strangers to understand. She studied French, German, Latin and Greek."],
["Radcliffe","With Sullivan spelling lectures into her hand, Helen studied at Radcliffe College, the women’s college linked to Harvard. In 1904 she graduated with honors, the first deafblind person to earn a bachelor’s degree."],
["Author","While at college, Helen wrote The Story of My Life, published in 1903. She wrote about a dozen books and hundreds of articles on blindness, faith, society and politics. Her books were translated into many languages."],
["Activist","Helen campaigned for women’s right to vote, workers’ rights and peace. She helped found the American Civil Liberties Union in 1920. For more than 40 years she worked for the American Foundation for the Blind, raising money and awareness."],
["World traveler","Helen visited more than 35 countries, meeting presidents and kings and encouraging services for blind and deaf people. She visited Japan several times, and her tours inspired new schools and services for blind people."],
["Later life","Anne Sullivan stayed with Helen until Sullivan’s death in 1936. Helen continued working with other companions. She received many honors, including the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 1964. She died on 1 June 1968."],
["Her legacy","Helen Keller’s life showed that people with disabilities can learn, achieve and lead. The play and film The Miracle Worker tells the story of her early education. Organizations bearing her name continue to fight blindness and poor health worldwide."]],
sp:["The Miracle Worker","The Miracle Worker, a play by William Gibson first performed on Broadway in 1959, tells the story of Anne Sullivan teaching young Helen, ending with the famous scene at the water pump. A 1962 film version won Oscars for Anne Bancroft as Sullivan and Patty Duke as Helen. The title refers to Sullivan, whose patience and determination unlocked Helen’s mind. Helen herself always gave Sullivan credit for her achievements."],
g:[["Deafblind","Having both hearing and sight loss"],["Braille","Raised dots read by touch"],["Manual alphabet","Letters spelled with the hands"],["Activist","A person who campaigns for change"]],
f:["Helen Keller learned 30 words on the day she understood water.","She met every US president from Grover Cleveland to Lyndon Johnson.","She helped found the ACLU."]},

{id:"nightingale",t:"Florence Nightingale",i:"🏮",w:"Florence Nightingale",a:["Florence Nightingale","Nightingale"],born:"12 May 1820, Florence, Italy",died:"13 August 1910, London",nat:"British",known:"Founding modern nursing and using statistics to improve health",r:["hygiene","stats","vacc"],
l:"Florence Nightingale was a British nurse, statistician and reformer who founded modern nursing. During the Crimean War she improved conditions in military hospitals and became known as the Lady with the Lamp. She used statistics and diagrams to persuade governments to improve hospitals and public health.",
d:[["1820","Born in Florence, Italy"],["1854","Leads nurses to the Crimean War"],["1858","Elected to the Royal Statistical Society"],["1859","Publishes Notes on Nursing"],["1860","Opens the Nightingale Training School"],["1907","First woman awarded the Order of Merit"],["1910","Dies in London"]],
s:[["A wealthy family","Florence Nightingale was born on 12 May 1820 in Florence, Italy, and named after the city. Her wealthy British family expected her to marry and lead a comfortable life. Her father taught her mathematics, languages and history."],
["A calling","At 16, Florence felt called by God to serve. She wanted to become a nurse, which shocked her family, as nursing was then seen as low work. She refused offers of marriage and trained in Germany at Kaiserswerth in 1850–1851."],
["The Crimean War","In 1854, during the Crimean War between Russia and an alliance including Britain, France and the Ottoman Empire, reports described terrible conditions for wounded British soldiers. Nightingale led 38 nurses to the army hospital at Scutari, near Istanbul."],
["Scutari","The hospital was filthy, overcrowded and short of supplies. Soldiers lay on floors, and many more died of diseases such as cholera, typhus and dysentery than of wounds. Nightingale organized cleaning, laundry, kitchens and supplies. A sanitary commission later improved drains and ventilation."],
["The Lady with the Lamp","At night, Nightingale walked the long wards with a lamp, checking on patients. Newspaper reports called her the Lady with the Lamp, and she became a national heroine. Soldiers wrote that they kissed her shadow as she passed."],
["Statistics","Nightingale was a gifted mathematician. After the war, she analyzed army death records and showed that most deaths were preventable. She presented her findings in polar area diagrams, a kind of pie chart, which made the data easy to understand."],
["Reforming health","Nightingale used her evidence to persuade the government to reform army hospitals and later public health in Britain and India. She advised on hospital design, sanitation and nursing in many countries, though she spent much of her later life ill in bed."],
["Nursing school","In 1860 Nightingale used donations from the public to open the Nightingale Training School for Nurses at St Thomas’ Hospital in London. Its graduates spread her methods worldwide, turning nursing into a respected profession."],
["Notes on Nursing","Her book Notes on Nursing, published in 1859, gave practical advice on fresh air, cleanliness, light, quiet and good food. It was written for ordinary women caring for family members as well as for nurses, and it became a best seller."],
["Her legacy","Nightingale died on 13 August 1910. International Nurses Day is celebrated on her birthday, 12 May. During the COVID-19 pandemic, temporary hospitals in Britain were named Nightingale Hospitals in her honor. Nurses still take the Nightingale Pledge in some countries."]],
sp:["The rose diagram","Florence Nightingale’s most famous chart, the polar area diagram, sometimes called a coxcomb or rose diagram, shows deaths in the Crimean War month by month. Each wedge is divided into colored areas for deaths from preventable diseases, from wounds and from other causes. The huge blue areas for disease made the point instantly: far more soldiers died from poor hospital conditions than from battle. The diagram is still taught as a classic example of using pictures to communicate data."],
g:[["Sanitation","Keeping places clean and safe from disease"],["Statistician","An expert in collecting and analyzing data"],["Polar area diagram","A circular chart of wedges"],["Ward","A large room in a hospital"]],
f:["Florence Nightingale was named after the city where she was born.","She was the first woman elected to the Royal Statistical Society.","International Nurses Day is held on her birthday."]},

{id:"armstrong",t:"Neil Armstrong",i:"👨‍🚀",w:"Neil Armstrong",a:["Neil Armstrong","Buzz Aldrin"],born:"5 August 1930, Wapakoneta, Ohio, USA",died:"25 August 2012, Cincinnati, Ohio, USA",nat:"American",known:"Being the first person to walk on the Moon",r:["space","moon","iss"],
l:"Neil Armstrong was an American astronaut and engineer who became the first person to walk on the Moon on 20 July 1969, as commander of Apollo 11. His words as he stepped onto the surface, That’s one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind, became among the most famous ever spoken.",
d:[["1930","Born in Wapakoneta, Ohio"],["1951","Flies combat missions in the Korean War"],["1962","Selected as a NASA astronaut"],["1966","Commands Gemini 8; first docking in space"],["20 July 1969","Walks on the Moon"],["2012","Dies aged 82"]],
s:[["Early love of flight","Neil Alden Armstrong was born on 5 August 1930 in Wapakoneta, Ohio. He took his first airplane ride at six and earned his pilot’s license at 16, before he had a driver’s license. He built model airplanes and dreamed of flying."],
["Navy pilot","Armstrong studied aeronautical engineering at Purdue University on a Navy scholarship. During the Korean War he flew 78 combat missions as a naval aviator. Once he had to eject after his plane was damaged."],
["Test pilot","After the war, Armstrong became a test pilot, flying more than 200 kinds of aircraft, including the rocket-powered X-15, which flew to the edge of space at more than 6,000 km/h."],
["Astronaut","Armstrong was selected as an astronaut in 1962. In 1966 he commanded Gemini 8, which made the first docking of two spacecraft in orbit. When a thruster stuck, sending the craft into a dangerous spin, he calmly regained control and landed safely."],
["Apollo 11","Armstrong was chosen to command Apollo 11, with Buzz Aldrin as lunar module pilot and Michael Collins as command module pilot. The Saturn V rocket launched on 16 July 1969 from Florida, carrying them toward the Moon."],
["The landing","On 20 July 1969, as the lunar module Eagle descended, Armstrong saw the computer guiding them toward a field of boulders. He took manual control and flew to a safe spot with only seconds of fuel to spare. He radioed: Houston, Tranquility Base here. The Eagle has landed."],
["One small step","Hours later, Armstrong climbed down the ladder and stepped onto the Moon, about 600 million people watching on television. He and Aldrin spent about two and a half hours outside, collecting samples, setting up experiments and planting the US flag."],
["Return home","Armstrong, Aldrin and Collins splashed down in the Pacific Ocean on 24 July 1969. After quarantine, they toured the world, including a visit to Asia. Armstrong never flew in space again."],
["A private man","Armstrong became a university engineering professor and businessman. He avoided fame, rarely gave interviews and saw himself as one of many thousands of people who made the landing possible. He died on 25 August 2012, aged 82."],
["His legacy","Armstrong’s footprint symbolized human courage and curiosity. Twelve people walked on the Moon between 1969 and 1972. Today, space agencies are returning through the Artemis program, whose crewed Artemis II mission flew around the Moon in April 2026, building on what Armstrong and the Apollo astronauts achieved."]],
sp:["A or no a?","Armstrong said he meant to say, That’s one small step for a man, one giant leap for mankind, but the a cannot be clearly heard in the recording. Without it, the sentence seems to contradict itself, since man and mankind mean the same thing. Armstrong believed he said it but that it was lost in transmission. Researchers who analyzed the audio have reached different conclusions. Either way, the meaning was clear to the world."],
g:[["Astronaut","A person trained to travel in space"],["Lunar module","The spacecraft that landed on the Moon"],["Docking","Joining two spacecraft in orbit"],["Test pilot","A pilot who flies new aircraft"]],
f:["Armstrong earned his pilot’s license before his driver’s license.","His boot prints are still on the Moon.","About 600 million people watched the landing."]},

{id:"mansamusa",t:"Mansa Musa",i:"🪙",w:"Mansa Musa",a:["Mansa Musa"],born:"c. 1280, Mali Empire",died:"c. 1337, Mali Empire",nat:"Malian",known:"Ruling the wealthy Mali Empire and his famous pilgrimage to Mecca",r:["islam","trade","money"],
l:"Mansa Musa was a ruler of the Mali Empire in West Africa in the early 1300s. His empire was rich in gold, and his pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324 was so lavish that he is often described as one of the richest people in history. He made Timbuktu a great center of Islamic learning.",
d:[["c. 1280","Born in the Mali Empire"],["c. 1312","Becomes mansa, emperor, of Mali"],["1324","Makes his pilgrimage to Mecca, passing through Cairo"],["c. 1327","Djinguereber Mosque built in Timbuktu"],["c. 1337","Dies"],["1375","Shown holding gold on the Catalan Atlas"]],
s:[["The Mali Empire","The Mali Empire, founded by Sundiata Keita in the 1200s, stretched across West Africa from the Atlantic coast to beyond the Niger River, covering parts of today’s Mali, Senegal, Guinea and Niger. It grew rich from gold mines and trade across the Sahara."],
["Becoming mansa","Musa became mansa, meaning emperor or king of kings, around 1312. According to one account, his predecessor had sailed west into the Atlantic with a great fleet and never returned, leaving Musa in charge."],
["Gold and salt","Mali controlled much of the world’s gold supply. Traders carried gold north across the Sahara on camel caravans and brought back salt, which was so valuable it was sometimes traded weight for weight with gold. Copper, ivory and cloth were also traded."],
["The pilgrimage","In 1324 Musa set out on the Hajj, the pilgrimage to Mecca. Arab writers said he traveled with thousands of people and many camels carrying gold. He gave away gold generously along the way."],
["Gold in Cairo","When Musa passed through Cairo, he gave and spent so much gold that its value fell in the city for years, according to the historian al-Umari, who visited Cairo about 12 years later. The story spread his fame across the Mediterranean world."],
["Timbuktu","Musa expanded Mali to include Timbuktu and Gao. He brought back scholars and the architect Abu Ishaq al-Sahili, who is said to have designed the Djinguereber Mosque in Timbuktu. The city became a center of trade and Islamic scholarship."],
["Learning","Under Musa and his successors, Timbuktu’s mosques and schools attracted scholars from across Africa and the Middle East. Families collected manuscripts on astronomy, mathematics, law, medicine and religion. Hundreds of thousands of these manuscripts survive."],
["On the map","Europeans learned of Mali’s wealth. On the Catalan Atlas, a famous map made in Spain in 1375, Musa is shown seated on a throne, wearing a crown and holding a gold nugget, labeled as the richest and most noble king."],
["How rich?","Some modern writers call Musa the richest person who ever lived, but his wealth cannot be measured in today’s money. He controlled vast gold resources at a time when gold was extremely valuable, and his wealth was tied to the power of his empire."],
["His legacy","Musa died around 1337. The Mali Empire declined in the following centuries. His reign is remembered as a golden age of West African history, and the Djinguereber Mosque still stands as part of a UNESCO World Heritage Site in Timbuktu."]],
sp:["The Catalan Atlas","The Catalan Atlas, made around 1375 by Abraham Cresques, a Jewish mapmaker on the island of Majorca, is one of the most important maps of the Middle Ages. It shows the known world from the Atlantic to China, decorated with gold and silver. In the middle of Africa sits Mansa Musa, holding a golden scepter and a large gold nugget. The image shows how far the fame of Mali’s wealth had spread. The atlas is now in the National Library of France."],
g:[["Mansa","A title meaning emperor in the Mali Empire"],["Hajj","The pilgrimage to Mecca"],["Caravan","A group of traders traveling together"],["Manuscript","A handwritten document"]],
f:["Mansa Musa’s gifts lowered the value of gold in Cairo.","He appears on the Catalan Atlas of 1375.","The Djinguereber Mosque is built of mud brick and wood."]}
);

;
