// People, part 8: leaders and reformers from Africa, the Americas, Asia and the Islamic world.
window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"ashoka",t:"Ashoka the Great",i:"☸️",w:"Ashoka",a:["Ashoka","Ashoka the Great"],born:"c. 304 BCE, Pataliputra (now Patna, India)",died:"c. 232 BCE",nat:"Indian (Maurya Empire)",known:"Ruling most of India and spreading Buddhism after renouncing war",r:["indus","buddh","indiacountry"],
l:"Ashoka was an emperor of the Maurya dynasty who ruled almost all of the Indian subcontinent in the 200s BCE. After a bloody conquest of the kingdom of Kalinga, he was filled with remorse, turned to Buddhism and spent the rest of his reign promoting nonviolence, tolerance and the welfare of his people. His edicts, carved on rocks and pillars, are among the oldest surviving writings in India.",
d:[["c. 304 BCE","Born, a grandson of the empire’s founder, Chandragupta Maurya"],["c. 268 BCE","Becomes emperor"],["c. 261 BCE","Conquers Kalinga in a war that kills over 100,000 people"],["c. 260 BCE","Turns to Buddhism"],["c. 232 BCE","Dies; the empire declines within decades"]],
s:[["A royal family","Ashoka was born into the Maurya dynasty, which his grandfather Chandragupta had founded around 321 BCE after driving out the generals left behind by Alexander the Great. His father, Bindusara, expanded the empire southward. As a young prince Ashoka served as a governor, putting down revolts, and he won the throne after a struggle with his brothers around 268 BCE."],
["The Kalinga war","Around 261 BCE Ashoka invaded Kalinga, a kingdom on India’s east coast, roughly modern Odisha. By his own account, 100,000 people were killed, 150,000 were carried away, and many more died of hunger and disease. Seeing the suffering, he wrote, filled him with deep sorrow and regret. It was the last war of conquest he ever fought."],
["Turning to the dharma","After Kalinga, Ashoka became a devoted Buddhist and set out to rule by dharma, a word meaning right conduct or moral law. He urged kindness to all living things, respect for parents and teachers, generosity to the poor and tolerance between religions. He gave up hunting, limited the killing of animals for the royal kitchen and sent officials to teach dharma across the empire."],
["The edicts","Ashoka had his messages carved on rocks and polished stone pillars throughout his empire, from Afghanistan to southern India. Written in several languages and scripts, including Prakrit in the Brahmi script and Greek and Aramaic in the northwest, they speak directly to his subjects. Rediscovered and deciphered in the 1830s by James Prinsep, they are a priceless source on ancient India."],
["Building and welfare","Ashoka had wells dug, rest houses built and shade trees planted along roads for travelers. He set up hospitals and clinics for people and animals and encouraged the growing of medicinal herbs. Buddhist tradition says he built 84,000 stupas, domed shrines holding relics of the Buddha, including the great stupa at Sanchi in its earliest form."],
["Spreading Buddhism","Ashoka sent missionaries far beyond India. According to tradition his son Mahinda and daughter Sanghamitta carried Buddhism to Sri Lanka, where it remains the main religion. Envoys also went to Southeast Asia and to the Greek kingdoms of the Mediterranean. These missions helped turn Buddhism from a small Indian sect into a major world religion."],
["A ruler of all faiths","Although a Buddhist, Ashoka supported Brahmins, Jains and other religious groups. One of his edicts says that whoever honors his own sect while condemning others harms his own sect, and that people should listen to and respect the teachings of others. This idea of religious tolerance is one reason he is admired by people of many faiths today."],
["The empire after him","Ashoka died around 232 BCE. His successors were weaker, and within about 50 years the Maurya Empire broke apart. For centuries he was remembered mainly in Buddhist legends, until scholars deciphered his inscriptions in the 1800s and realized how much of India he had ruled and what he had tried to do."],
["Symbols of India","When India became a republic in 1950, it chose the Lion Capital of Ashoka, four lions back to back from the top of a pillar at Sarnath, as its national emblem. The 24-spoked Ashoka Chakra, a wheel representing dharma, stands at the center of India’s flag. They appear on banknotes, coins and passports."],
["His legacy","Historians debate how far Ashoka truly lived up to his ideals, and some note that he kept a large army and the death penalty. Yet few rulers in history have publicly regretted their wars and tried to govern by compassion. The writer H. G. Wells called him one of the greatest kings who ever lived, shining almost alone like a star."]],
sp:["The Lion Capital","At Sarnath, near Varanasi, where the Buddha gave his first sermon, Ashoka raised a polished sandstone pillar topped by four Asiatic lions standing back to back above a wheel. Excavated in 1905 and now kept in the Sarnath Museum, the Lion Capital is a masterpiece of ancient Indian sculpture. Its image, with the motto Satyameva Jayate, truth alone triumphs, is India’s national emblem."],
g:[["Dharma","In Ashoka’s edicts, right conduct and moral law"],["Edict","An official public announcement or order"],["Stupa","A dome-shaped Buddhist shrine"],["Dynasty","A series of rulers from the same family"]],
f:["Ashoka’s wheel, the Ashoka Chakra, is at the center of India’s flag.","His edicts were written in several languages, including Greek and Aramaic.","Ashoka’s inscriptions were deciphered in the 1830s by James Prinsep."]},

{id:"saladin",t:"Saladin",i:"⚔️",w:"Saladin",a:["Saladin","Salah ad-Din"],born:"c. 1137, Tikrit (now in Iraq)",died:"4 March 1193, Damascus",nat:"Kurdish; sultan of Egypt and Syria",known:"Uniting Egypt and Syria and retaking Jerusalem from the Crusaders",r:["crusades","islam","egyptcountry","byzant"],
l:"Saladin, in Arabic Salah ad-Din Yusuf ibn Ayyub, was a Kurdish Muslim military leader who became sultan of Egypt and Syria and founded the Ayyubid dynasty. In 1187 he defeated the Crusaders at the Battle of Hattin and recaptured Jerusalem. Famous for his generosity and honor even toward his enemies, he was admired in both the Muslim world and Christian Europe.",
d:[["c. 1137","Born in Tikrit to a Kurdish family"],["1169","Becomes vizier of Fatimid Egypt"],["1174","Takes control of Damascus after Nur ad-Din’s death"],["1187","Wins the Battle of Hattin and retakes Jerusalem"],["1192","Makes a truce with Richard the Lionheart"],["1193","Dies in Damascus"]],
s:[["Early life","Saladin was born around 1137 in Tikrit, in what is now Iraq, into a Kurdish family of soldiers. He grew up in Baalbek and Damascus, where his father and uncle served the Turkish ruler Zengi and later his son Nur ad-Din. As a young man he studied religion and enjoyed poetry, and he had little reputation as a warrior."],
["Into Egypt","In the 1160s Nur ad-Din sent Saladin’s uncle, Shirkuh, with Saladin at his side, to Egypt, then ruled by the weakening Fatimid caliphs. After Shirkuh died in 1169, Saladin became vizier, the chief minister. In 1171 he ended the Fatimid caliphate and brought Egypt back under the Sunni Abbasid caliph in Baghdad."],
["Uniting the Muslim lands","After Nur ad-Din died in 1174, Saladin took Damascus and over the next decade brought Aleppo, Mosul and much of Syria and northern Iraq under his control. For the first time in a century, the Crusader states faced a single Muslim ruler controlling the lands around them on several sides."],
["Hattin and Jerusalem","On 4 July 1187, Saladin trapped a Crusader army near the Horns of Hattin, above the Sea of Galilee, cutting it off from water in the summer heat. The Crusaders were crushed. In October, after a short siege, Jerusalem surrendered. Unlike the Crusaders, who massacred the city’s people in 1099, Saladin allowed many inhabitants to ransom themselves and leave."],
["The Third Crusade","The fall of Jerusalem shocked Europe, and the kings of England, France and the Holy Roman Empire launched the Third Crusade. Richard I of England, the Lionheart, captured the port of Acre in 1191 and won battles at Arsuf and Jaffa, but he could not retake Jerusalem. In 1192 Saladin and Richard agreed to a truce."],
["The Treaty of Jaffa","The truce of 1192 left Jerusalem under Muslim rule while allowing Christian pilgrims to visit freely, and left the Crusaders a strip of coast from Tyre to Jaffa. Richard sailed home, and the two great rivals never met in person, though they exchanged messages and, according to stories, gifts."],
["A generous ruler","Saladin was known for his piety, mercy and generosity. Stories tell that he sent fruit and snow to Richard when the king was ill and a fresh horse when Richard’s was killed in battle. He gave away so much of his wealth that, when he died, there was not enough money in his treasury to pay for his funeral."],
["Builder and patron","Saladin built the Citadel of Cairo, a great fortress that remained the seat of Egypt’s rulers for 700 years, and founded schools, hospitals and religious colleges. He supported scholars and Sunni learning, and his rule brought stability to Egypt and Syria after decades of war."],
["Death and the Ayyubids","Saladin fell ill with a fever and died in Damascus on 4 March 1193, at about 55. He was buried near the Umayyad Mosque, where his tomb can still be visited. His relatives, the Ayyubid dynasty, ruled Egypt and Syria until 1250, when they were replaced by the Mamluks."],
["A legend in two worlds","In the Muslim world Saladin became a symbol of unity and the liberation of Jerusalem. In medieval Europe, writers turned him into a model of chivalry, and the Italian poet Dante placed him among the virtuous non-Christians in his Divine Comedy. Novels and films have retold his story many times since."]],
sp:["The Citadel of Cairo","In 1176 Saladin began building a fortress on a spur of the Mokattam Hills overlooking Cairo, to protect the city from Crusaders and rivals. Its walls, towers and a deep well cut into the rock, called Joseph’s Well, made it nearly impregnable. The Citadel remained the home of Egypt’s rulers until the 1800s, when Muhammad Ali built his great mosque inside it, now one of Cairo’s landmarks."],
g:[["Sultan","A Muslim ruler"],["Vizier","A chief minister in a Muslim state"],["Crusader states","Kingdoms set up by European Crusaders in the Middle East"],["Truce","An agreement to stop fighting for a time"]],
f:["Saladin was Kurdish, born in Tikrit in present-day Iraq.","When he died, his treasury did not have enough money to pay for his funeral.","His tomb is beside the Umayyad Mosque in Damascus."]},

{id:"sejong",t:"Sejong the Great",i:"📜",w:"Sejong the Great",a:["Sejong the Great","King Sejong"],born:"15 May 1397, Hanseong (now Seoul)",died:"8 April 1450, Hanseong",nat:"Korean (Joseon dynasty)",known:"Creating the Korean alphabet, Hangul, and promoting science",r:["korea","alphabet","southkoreacountry"],
l:"Sejong the Great was the fourth king of Korea’s Joseon dynasty, ruling from 1418 to 1450. He is best known for creating Hangul, the Korean alphabet, so that ordinary people could learn to read and write. His reign was also a golden age of science, music, farming and government, and he is the only Korean king, with one other, honored with the title the Great.",
d:[["1397","Born a son of King Taejong"],["1418","Becomes king at 21"],["1420","Expands the Hall of Worthies, a royal research institute"],["1441","A standardized rain gauge is introduced"],["1443","Completes the Korean alphabet"],["1446","Publishes Hangul as the Correct Sounds for the Instruction of the People"],["1450","Dies in Hanseong"]],
s:[["A scholarly prince","Yi Do, later King Sejong, was born in 1397, the third son of King Taejong. He loved books so much that his father worried about his health and once took his books away. Because of his wisdom and good character, Taejong chose him as heir over his older brothers, and Sejong became king in 1418 at the age of 21."],
["The Hall of Worthies","Sejong gathered the brightest scholars of Korea in the Jiphyeonjeon, the Hall of Worthies, a royal academy. They studied history, law, medicine, farming, astronomy and language, and wrote books for the king and the people. Many of his great projects began there."],
["Creating Hangul","At the time, Koreans wrote using Chinese characters, which took years to learn, so most people could not read. In 1443 Sejong completed a new alphabet of 28 letters designed to match the sounds of Korean. Some consonant shapes show the position of the mouth or tongue, and vowels are built from symbols for heaven, earth and humankind."],
["Opposition","Many scholars and officials opposed the new script, arguing that Chinese characters were the mark of civilization and that a simple alphabet would weaken learning. Sejong defended it, saying he made it out of pity for the common people who could not express their thoughts. It was published in 1446 in a book called Hunminjeongeum, the Correct Sounds for the Instruction of the People."],
["Science and inventions","Sejong supported scientists such as Jang Yeong-sil, a man of humble birth who rose to court rank. They built water clocks that struck the hours automatically, sundials, celestial globes and a standard rain gauge, introduced in 1441, which allowed rainfall to be measured across the country, two centuries before such gauges were used in Europe."],
["Farming and calendars","To help farmers, Sejong had officials collect the methods of experienced Korean farmers and publish them in a farming handbook suited to Korea’s climate. His astronomers produced a calendar based on observations from Seoul, rather than relying only on Chinese calendars. Fair land taxes were set according to soil quality and harvests."],
["Music and medicine","Sejong worked with the musician Bak Yeon to reform court music, standardizing instruments and creating new pieces. He also ordered the compilation of great medical encyclopedias that combined Korean remedies with Chinese medicine, using herbs that grew in Korea so ordinary people could afford treatment."],
["Defending the kingdom","Sejong strengthened Korea’s northern border along the Yalu and Tumen rivers, which still form much of the border today, and early in his reign Korea sent an expedition against pirates based on Tsushima Island, in 1419. He also improved weapons, including early rocket launchers."],
["A caring ruler","Sejong was known for his concern for ordinary people. He reduced harsh punishments, gave maternity leave to slave women and their husbands, and asked his officials for honest advice. He worked so hard that he suffered from poor health, including diabetes and failing eyesight, in his later years. He died in 1450."],
["His legacy","Hangul was suppressed by some later rulers but survived and became Korea’s national script in the 1900s. Today Sejong appears on the 10,000-won banknote, a huge statue of him sits in Gwanghwamun Square in Seoul, and Hangul Day, 9 October, is a national holiday. UNESCO’s prize for literacy is named the King Sejong Literacy Prize."]],
sp:["The rain gauge","In 1441 Korea introduced the cheugugi, a cylindrical bronze rain gauge set on a stone base, and placed copies in towns across the country. Officials measured rainfall after each storm and reported it to the capital, building a national record of rainfall that helped plan farming and taxes. It was one of the earliest standardized rain-gauge networks in the world, about two centuries before similar measurements in Europe."],
g:[["Alphabet","A set of letters that stand for sounds"],["Joseon","The Korean dynasty that ruled from 1392 to 1910"],["Literacy","The ability to read and write"],["Rain gauge","An instrument for measuring rainfall"]],
f:["Sejong appears on South Korea’s 10,000-won note.","Hangul Day, 9 October, is a national holiday in South Korea.","UNESCO’s King Sejong Literacy Prize is named after him."]},

{id:"bolivar",t:"Simón Bolívar",i:"🗡️",w:"Simón Bolívar",a:["Simón Bolívar","Bolívar"],born:"24 July 1783, Caracas, Venezuela",died:"17 December 1830, Santa Marta, Colombia",nat:"Venezuelan",known:"Leading the independence of much of South America from Spain",r:["explor","decol","usrev","spanish"],
l:"Simón Bolívar was a Venezuelan military and political leader who led the struggle for independence from Spain across much of South America. Called El Libertador, the Liberator, he helped free the lands that became Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Panama and Bolivia, which is named after him. He dreamed of a united South America, a dream that broke apart before his death.",
d:[["1783","Born in Caracas"],["1805","Vows in Rome to free his homeland"],["1813","Wins the title the Liberator after his Admirable Campaign"],["1819","Crosses the Andes and wins the Battle of Boyacá"],["1821","Wins the Battle of Carabobo, securing Venezuela"],["1824","Final victories in Peru at Junín and Ayacucho"],["1830","Dies of tuberculosis in Santa Marta"]],
s:[["A rich orphan","Simón Bolívar was born in 1783 in Caracas into one of the richest families in Venezuela, then part of Spain’s empire. His father died when he was two and his mother when he was nine. He was raised by relatives and tutors, including Simón Rodríguez, who taught him the ideas of the Enlightenment about liberty and reason."],
["Europe and a vow","As a young man Bolívar traveled to Spain, where he married in 1802, but his wife died of fever less than a year after they returned to Venezuela. Heartbroken, he went back to Europe, saw Napoleon crowned in Paris and, in 1805 on Rome’s Monte Sacro, swore not to rest until his homeland was free from Spain."],
["The fight begins","When Napoleon invaded Spain in 1808, its colonies began governing themselves. Venezuela declared independence in 1811, but the first republic collapsed. Bolívar fled, then returned in 1813, sweeping through Venezuela in his Admirable Campaign and winning the title Liberator. The war was savage, and he too ordered harsh reprisals."],
["Defeat and exile","Royalist forces, with many local fighters, crushed the second republic in 1814. Bolívar fled to Jamaica and Haiti. In his Jamaica Letter of 1815, he set out his hopes for a free Spanish America. Haiti’s president, Alexandre Pétion, gave him ships and weapons, asking only that he free the enslaved people of the lands he liberated."],
["Across the Andes","In 1819 Bolívar led an army across the flooded plains and over the freezing Andes, a march that killed many men and horses, and surprised the Spanish at the Battle of Boyacá. Bogotá fell, and the Republic of Colombia, later called Gran Colombia, was proclaimed, with Bolívar as president."],
["Victory in the north","In 1821 Bolívar’s army won the Battle of Carabobo, freeing Venezuela, and in 1822 his general Antonio José de Sucre won at Pichincha, freeing Ecuador. That year Bolívar met José de San Martín, liberator of Argentina and Chile, at Guayaquil. San Martín withdrew, leaving Bolívar to finish the war."],
["Peru and Bolivia","In 1824 Bolívar’s forces won the Battle of Junín, and Sucre’s victory at Ayacucho effectively ended Spanish rule in South America. In 1825 Upper Peru became a new republic and named itself Bolivia in his honor. Bolívar wrote its constitution, which made him a president for life in all but name."],
["A broken dream","Bolívar wanted the new nations to stay united, and in 1826 he called a congress in Panama to form a league of American republics. But regional leaders, rivalries and distrust of his power tore Gran Colombia apart. He survived an assassination attempt in 1828, and Venezuela and Ecuador broke away in 1830."],
["Last days","Ill with tuberculosis and disillusioned, Bolívar resigned in 1830 and set out for exile in Europe, but he died on 17 December 1830 near Santa Marta, Colombia, at the age of 47. He is said to have written that those who serve a revolution plough the sea."],
["His legacy","Bolívar is honored across Latin America. Bolivia and Venezuela, officially the Bolivarian Republic, bear his name, as do Venezuela’s currency, the bolívar, and countless squares, streets and statues. Historians admire his courage and vision while also debating his authoritarian side and his complex record on slavery and Indigenous rights."]],
sp:["The crossing of the Andes","In June and July 1819, Bolívar led about 2,500 soldiers from the hot plains of Venezuela, the llanos, through flooded lands and up the frozen Páramo de Pisba pass, at about 4,000 meters. Many soldiers, poorly clothed, died of cold, and almost all the horses were lost. But the Spanish did not expect an army from that direction, and on 7 August Bolívar won the decisive Battle of Boyacá."],
g:[["Liberator","A person who sets others free"],["Royalist","A supporter of the king, here of Spanish rule"],["Gran Colombia","The republic of Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador and Panama from 1819 to 1831"],["Enlightenment","An 18th-century movement emphasizing reason and liberty"]],
f:["The country of Bolivia is named after Simón Bolívar.","Venezuela’s currency is called the bolívar.","Bolívar fought more than 100 battles in the wars of independence."]},

{id:"toussaint",t:"Toussaint Louverture",i:"⛓️",w:"Toussaint Louverture",a:["Toussaint Louverture"],born:"c. 1743, Saint-Domingue (now Haiti)",died:"7 April 1803, Fort de Joux, France",nat:"Haitian",known:"Leading the Haitian Revolution, the only successful revolt of enslaved people to found a nation",r:["frrev","usrev","decol"],
l:"Toussaint Louverture was a formerly enslaved man who became the leader of the Haitian Revolution in the French colony of Saint-Domingue. A brilliant general and statesman, he defeated French, Spanish and British forces and ended slavery in the colony. Though he was captured and died in a French prison, the revolution he led created Haiti in 1804, the first independent Black republic and the only nation born of a successful uprising of enslaved people.",
d:[["c. 1743","Born enslaved on a plantation near Cap-Français"],["c. 1776","Gains his freedom"],["1791","The great slave uprising begins in Saint-Domingue"],["1794","France abolishes slavery; Toussaint joins the French side"],["1801","Issues a constitution making him governor for life"],["1802","Is captured by French forces and deported"],["1803","Dies in prison in France"]],
s:[["The richest colony","In the 1700s, Saint-Domingue, the western part of the island of Hispaniola, was France’s richest colony, producing much of the world’s sugar and coffee. Its wealth rested on about half a million enslaved Africans, worked brutally on plantations, who far outnumbered the white colonists and free people of color."],
["Early life","Toussaint was born into slavery around 1743 on the Bréda plantation. Unusually, he learned to read and write, knew herbal medicine and became a coachman and steward. He gained his freedom around 1776 and even rented a small coffee farm, while remaining closely tied to the enslaved community."],
["The uprising","In August 1791, inspired partly by the French Revolution’s ideas of liberty and equality, enslaved people in northern Saint-Domingue rose in a massive revolt, burning plantations. Toussaint soon joined, using his knowledge of medicine and organization, and rose to become one of its most important commanders."],
["Changing sides","At first the rebels allied with Spain, which held the eastern part of the island. When the French revolutionary government abolished slavery in its colonies in 1794, Toussaint switched to the French side. He took the name Louverture, meaning the opening, and built a disciplined army of formerly enslaved soldiers."],
["Defeating empires","Toussaint drove out the Spanish and then a large British invasion force, which had hoped to seize the colony and restore slavery. The British withdrew in 1798, having lost thousands of soldiers to fighting and disease. Toussaint also defeated rivals within the colony and by 1801 controlled the whole island."],
["Governor of Saint-Domingue","In 1801 Toussaint issued a constitution that abolished slavery forever and made him governor for life, while still recognizing France. He tried to restore the plantation economy with paid but strictly controlled labor, encouraged trade with Britain and the United States, and welcomed back white planters willing to accept the new order."],
["Napoleon’s invasion","Napoleon Bonaparte, now ruling France, wanted to restore French control and slavery. In 1802 he sent a huge army under his brother-in-law, General Leclerc. After months of fierce fighting, Toussaint agreed to a truce, but he was tricked into a meeting, arrested and shipped to France."],
["Death in the mountains","Toussaint was imprisoned in Fort de Joux, a cold fortress in the Jura Mountains. As he was taken aboard ship, he reportedly warned that in overthrowing him the French had cut down only the trunk of the tree of liberty, which would grow back from its many deep roots. He died of illness and cold on 7 April 1803."],
["Haiti is born","Toussaint’s former officers, led by Jean-Jacques Dessalines, continued the fight. Yellow fever and fierce resistance destroyed the French army, and in 1803 France was defeated at the Battle of Vertières. On 1 January 1804 Dessalines declared the independence of Haiti, taking back an Indigenous Taíno name for the island."],
["His legacy","The Haitian Revolution terrified slaveholders across the Americas and inspired enslaved and colonized people everywhere. Napoleon’s defeat in Haiti also led him to sell the vast Louisiana territory to the United States in 1803. Toussaint is remembered as one of the great liberators of history, and his story has inspired poems, plays and histories."]],
sp:["The Black Jacobins","In 1938 the Trinidadian historian C. L. R. James published The Black Jacobins, a history of Toussaint Louverture and the Haitian Revolution. It showed the revolution as a central event of the age of revolutions, alongside those in America and France, led by enslaved people who took the ideals of liberty and equality more seriously than many Europeans did. The book helped bring Toussaint’s story to a worldwide audience."],
g:[["Plantation","A large farm growing crops such as sugar, worked by many laborers"],["Abolition","Ending slavery by law"],["Colony","A territory ruled by another country"],["Saint-Domingue","The French colony that became Haiti"]],
f:["Haiti became independent on 1 January 1804.","Louverture means the opening in French.","Napoleon’s defeat in Haiti helped lead to the Louisiana Purchase."]},

{id:"nkrumah",t:"Kwame Nkrumah",i:"✊",w:"Kwame Nkrumah",a:["Kwame Nkrumah"],born:"21 September 1909, Nkroful, Gold Coast (now Ghana)",died:"27 April 1972, Bucharest, Romania",nat:"Ghanaian",known:"Leading Ghana to independence and championing African unity",r:["decol","mandela","gandhi"],
l:"Kwame Nkrumah was a Ghanaian politician who led the British colony of the Gold Coast to independence in 1957 as Ghana, the first country in sub-Saharan Africa to free itself from colonial rule. As prime minister and then president, he championed Pan-Africanism, the idea of a united Africa, and helped found the Organization of African Unity. His rule later became authoritarian, and he was overthrown in 1966.",
d:[["1909","Born in Nkroful, Gold Coast"],["1935","Travels to the United States to study"],["1947","Returns to the Gold Coast"],["1949","Founds the Convention People’s Party"],["1957","Ghana becomes independent with Nkrumah as prime minister"],["1963","Helps found the Organization of African Unity"],["1966","Overthrown in a military coup"]],
s:[["Early life","Kwame Nkrumah was born in 1909 in Nkroful, a village in the southwest of the Gold Coast, a British colony. His mother was a trader. He was educated at mission schools and trained as a teacher at Achimota College, where he was inspired by the educator James Aggrey, who believed Africans should be proud of their heritage."],
["Studying abroad","In 1935 Nkrumah sailed to the United States, where he studied at Lincoln University and the University of Pennsylvania, working odd jobs to pay his way. He read the works of Marcus Garvey and W. E. B. Du Bois and joined African student groups. In 1945 he moved to London and helped organize the Fifth Pan-African Congress in Manchester."],
["Back to the Gold Coast","In 1947 Nkrumah returned home to work for a nationalist party. Frustrated by its caution, in 1949 he founded the Convention People’s Party with the slogan Self-Government Now. He organized boycotts and strikes in a campaign he called Positive Action, inspired by Gandhi’s nonviolence."],
["From prison to power","In 1950 the British jailed Nkrumah for sedition. While he was in prison, his party won the 1951 election by a landslide, and the governor released him to lead the government. Over the next six years he worked with Britain toward independence."],
["Independence","At midnight on 6 March 1957, Nkrumah declared the Gold Coast free and renamed it Ghana, after an ancient West African empire. He told the crowd that Ghana was free forever and that its independence was meaningless unless it was linked with the total liberation of Africa. Ghana inspired independence movements across the continent."],
["Building a nation","Nkrumah’s government built schools, hospitals, roads and a new port at Tema, and the huge Akosombo Dam on the Volta River, creating Lake Volta, one of the largest artificial lakes in the world. He wanted Ghana to industrialize quickly, but falling cocoa prices, debt and costly projects strained the economy."],
["Pan-Africanism","Nkrumah believed that Africa could only be truly free and strong if it united. He hosted conferences of African leaders and supported liberation movements. In 1963 he helped found the Organization of African Unity in Addis Ababa, though most leaders rejected his call for a single African government."],
["Toward one-party rule","Nkrumah grew increasingly authoritarian. Opponents could be jailed without trial under a Preventive Detention Act, and in 1964 Ghana became a one-party state with him as president for life. A personality cult grew around him, and the economy worsened."],
["Overthrow and exile","In February 1966, while Nkrumah was on a trip to China and Vietnam, the army and police seized power in Ghana. He went into exile in Guinea, where his friend President Sékou Touré named him co-president. He continued to write about Africa and died of cancer in Bucharest, Romania, in 1972."],
["His legacy","Nkrumah remains a towering and debated figure, admired as a father of African independence and Pan-Africanism, criticized for his authoritarian rule. In 2000 listeners of the BBC in Africa voted him Africa’s man of the millennium. His mausoleum stands in Accra, and the African Union, which replaced the Organization of African Unity in 2002, carries on his dream of unity."]],
sp:["Independence night","At midnight on 6 March 1957, at the Old Polo Grounds in Accra, the British flag was lowered and Ghana’s red, gold and green flag with its black star was raised. Kwame Nkrumah, wearing a cap of the prisoners who had been jailed with him, danced and wept as he addressed the crowd. Guests included Martin Luther King Jr., who said the moment made him think of the American struggle for freedom."],
g:[["Pan-Africanism","The idea that Africans and people of African descent should unite"],["Colony","A territory ruled by another country"],["Sedition","Speech or action encouraging rebellion against authority"],["Coup","The sudden seizure of power, often by the army"]],
f:["Ghana takes its name from an ancient West African empire.","Lake Volta, created by Nkrumah’s Akosombo Dam, is one of the largest artificial lakes in the world.","Martin Luther King Jr. attended Ghana’s independence celebrations in 1957."]}
);

;
