// People, part 9: scientists, explorers and campaigners from around the world, with more women.
window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"ibnsina",t:"Ibn Sina (Avicenna)",i:"⚕️",w:"Avicenna",a:["Ibn Sina","Avicenna"],born:"c. 980, near Bukhara (now Uzbekistan)",died:"June 1037, Hamadan, Persia (now Iran)",nat:"Persian",known:"The Canon of Medicine and major works of philosophy",r:["goldage","irancountry","immune","scimeth"],
l:"Ibn Sina, known in Europe as Avicenna, was a Persian physician, philosopher and scientist of the Islamic Golden Age. His Canon of Medicine summed up the medical knowledge of the Greek, Persian and Islamic worlds and was used as a textbook in universities from the Middle East to Europe for some 600 years. He wrote hundreds of works on medicine, philosophy, astronomy, mathematics and music.",
d:[["c. 980","Born near Bukhara"],["c. 997","Cures the ruler of Bukhara and gains access to the royal library"],["c. 1012","Begins writing The Canon of Medicine"],["c. 1027","Completes The Book of Healing"],["1037","Dies in Hamadan"],["1473","The Canon is printed in Latin in Europe"]],
s:[["A child prodigy","Ibn Sina was born around 980 near Bukhara, then a capital of the Persian Samanid dynasty. By the age of ten he had memorized the Quran, and he quickly mastered logic, mathematics and philosophy. He later said he read Aristotle’s Metaphysics 40 times without understanding it, until a short commentary by al-Farabi made it clear."],
["A young doctor","Ibn Sina turned to medicine as a teenager and found it easy. By 16 he was treating patients, and at about 17 he cured the Samanid ruler of a serious illness. As a reward he was allowed to use the royal library, one of the finest of its time, where he read books on every subject."],
["A wandering life","After the Samanid dynasty fell, Ibn Sina moved from court to court across Persia, serving rulers as a physician and sometimes as a minister. Political turmoil meant he was at times imprisoned or forced to flee in disguise. Through it all he kept writing, often at night, dictating to students."],
["The Canon of Medicine","Ibn Sina’s greatest medical work, The Canon of Medicine, filled five books. It described diseases and their symptoms, the uses of hundreds of drugs and methods of diagnosis such as taking the pulse. It organized the ideas of Greek doctors such as Galen and Hippocrates together with Persian, Indian and Islamic knowledge and his own observations."],
["New ideas in medicine","The Canon recognized that some diseases, such as tuberculosis, are contagious and can spread through soil and water. It suggested rules for testing new medicines, including trying them on simple cases and checking that effects are consistent, ideas close to modern clinical trials. It also described the link between emotions and physical health."],
["The Book of Healing","Ibn Sina’s philosophical encyclopedia, The Book of Healing, despite its name, covered logic, natural science, mathematics and metaphysics. He developed Aristotle’s ideas and added his own, including a famous thought experiment, the floating man, which argued that a person would know they exist even without any senses."],
["Science and mathematics","Ibn Sina also wrote on astronomy, geology, chemistry and physics. He described how mountains form through erosion and earth movements, observed a transit of Venus and argued against the alchemists’ claim that metals could be turned into gold. He wrote on music theory as well."],
["Death","Ibn Sina lived an intense life of work and pleasure. He suffered from a stomach illness and died in 1037 in Hamadan, in western Iran, at about 57. His tomb there, rebuilt in the 1950s with a tower inspired by an ancient monument, is a place of pilgrimage for many Iranians."],
["Influence in Europe","In the 1100s, scholars in Spain translated the Canon into Latin. It became a standard medical textbook in European universities, such as Montpellier and Leuven, until the 1600s. Thomas Aquinas and other medieval philosophers studied his writings. Dante named him among the great thinkers in the Divine Comedy."],
["His legacy","Ibn Sina is remembered as one of the most important scholars of all time. Iran, Uzbekistan, Tajikistan and Afghanistan all claim him as a national figure, and his name is given to universities, hospitals and a lunar crater. The medical profession still honors his insistence on careful observation and testing."]],
sp:["The floating man","Ibn Sina asked readers to imagine a person created fully grown, floating in the air, blindfolded, with limbs spread so they touch nothing and hear nothing. Such a person, he argued, would still know that they exist, even with no information from the senses. This proved, he said, that the self, or soul, is something different from the body. The idea anticipated René Descartes’s I think, therefore I am by about 600 years."],
g:[["Physician","A doctor of medicine"],["Canon","A collection of authoritative rules or writings"],["Contagious","Able to spread from one person to another"],["Metaphysics","The branch of philosophy about existence and reality"]],
f:["Ibn Sina was treating patients by the age of 16.","His Canon of Medicine was used in European universities for about 600 years.","A crater on the Moon is named Avicenna."]},

{id:"hypatia",t:"Hypatia",i:"📐",w:"Hypatia",a:["Hypatia"],born:"c. 360 CE, Alexandria, Egypt",died:"March 415, Alexandria",nat:"Greek (Roman Egypt)",known:"Mathematics, astronomy and teaching philosophy in ancient Alexandria",r:["greece","euclid","egyptcountry","scimeth"],
l:"Hypatia was a mathematician, astronomer and philosopher in Alexandria, Egypt, around the year 400 CE. She is the first woman mathematician whose life is reasonably well recorded. A popular teacher who led a school of philosophy, she was murdered by a Christian mob in 415, and her death has made her a symbol of learning and of women in science.",
d:[["c. 360","Born in Alexandria"],["c. 380s","Begins teaching philosophy and mathematics"],["c. 400","Becomes head of a school of Neoplatonist philosophy"],["412","Cyril becomes bishop of Alexandria"],["415","Murdered by a mob in Alexandria"]],
s:[["The city of learning","Alexandria, founded by Alexander the Great in 331 BCE, was the greatest center of learning in the ancient world. Its library and museum drew scholars from around the Mediterranean, including Euclid and Eratosthenes. By Hypatia’s time it was part of the Roman Empire and a bustling city of Greeks, Egyptians, Jews and Christians."],
["Her father","Hypatia was the daughter of Theon, a mathematician and astronomer who wrote commentaries on Euclid’s Elements and Ptolemy’s astronomy. Theon educated her himself, and she soon surpassed him. Ancient writers say he trained her body as well as her mind, so she was strong and healthy as well as learned."],
["Mathematician","Hypatia worked on some of the hardest mathematics of her day. She is believed to have written commentaries on Diophantus’s Arithmetica, an early work of algebra, and on Apollonius’s Conics, about the curves made by slicing a cone, such as ellipses and parabolas. She also helped her father edit Ptolemy’s great work on astronomy."],
["Astronomer","Hypatia studied the movements of the Sun, Moon and planets. A letter from her student Synesius asks her help in making an astrolabe, an instrument for measuring the positions of stars, and a hydrometer, for measuring the density of liquids. She may have helped prepare astronomical tables."],
["A celebrated teacher","Hypatia taught philosophy, especially the ideas of Plato and Plotinus, known as Neoplatonism. Students came from across the Mediterranean to hear her, both pagans and Christians. She lectured in public, wearing a scholar’s cloak, and was admired for her wisdom, modesty and self-control."],
["A respected adviser","Hypatia was consulted by Alexandria’s leaders, including the Roman governor, Orestes. As a woman of great learning and influence in public life, she was unusual for her time. Her student Synesius, who became a bishop, wrote to her with deep respect, calling her mother, sister, teacher and benefactor."],
["A city in conflict","In the early 400s, Alexandria was torn by conflict between pagans, Christians and Jews and between the governor Orestes and the powerful bishop Cyril. Some Christians spread rumors that Hypatia was preventing the two men from making peace and accused her of witchcraft."],
["Her murder","In March 415 a mob of Christians led by a man named Peter seized Hypatia from her carriage, dragged her into a church and killed her brutally. Her death shocked many people at the time. Historians still debate how far Cyril was responsible, but most agree she was caught up in a violent political struggle."],
["Lost works","None of Hypatia’s writings survive in her own name, and we know her mainly through letters of her students and the accounts of later historians. Some scholars think her editing may survive in versions of her father’s commentaries. The loss shows how much knowledge from the ancient world has disappeared."],
["A lasting symbol","Over the centuries, Hypatia has been portrayed as a martyr for reason, a heroine of women’s learning and a figure in novels, plays and films, such as Agora in 2009. A journal of feminist philosophy and an asteroid bear her name. Modern historians try to separate the real woman from the legend."]],
sp:["Conic sections","Hypatia studied the work of Apollonius of Perga on conic sections, the curves produced when a flat plane slices through a cone at different angles: a circle, an ellipse, a parabola and a hyperbola. These curves seemed purely abstract in her day, but more than a thousand years later Johannes Kepler found that planets travel in ellipses, and parabolas describe the path of a thrown ball or the shape of a satellite dish."],
g:[["Neoplatonism","A school of philosophy based on the ideas of Plato"],["Astrolabe","An instrument used to measure the positions of stars and planets"],["Commentary","A book that explains and discusses another work"],["Conic section","A curve made by slicing a cone with a plane"]],
f:["Hypatia is the first woman mathematician whose life is reasonably well documented.","None of her writings survive under her own name.","An asteroid, 238 Hypatia, is named after her."]},

{id:"ramanujan",t:"Srinivasa Ramanujan",i:"∞",w:"Srinivasa Ramanujan",a:["Srinivasa Ramanujan","Ramanujan"],born:"22 December 1887, Erode, India",died:"26 April 1920, Kumbakonam, India",nat:"Indian",known:"Brilliant discoveries in number theory, infinite series and continued fractions",r:["infin","pi","prime","indiacountry"],
l:"Srinivasa Ramanujan was an Indian mathematician who, with almost no formal training, made extraordinary discoveries in number theory, infinite series and other areas of mathematics. After he wrote to the English mathematician G. H. Hardy in 1913, he was invited to Cambridge, where he became one of the youngest Fellows of the Royal Society. He died at only 32, leaving notebooks of results that mathematicians are still exploring.",
d:[["1887","Born in Erode, Tamil Nadu"],["1903","Studies a book of mathematical results that inspires him"],["1913","Writes to G. H. Hardy in Cambridge"],["1914","Travels to England"],["1918","Elected Fellow of the Royal Society"],["1919","Returns to India, gravely ill"],["1920","Dies at the age of 32"]],
s:[["A gifted child","Srinivasa Ramanujan was born in 1887 in Erode, in southern India, and grew up in the town of Kumbakonam, where his father was a clerk in a cloth shop. He showed a remarkable talent for numbers from an early age, and by his early teens he had mastered advanced trigonometry and was discovering theorems of his own."],
["A book of results","At about 16, Ramanujan borrowed a book by G. S. Carr listing thousands of mathematical results with few proofs. It inspired him to work out formulas himself, filling notebooks with discoveries. He became so absorbed in mathematics that he failed his other college subjects and lost his scholarship."],
["Poverty and persistence","For years Ramanujan struggled, without a degree or a steady job, while working on mathematics. He married in 1909, as was the custom, and eventually found work as a clerk at the Madras Port Trust in 1912, whose officials encouraged his research. Indian mathematicians began to notice his work."],
["A letter to Cambridge","In January 1913, Ramanujan wrote to G. H. Hardy, a leading mathematician at Cambridge, enclosing pages of formulas. Hardy at first suspected a hoax, but after studying them with his colleague J. E. Littlewood, he concluded they must have been written by a mathematician of the highest class, because no one could have the imagination to invent them."],
["To England","Ramanujan, a devout Hindu, hesitated to cross the sea, which his community forbade, but in 1914 he sailed for England. At Cambridge he worked with Hardy, who taught him the rigorous methods of proof he lacked, while Ramanujan’s intuition led them to new results. Hardy later called their collaboration the one romantic incident in his life."],
["Partitions","Ramanujan and Hardy found an astonishing formula for the number of partitions of a number, the ways it can be written as a sum of whole numbers. For example, 4 can be written in five ways: 4, 3+1, 2+2, 2+1+1 and 1+1+1+1. Their formula gives answers for huge numbers with amazing accuracy."],
["Infinite series and pi","Ramanujan discovered remarkable infinite series for calculating pi that converge extremely fast, adding many correct digits with each term. Versions of his formulas have been used by computers to calculate pi to billions and trillions of digits. He also worked on continued fractions, elliptic functions and mock theta functions."],
["Honors and illness","In 1918 Ramanujan was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society, one of the youngest ever, and a Fellow of Trinity College, Cambridge, the first Indian to receive both of these honors. But the cold, wartime food shortages and his strict vegetarian diet harmed his health, and he fell seriously ill, probably with tuberculosis and a liver infection."],
["Return and death","Ramanujan returned to India in 1919 and continued working from his sickbed. He died on 26 April 1920 in Kumbakonam, at just 32. In his last year he wrote about mock theta functions, which mathematicians only fully understood in the 2000s, finding connections to modern physics, including black holes."],
["His legacy","Ramanujan’s notebooks contain nearly 4,000 results, most of them later proved correct. A lost notebook, rediscovered in 1976, revealed more. His birthday, 22 December, is National Mathematics Day in India, and his life was told in the film The Man Who Knew Infinity in 2015. A prize for young mathematicians from developing countries bears his name."]],
sp:["The taxicab number","When Hardy visited Ramanujan in hospital, he said his taxi had the dull number 1729. No, Ramanujan replied at once, it is a very interesting number: the smallest number that can be expressed as the sum of two cubes in two different ways, 1 cubed plus 12 cubed and 9 cubed plus 10 cubed. Such numbers are now called taxicab numbers, and 1729 is known as the Hardy–Ramanujan number."],
g:[["Number theory","The branch of mathematics that studies whole numbers"],["Infinite series","A sum with infinitely many terms"],["Partition","A way of writing a number as a sum of whole numbers"],["Theorem","A mathematical statement that has been proved"]],
f:["The number 1729 is known as the Hardy–Ramanujan number.","Ramanujan’s birthday, 22 December, is National Mathematics Day in India.","His notebooks contain nearly 4,000 results."]},

{id:"kjohnson",t:"Katherine Johnson",i:"🚀",w:"Katherine Johnson",a:["Katherine Johnson"],born:"26 August 1918, White Sulphur Springs, West Virginia, United States",died:"24 February 2020, Newport News, Virginia",nat:"American",known:"Calculating flight paths for early American spaceflights",r:["apollo","space","rocket","mlk"],
l:"Katherine Johnson was an African American mathematician whose calculations of orbital paths helped send the first Americans into space and to the Moon. Working at NASA and its predecessor, at first as one of the human computers in a racially segregated office, she checked the computer calculations for John Glenn’s orbital flight in 1962 and helped plan the Apollo missions. In 2015 she received the Presidential Medal of Freedom.",
d:[["1918","Born in White Sulphur Springs, West Virginia"],["1937","Graduates from college at 18"],["1953","Joins the West Area Computing unit at Langley"],["1961","Calculates the flight path for Alan Shepard’s flight"],["1962","Checks the calculations for John Glenn’s orbit"],["1969","Works on calculations for Apollo 11"],["2015","Receives the Presidential Medal of Freedom"]],
s:[["A gift for numbers","Katherine Coleman was born in 1918 in West Virginia. She loved counting everything, steps, dishes, stars. Schools for Black children in her county stopped at eighth grade, so her family moved to another town so she could continue. She started high school at 10 and finished it at 14."],
["College and a pioneer","At West Virginia State College, the mathematician William Schieffelin Claytor, one of the first African Americans to earn a doctorate in mathematics, created courses just for her and encouraged her to become a research mathematician. She graduated in 1937 at 18 with degrees in mathematics and French, and later became one of the first Black students to attend West Virginia University’s graduate school."],
["Human computers","In 1953 Johnson joined the National Advisory Committee for Aeronautics, NACA, at Langley in Virginia. Before electronic computers were reliable, teams of women called computers did complex calculations by hand. Black women worked in a separate unit, the West Area Computers, and had to use separate dining and restroom facilities."],
["Asking questions","Johnson was soon assigned to the Flight Research Division. She asked to attend editorial meetings where engineers discussed their work, though women had not done so before, and kept asking until she was allowed. Her colleagues came to rely on her skill with analytic geometry."],
["The space race","After the Soviet Union launched Sputnik in 1957, NACA became NASA in 1958. Johnson calculated the trajectory for Alan Shepard’s flight in 1961, the first American in space, working out exactly when and where the spacecraft should launch and land."],
["Get the girl to check","In 1962 NASA used new electronic computers to calculate John Glenn’s orbit around Earth. Glenn, wary of the machines, asked that Johnson check the numbers by hand. If she says they’re good, he reportedly said, then I’m ready to go. She spent days checking, and the flight was a success."],
["To the Moon","Johnson helped calculate the paths for the Apollo missions, including Apollo 11’s journey to the Moon in 1969 and the timing for the lunar module to meet the command module in lunar orbit. She also worked on plans that helped bring the crew of Apollo 13 home safely in 1970 and later on the Space Shuttle."],
["Family and faith","Johnson married James Goble in 1939, and they had three daughters. After he died, she married James Johnson in 1959. She sang in her church choir for decades and encouraged young people, especially girls and students of color, to study science and mathematics."],
["Recognition at last","Johnson retired from NASA in 1986 after 33 years. For decades her work was little known outside NASA. In 2015 President Barack Obama awarded her the Presidential Medal of Freedom, and in 2016 the book and film Hidden Figures made her story famous around the world."],
["Her legacy","In 2017 NASA opened a computational research facility at Langley named after her, and in 2019 she received the Congressional Gold Medal. She died in 2020 at 101. Her story helped bring attention to the many women and people of color whose contributions to science had been overlooked."]],
sp:["Hidden Figures","In 2016 the writer Margot Lee Shetterly published Hidden Figures, about Katherine Johnson and her fellow Black mathematicians Dorothy Vaughan and Mary Jackson at Langley. The film based on it, released the same year, was nominated for the Oscar for Best Picture. Johnson, then 98, attended the Oscars ceremony, where the audience gave her a standing ovation."],
g:[["Trajectory","The path followed by a moving object"],["Orbit","The curved path of an object around a planet or star"],["Segregation","The forced separation of people by race"],["Computer","Here, a person who did complex calculations by hand"]],
f:["Katherine Johnson finished high school at the age of 14.","John Glenn asked her personally to check the computer’s calculations before his flight.","She lived to the age of 101."]},

{id:"tereshkova",t:"Valentina Tereshkova",i:"👩‍🚀",w:"Valentina Tereshkova",a:["Valentina Tereshkova"],born:"6 March 1937, Bolshoye Maslennikovo, Soviet Union (now Russia)",died:"",nat:"Russian (Soviet)",known:"Becoming the first woman in space, in 1963",r:["space","rocket","coldwar","russiacountry"],
l:"Valentina Tereshkova is a Russian former cosmonaut who in June 1963 became the first woman to fly in space. Aboard Vostok 6, she orbited Earth 48 times in nearly three days, longer than all the American astronauts who had flown until then combined. A former textile worker and amateur parachutist, she later became an engineer, a politician and a symbol of the Soviet space program.",
d:[["1937","Born in a village near Yaroslavl"],["1959","Makes her first parachute jump"],["1962","Selected for cosmonaut training"],["16 June 1963","Launches aboard Vostok 6"],["19 June 1963","Returns to Earth after 48 orbits"],["1977","Earns a doctorate in engineering"]],
s:[["A village childhood","Valentina Tereshkova was born in 1937 in a village in the Yaroslavl region of central Russia. Her father, a tractor driver, was killed in the Soviet–Finnish war when she was two. Her mother raised the children alone, working in a textile factory."],
["Factory worker","Tereshkova left school at 16 to work, first in a tire factory and then in a textile mill, while continuing her studies by correspondence. She joined the local youth organization and became secretary of the Communist youth league at her factory."],
["Parachutist","At 22 she joined a local aviation club and made her first parachute jump. She found it thrilling and made more than 120 jumps. This hobby turned out to be the key to her future, because early Soviet cosmonauts had to parachute out of their capsule before it landed."],
["Chosen for space","After Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space in 1961, Soviet leaders decided to send a woman too, partly to beat the Americans. In 1962 Tereshkova and four other women, all parachutists, were selected from hundreds of applicants. They trained in centrifuges, isolation chambers and weightless flights."],
["Vostok 6","On 16 June 1963, Tereshkova launched aboard Vostok 6, with the call sign Chaika, meaning seagull. She was 26. She spent almost three days in orbit, circling Earth 48 times, keeping a flight log and taking photographs used to study the atmosphere."],
["A difficult flight","The flight was not easy. Tereshkova felt sick and uncomfortable in her cramped capsule, and she later revealed that an error in the spacecraft’s programming would have sent it higher rather than lower until she noticed and it was corrected. She ejected from the capsule at about 6 kilometers up and parachuted to the ground in Kazakhstan."],
["A hero","Tereshkova returned to a hero’s welcome and toured the world as a symbol of Soviet achievement and of women’s equality. In November 1963 she married fellow cosmonaut Andriyan Nikolayev, and their daughter, Elena, was the first child born to two parents who had both been in space."],
["Engineer","Tereshkova never flew in space again. She studied at the Zhukovsky Air Force Engineering Academy, graduating in 1969, and earned a doctorate in engineering in 1977. She worked on the space program and rose to the rank of major general."],
["Politics","Tereshkova became a prominent politician, serving in Soviet governing bodies and representing the Soviet Union at international women’s conferences. After the fall of the Soviet Union she remained in public life, and since 2011 she has been a member of the Russian parliament, the State Duma."],
["Her legacy","Tereshkova remained the only woman to have flown solo in space. Twenty years passed before another Soviet woman, Svetlana Savitskaya, flew in 1982, and the first American woman, Sally Ride, flew in 1983. A crater on the far side of the Moon and a minor planet are named after her."]],
sp:["Chaika in orbit","During her flight, Tereshkova spoke by radio with Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev and with Valery Bykovsky, who was orbiting at the same time in Vostok 5. People across the world watched television pictures from inside her capsule. Her call sign, Chaika, the seagull, became famous. Asked years later what she would say to a seagull, she reportedly said she would tell it how beautiful Earth looks from above."],
g:[["Cosmonaut","A Russian or Soviet astronaut"],["Orbit","A curved path around a planet"],["Capsule","A small spacecraft for crew"],["Centrifuge","A spinning machine used to test the effects of strong forces on the body"]],
f:["Valentina Tereshkova was 26 when she flew into space.","She orbited Earth 48 times.","She is still the only woman to have flown a solo space mission."]},

{id:"tubman",t:"Harriet Tubman",i:"🕯️",w:"Harriet Tubman",a:["Harriet Tubman"],born:"c. 1822, Dorchester County, Maryland, United States",died:"10 March 1913, Auburn, New York",nat:"American",known:"Escaping slavery and leading others to freedom on the Underground Railroad",r:["lincoln","mlk","usacountry"],
l:"Harriet Tubman was an American woman who escaped slavery in 1849 and then returned again and again to the South to lead family members and others to freedom through the Underground Railroad, a secret network of routes and safe houses. During the American Civil War she served as a scout, spy and nurse for the Union army and led a raid that freed more than 700 enslaved people. She later campaigned for women’s right to vote.",
d:[["c. 1822","Born into slavery in Maryland"],["1849","Escapes to Philadelphia"],["1850","The Fugitive Slave Act makes escape more dangerous"],["1850s","Makes about 13 trips to lead people to freedom"],["1863","Leads the Combahee River Raid"],["1913","Dies in Auburn, New York"]],
s:[["Born into slavery","Harriet Tubman was born Araminta Ross around 1822 on a plantation in Dorchester County, Maryland. Her parents were enslaved, and she was hired out to work for other people from about the age of five. Several of her sisters were sold away, a cruelty that haunted her family."],
["A terrible injury","As a teenager, Tubman was struck on the head by a heavy metal weight thrown by an overseer at another enslaved person. She suffered seizures, headaches and sudden sleeping spells for the rest of her life, as well as vivid visions and dreams, which she believed were messages from God."],
["Escape","In 1844 she married John Tubman, a free Black man, and took the name Harriet. In 1849, fearing she would be sold, she escaped, walking about 145 kilometers north at night, guided by the North Star, to Pennsylvania, a free state. I had crossed the line, she later said. I was free."],
["The Underground Railroad","The Underground Railroad was not a real railroad but a secret network of people, Black and white, who helped escaping enslaved people travel north, with hiding places called stations and guides called conductors. After 1850, when the Fugitive Slave Act required free states to return escapees, many fled all the way to Canada."],
["Conductor","Over about 11 years Tubman returned to Maryland about 13 times, leading around 70 people to freedom, including her parents and brothers, and giving instructions to dozens more. She traveled at night, often in winter, used disguises and spirituals as coded signals, and carried a pistol. She said she never lost a passenger."],
["Moses","Tubman became known as Moses, after the biblical leader who led his people out of slavery in Egypt. Slaveholders offered rewards for the capture of escapees she helped, but she was never caught. She worked with abolitionists such as Frederick Douglass and William Still and supported John Brown’s plans for a raid against slavery."],
["The Civil War","When the Civil War began in 1861, Tubman joined the Union army in South Carolina as a cook, nurse, scout and spy. She organized a network of spies among the enslaved people of the region and gathered information about Confederate positions."],
["The Combahee River Raid","On 2 June 1863, Tubman guided Union gunboats carrying Black soldiers up the Combahee River in South Carolina. They destroyed Confederate supplies and freed more than 700 enslaved people, who rushed to the boats. She was the first woman known to lead an armed raid in the American Civil War."],
["Later years","After the war, Tubman settled in Auburn, New York, where she cared for her elderly parents and others in need, though she struggled for years to get a government pension for her service. She campaigned for women’s right to vote and opened a home for elderly and poor African Americans."],
["Her legacy","Tubman died in 1913, around the age of 91, and was buried with military honors. She is remembered as one of the bravest figures in American history. Schools, ships and parks are named after her, and plans were announced in 2016 to put her portrait on the 20-dollar bill."]],
sp:["Following the North Star","Escaping enslaved people often traveled at night and found their way north by the North Star, Polaris, which stays almost fixed in the sky above the North Pole. They learned to find it from the Big Dipper, whose two end stars point toward it, and sang a song called Follow the Drinking Gourd, a name for the Dipper. On cloudy nights, Tubman and others felt for moss, which tends to grow on the north side of trees."],
g:[["Underground Railroad","A secret network that helped enslaved people escape to freedom"],["Abolitionist","A person who worked to end slavery"],["Conductor","A guide on the Underground Railroad"],["Fugitive Slave Act","An 1850 US law requiring escaped enslaved people to be returned"]],
f:["Harriet Tubman was nicknamed Moses.","She said she never lost a single passenger on the Underground Railroad.","She led a Civil War raid that freed more than 700 people."]}
);

